  if (slug === '6-day-golden-triangle-tour-with-tiger-safari') {
    return [
      { question: "What is the day-by-day itinerary for the 6-day Golden Triangle tour with tiger safari?", answer: "**Day 1 — Delhi:** Arrive and transfer to your hotel; evening orientation walk or leisure time. **Day 2 — Delhi sightseeing:** Old Delhi (Jama Masjid, Red Fort, Chandni Chowk rickshaw ride) and New Delhi (India Gate, Humayun's Tomb, Qutub Minar) with a licensed guide. **Day 3 — Delhi to Agra (200 km, ~3.5 hrs):** Taj Mahal visit, Agra Fort, optional Mehtab Bagh at sunset. **Day 4 — Agra to Ranthambore (280 km, ~5 hrs):** Drive via Fatehpur Sikri stop; evening at leisure near the national park. **Day 5 — Ranthambore tiger safari:** Morning and afternoon jeep safaris inside Ranthambore National Park with a park naturalist. **Day 6 — Ranthambore to Jaipur (180 km, ~3 hrs):** Amber Fort, City Palace, Hawa Mahal; drop-off in Jaipur or return drive to Delhi. For a condensed version, consider the [4-day Golden Triangle & Ranthambore tour](/india/delhi/golden-triangle-ranthambore-tiger-safari-4-days)." },
      { question: "Are hotels included in the 6-day Golden Triangle tour price?", answer: "Yes — **5 nights of hotel accommodation** are included: 1 night in Delhi, 1 night in Agra, 2 nights near Ranthambore National Park, and 1 night in Jaipur. The standard package uses comfortable **3-star hotels** with air conditioning, Wi-Fi, and hot water. Upgrades to 4-star heritage properties or 5-star luxury hotels (such as Oberoi Amarvilas in Agra or Taj Rambagh Palace in Jaipur) are available at supplemental pricing. In Ranthambore, accommodation is typically a wildlife lodge or resort situated within **5–10 km** of the park gate, adding to the jungle atmosphere." },
      { question: "What are the tiger safari details at Ranthambore National Park?", answer: "The tour includes **two jeep safaris** (morning and afternoon) inside Ranthambore National Park on Day 5. Morning safaris run from **6:00–9:30 AM** and afternoon safaris from **2:30–6:00 PM** (timings vary seasonally). The vehicle is an open-top **Gypsy jeep** seating 6 passengers, accompanied by a government-certified park naturalist who tracks tiger movements and knows individual tigers by name. Ranthambore has a tiger sighting probability of approximately **60–75%** per safari day — among the highest in India. All **park entry fees and safari charges** (approximately ₹1,500–₹2,500 per person per safari for foreign nationals) are included in the tour price." },
      { question: "Are meals included in the 6-day Golden Triangle tiger safari tour?", answer: "**Daily breakfasts** at all hotels are included in the standard package. Lunches and dinners can be added at the time of booking for an additional cost of approximately **₹500–₹800 per meal** at quality restaurants. During the Ranthambore segment, a packed breakfast is provided before the early morning safari, and a full lunch is arranged at the jungle lodge between safaris. The tour guide recommends excellent local restaurants at each stop — from Mughlai cuisine in Old Delhi's Chandni Chowk to Rajasthani thali in Jaipur. Vegetarian, vegan, Jain, halal, and allergen-aware options are available at all meal stops." },
      { question: "What type of vehicle is used for the 6-day Golden Triangle tour?", answer: "A **private air-conditioned vehicle** is provided for the entire 6-day circuit — typically a Toyota Innova Crysta (6-seat SUV) for groups up to 5, or a sedan (Swift Dzire or Honda Amaze) for couples and solo travellers. For groups of 7 or more, a **tempo traveller** (12–15 seats) is arranged. The same driver accompanies you throughout all 6 days, ensuring continuity and familiarity with your preferences. The vehicle is equipped with USB charging ports and bottled water. Inside Ranthambore National Park, a separate open-top Gypsy jeep is used for safaris, as private vehicles are not permitted inside the park." },
      { question: "Is the 6-day Golden Triangle tour private or a group tour?", answer: "This is a **fully private tour** — your group travels alone with a dedicated driver and guide, with no other tourists sharing your vehicle or itinerary. The private format allows complete flexibility: you can spend extra time at sites you enjoy, skip locations that don't interest you, and adjust start times to suit your pace. Licensed guides are provided in each city — a Delhi-Agra specialist for Days 2–3, a park naturalist for Ranthambore safaris on Day 5, and a Jaipur specialist on Day 6. This is not a fixed-departure group tour; your trip runs on your chosen dates." },
      { question: "What cities are covered on the 6-day Golden Triangle with Ranthambore tour?", answer: "The tour covers **four destinations** in a logical loop: **Delhi** (India's capital — Mughal and British-era monuments), **Agra** (home of the Taj Mahal and Agra Fort), **Ranthambore** (India's most famous tiger reserve in Rajasthan), and **Jaipur** (the 'Pink City' — Amber Fort, City Palace, Hawa Mahal). The total driving distance across all 6 days is approximately **900–950 km**. The route forms a rough diamond shape across northern India, and each driving segment is **3–5 hours**, keeping travel manageable. For Delhi-only sightseeing in more depth, see our [Delhi full-day guided tour](/india/delhi/delhi-full-day-guided-tour)." },
      { question: "What is the best season to do the 6-day Golden Triangle with tiger safari?", answer: "**October to March** is the ideal season, combining comfortable weather for monument sightseeing (18–28°C) with Ranthambore National Park being open for safaris. **March to May** offers the highest tiger sighting probability (vegetation thins, tigers gather at water sources) but temperatures reach **40–46°C**. The park is **closed from July to September** during monsoon season, making the tour unavailable in those months. For the best balance of comfortable weather and good tiger sighting odds, **November to February** is optimal. January mornings can be foggy in Delhi and Agra, adding atmosphere to sunrise visits at the [Taj Mahal](/india/agra/taj-mahal-sunrise-tour)." },
      { question: "What fitness level is needed for the 6-day Golden Triangle tiger safari tour?", answer: "The tour requires only a **moderate fitness level** — no strenuous hiking or climbing is involved. The most physically demanding activity is walking through monument complexes: the Taj Mahal gardens involve approximately **1.5 km of flat walking**, Amber Fort has ramps and steps (elephant rides or jeep rides to the top are available for ₹500–₹1,100), and Old Delhi's Chandni Chowk is a walking exploration of **1–2 km**. The tiger safaris are entirely vehicle-based — you remain seated in the Gypsy jeep throughout. Air-conditioned vehicle transfers between cities require sitting for **3–5 hours** per leg. The tour is suitable for ages 5 to 75+ and for travellers with moderate mobility." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy for the 6-day Golden Triangle tour?", answer: "Free cancellation is available up to **7 days before departure** for a full refund. Cancellations **3–6 days** before departure receive a **50% refund**. Cancellations within **48 hours** of departure are non-refundable due to pre-booked hotel rooms, safari permits, and vehicle arrangements. Ranthambore safari permits in particular must be booked well in advance through the government portal and are non-transferable once issued. Date changes (rescheduling) are free if requested **5+ days** before departure, subject to availability. Travel insurance covering trip cancellation is strongly recommended, especially during peak season (October–February)." },
      { question: "Are monument entry tickets included in the 6-day tour price?", answer: "Yes — **all monument entry tickets** are included: Red Fort (₹35 Indian / ₹500 foreign), Humayun's Tomb (₹35/₹550), Qutub Minar (₹35/₹550), Taj Mahal (₹50/₹1,100 + ₹200 mausoleum fee), Agra Fort (₹35/₹550), Fatehpur Sikri (₹35/₹550), Ranthambore National Park safari permits (₹1,500–₹2,500 per safari), Amber Fort (₹100/₹500), City Palace Jaipur (₹200/₹500), and Jantar Mantar (₹50/₹200). Camera fees where applicable are also included. The total ticket value for foreign nationals across all sites is approximately **₹7,000–₹9,000** — all pre-purchased so you skip ticket queues at every location." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved each day on the 6-day tour?", answer: "Daily walking varies by location: **Delhi (Day 2):** approximately **4–5 km** total across Old Delhi bazaar walks and New Delhi monument visits. **Agra (Day 3):** approximately **3–4 km** covering the Taj Mahal complex and Agra Fort. **Travel days (Days 1, 4):** minimal walking, primarily vehicle transfers. **Ranthambore (Day 5):** virtually **no walking** — safaris are entirely vehicle-based inside the park. **Jaipur (Day 6):** approximately **3–4 km** across Amber Fort, City Palace, and Hawa Mahal. All walking is on flat or gently graded surfaces, with rest stops and air-conditioned vehicle breaks between sites. Comfortable walking shoes are recommended." }
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'golden-triangle-ranthambore-tiger-safari-4-days') {
    return [
      { question: "What is the 4-day Golden Triangle & Ranthambore itinerary?", answer: "**Day 1 — Delhi to Agra (200 km, ~3.5 hrs):** Early morning pickup from your Delhi hotel, drive to Agra via Yamuna Expressway, visit the Taj Mahal and Agra Fort with a licensed guide, overnight in Agra. **Day 2 — Agra to Ranthambore (280 km, ~5 hrs):** Optional sunrise Taj Mahal revisit, then drive to Ranthambore with an en-route stop at Fatehpur Sikri; evening at the wildlife lodge. **Day 3 — Ranthambore safari & drive to Jaipur:** Morning jeep safari (6:00–9:30 AM), then drive to Jaipur (180 km, ~3 hrs); afternoon Jaipur sightseeing — Amber Fort, City Palace. **Day 4 — Jaipur to Delhi (270 km, ~5 hrs):** Morning Jaipur sightseeing (Hawa Mahal, Jantar Mantar), then return drive to Delhi with drop-off at hotel or airport. For a more relaxed pace with two safari days, see the [6-day Golden Triangle with tiger safari](/india/delhi/6-day-golden-triangle-tour-with-tiger-safari)." },
      { question: "What is the probability of seeing a tiger at Ranthambore on the 4-day tour?", answer: "Ranthambore National Park has one of the highest tiger sighting rates in India — approximately **60–75% probability** of spotting a Bengal tiger on any single safari outing. The park's relatively open terrain (lakeshores, grasslands, ancient ruins) means tigers are frequently visible in daylight rather than hidden in dense jungle. Your morning Gypsy jeep safari (3.5 hours) is led by a government-certified naturalist who tracks tiger movements and communicates with other jeeps via radio. While sightings cannot be guaranteed (these are wild animals), Ranthambore's resident population of **70+ tigers** and the park's open landscape provide excellent odds even on a single safari." },
      { question: "What category of hotels are included in the 4-day tour?", answer: "The standard 4-day package includes **3-star hotels** — 1 night in Agra (typically in the Taj Ganj area near the Taj Mahal), 1 night at a Ranthambore jungle lodge (within 5–10 km of the park gate), and 1 night in Jaipur (within the old city or close to major sites). All hotels provide air conditioning, Wi-Fi, hot water, and daily breakfast. Upgrades to **4-star heritage properties** (restored Rajput havelis) or **5-star luxury** (Oberoi, Taj Hotels, ITC) are available at supplemental pricing. The Ranthambore lodge options often feature a rustic jungle aesthetic with courtyards, wildlife artwork, and evening campfire areas." },
      { question: "What transport is used between cities on the 4-day Golden Triangle tour?", answer: "A **private air-conditioned vehicle** — typically a Toyota Innova Crysta (seats 6) or sedan (seats 3) — is provided for the entire 4-day circuit with the same driver throughout. Total driving distance across the 4 days is approximately **930 km**. The longest single drive is Agra to Ranthambore (**280 km, ~5 hours**), broken up by a stop at Fatehpur Sikri. All national highway roads are well-maintained. Inside Ranthambore National Park, a separate open-top **Gypsy jeep** (6 seats) is used for the safari, as private cars are not allowed inside. Tolls, parking fees, and driver accommodation are all included." },
      { question: "Are meals included in the 4-day Golden Triangle Ranthambore tour?", answer: "**Daily breakfasts** at all three hotels are included. A packed early breakfast is provided on the Ranthambore safari morning before the 6:00 AM departure. Lunches and dinners are **not included** in the standard package but can be added for approximately **₹500–₹800 per meal**. Your guide and driver recommend excellent local restaurants at each stop — Mughlai cuisine in Agra, Rajasthani thali in Jaipur, and jungle lodge dining at Ranthambore. Vegetarian, vegan, Jain, halal, and allergen-specific dietary requirements are accommodated at all stops when informed at booking." },
      { question: "How does the 4-day tour differ from the 6-day Golden Triangle with tiger safari?", answer: "The key differences: the **4-day version** includes **1 morning safari** at Ranthambore, while the [6-day version](/india/delhi/6-day-golden-triangle-tour-with-tiger-safari) includes **2 full safaris** (morning + afternoon), doubling your tiger sighting chances. The 6-day tour also spends a full day sightseeing in Delhi before departing for Agra, whereas the 4-day tour begins directly with the Agra drive on Day 1. The 6-day tour allows **2 nights at Ranthambore** (more relaxed pacing) versus 1 night in the 4-day version. The 4-day tour is ideal for travellers with limited time who want the essential Golden Triangle + Ranthambore experience; the 6-day is better for wildlife enthusiasts who want maximum safari time." },
      { question: "What should I pack for the Ranthambore tiger safari?", answer: "Essential safari packing: **Clothing** — earth-toned layers (khaki, olive, brown; avoid bright colours that startle wildlife), a warm fleece or jacket for chilly dawn safaris (October–February mornings can drop to **8–12°C** in open jeeps), a sun hat, and sunscreen. **Footwear** — closed-toe shoes or hiking sandals (the jeep is dusty). **Protection** — sunglasses, insect repellent (mosquitoes near lakes), and a scarf/bandana to cover your nose on dusty tracks. **Hydration** — a reusable water bottle (refilled at the lodge). **Binoculars** — highly recommended for spotting wildlife at distance (8x42 or 10x42 magnification). **Medication** — motion sickness tablets if prone to nausea on bumpy tracks." },
      { question: "What camera equipment is best for the Ranthambore tiger safari?", answer: "For tiger photography at Ranthambore, a **telephoto zoom lens** is essential — tigers can be spotted at distances of **50–200 metres**. A **100–400mm** or **200–600mm** zoom lens on a DSLR or mirrorless camera is ideal. Smartphone cameras with optical zoom (iPhone 15 Pro Max, Samsung S24 Ultra) can capture decent shots when tigers are closer (**30–50 metres**). Bring extra batteries (cold mornings drain batteries faster) and large-capacity memory cards (**64GB minimum**). A **beanbag** for resting long lenses on the jeep rail is more practical than a tripod in the open-top Gypsy. Set your camera to burst/continuous shooting mode — tiger movement can be sudden and brief." },
      { question: "How early do safari mornings start on the 4-day tour?", answer: "On the Ranthambore safari day (Day 3), you depart the lodge at approximately **5:30 AM** to reach the park gate by **6:00 AM** for the morning safari slot. The safari runs until approximately **9:30 AM** (3.5 hours). A packed early breakfast is provided before departure — coffee/tea, sandwiches, and fruit from the lodge. After the safari, a full hot breakfast is served at the lodge (around 10:00 AM) before the drive to Jaipur. The early start is essential because wildlife is most active in the cool dawn hours — Bengal tigers, sloth bears, and deer are all more visible before the heat of the day. Sunrise over the park's lakes and fort ruins is spectacular." },
      { question: "What is the maximum group size for the 4-day Golden Triangle tour?", answer: "This is a **fully private tour** — your group travels exclusively with your own driver and guide. There is no mixing with other tourists. The standard Gypsy jeep inside Ranthambore seats a **maximum of 6 passengers** (government regulation), so if your group exceeds 6, a second safari jeep is arranged. The private vehicle for intercity travel seats up to **6 passengers** (Innova Crysta); groups of 7–12 use a tempo traveller. Licensed city guides are provided at each destination — a Delhi/Agra specialist and a Jaipur specialist. For a comprehensive Delhi experience before starting the circuit, add our [Delhi full-day guided tour](/india/delhi/delhi-full-day-guided-tour) as a Day 0." },
      { question: "Is the 4-day Golden Triangle Ranthambore tour suitable for children?", answer: "Yes — the tour is suitable for children aged **5 and above**. Ranthambore National Park allows children of all ages on Gypsy jeep safaris (children under 5 must sit on a parent's lap). The safari is exciting and educational for children — spotting tigers, deer, monkeys, crocodiles, and hundreds of bird species. The Taj Mahal and Amber Fort also captivate younger visitors with their grandeur. **Children under 15** receive discounted entry at most Indian monuments (free at many). The private vehicle format means you can stop for breaks as needed, and the guide adjusts the pace and commentary for families. Pack snacks, water, and entertainment for the longer driving segments." }
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'taj-mahal-superfast-guided-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "Which train is used for the Taj Mahal superfast tour from Delhi?", answer: "The tour uses the **Gatimaan Express** (Train No. 12050) — India's fastest semi-high-speed train, operating at up to **160 km/h**. The Gatimaan Express departs from **Hazrat Nizamuddin Railway Station** in south Delhi (not New Delhi Railway Station) at **8:10 AM** and arrives at **Agra Cantt Station** at **9:50 AM** — a journey of just **1 hour 40 minutes** covering 188 km. The return train (Gatimaan Express 12049) departs Agra Cantt at **5:35 PM** and arrives back at Hazrat Nizamuddin by **7:30 PM**. The Gatimaan Express offers executive chair car class with airline-style reclining seats, onboard catering, and air conditioning." },
      { question: "Are confirmed (CNF) train tickets guaranteed on this tour?", answer: "Yes — **confirmed Gatimaan Express tickets** are pre-booked and guaranteed as part of the tour package. Gatimaan Express seats sell out weeks in advance (especially during October–March peak season), and the tour operator secures tickets through advance booking quotas. You receive your **confirmed e-ticket** with PNR number at least **48 hours before travel**. No waitlisting or RAC (Reservation Against Cancellation) — only confirmed seats. This is a significant advantage over self-booking, as independent travellers frequently find the Gatimaan Express sold out for their preferred date. Bring a government-issued photo ID (passport for foreign nationals) as it is checked onboard." },
      { question: "What is included in the Taj Mahal superfast train tour?", answer: "The package includes: **Gatimaan Express confirmed return tickets** (Delhi–Agra–Delhi), pickup from your Delhi hotel to Hazrat Nizamuddin Station (and return drop-off), a **licensed Agra guide** for the full day, private air-conditioned vehicle in Agra for all transfers between sites, **Taj Mahal entry ticket** (₹1,100 for foreign nationals + ₹200 mausoleum entry), **Agra Fort entry ticket** (₹550), and a sit-down **lunch** at a quality Agra restaurant. The guide meets you at Agra Cantt Station with a name board. Total time in Agra is approximately **8 hours** — ample for a comprehensive visit to the Taj Mahal, Agra Fort, and lunch." },
      { question: "How long do I spend at the Taj Mahal on this tour?", answer: "You spend approximately **2.5–3 hours** at the Taj Mahal complex, which is generous for a thorough visit. This includes time at the main gate (Great Gate or Darwaza-i-Rauza), the charbagh garden with the iconic reflecting pool, the main mausoleum platform (where you must wear shoe covers), and the interior of the mausoleum to see the marble screens and cenotaphs of Shah Jahan and Mumtaz Mahal. Your licensed guide provides the complete historical narrative — the 22-year construction, 20,000 artisans, and the inlaid semi-precious stone work (pietra dura). Photography time is built in. For a sunrise experience instead, consider the [Taj Mahal sunrise tour](/india/agra/taj-mahal-sunrise-tour)." },
      { question: "Is Agra Fort included in the Taj Mahal train tour?", answer: "Yes — **Agra Fort** is included as the second major site of the day. After the Taj Mahal visit and lunch, you visit the massive **16th-century red sandstone fort** (UNESCO World Heritage Site) that served as the main residence of the Mughal emperors until 1638. The fort's highlights include the Diwan-i-Am (Hall of Public Audience), Diwan-i-Khas (Hall of Private Audience), Sheesh Mahal (Mirror Palace), and the poignant Musamman Burj — the octagonal tower where the aged Shah Jahan was imprisoned by his son and spent his final years gazing at the Taj Mahal across the river. Your guide connects the Taj Mahal and Agra Fort narratives into a complete Mughal Empire story." },
      { question: "Is lunch included in the Taj Mahal superfast tour?", answer: "Yes — a **sit-down lunch** at a quality Agra restaurant is included, typically served between the Taj Mahal visit and the Agra Fort visit (around **1:00–2:00 PM**). The restaurant offers North Indian and Mughlai cuisine — butter chicken, biryani, kebabs, dal makhani, fresh naan — with comprehensive vegetarian, vegan, and dietary-restriction options. The lunch venue is a proper restaurant (not a tourist-trap buffet) selected for food quality and hygiene standards. Inform your guide of dietary requirements when you meet at Agra station. Water and soft drinks are included with the meal." },
      { question: "What station does the Gatimaan Express depart from in Delhi?", answer: "The Gatimaan Express departs from **Hazrat Nizamuddin Railway Station** — located in south Delhi, approximately **10 km from Connaught Place** and **15 km from Delhi airport**. This is NOT New Delhi Railway Station (which is in central Delhi near Paharganj). Your tour includes a **hotel pickup transfer** to the station, departing your hotel at approximately **6:30–7:00 AM** depending on hotel location and Delhi morning traffic. The station has a clean waiting lounge and platform amenities. Arrive at the station at least **20 minutes before departure** (8:10 AM). For hotels in south Delhi (Saket, Hauz Khas, Defence Colony), the station is very conveniently located." },
      { question: "What time do I return to Delhi on the Taj Mahal train tour?", answer: "The return Gatimaan Express (Train 12049) departs Agra Cantt at **5:35 PM** and arrives at Hazrat Nizamuddin Station, Delhi at **7:30 PM**. Your driver then transfers you from the station to your Delhi hotel, arriving at approximately **8:00–8:30 PM** depending on hotel location and evening traffic. This gives you a full **7.5 hours in Agra** (arrival 9:50 AM, departure 5:35 PM) — more than enough for a complete Taj Mahal visit, lunch, and Agra Fort. If you have an evening flight from Delhi airport (DEL), the driver can take you directly to the airport instead of your hotel — confirm this at booking." },
      { question: "Can I visit other Agra sites besides the Taj Mahal and Agra Fort?", answer: "With **8 hours** in Agra, you can comfortably add one or two additional sites beyond the Taj Mahal and Agra Fort. Popular additions: **Mehtab Bagh** (₹300 entry — garden across the Yamuna River with a spectacular Taj Mahal backdrop, excellent for photography), **Itmad-ud-Daula** (₹110/₹210 — the 'Baby Taj,' an exquisite marble tomb predating the Taj Mahal), or **Fatehpur Sikri** (₹50/₹550 — Emperor Akbar's abandoned city, 37 km outside Agra, adds ~2 hours). Discuss your priorities with your guide at the start of the day. For a separate comprehensive Agra experience, see the [Taj Mahal entry ticket tour](/india/agra/taj-mahal-entry-ticket)." },
      { question: "Is the Taj Mahal train tour better than driving from Delhi to Agra?", answer: "The Gatimaan Express train tour offers several advantages over driving: **Speed** — 1 hour 40 minutes by train versus 3–3.5 hours by car (each way), saving **3+ hours** of total travel time. **Comfort** — airline-style reclining seats with onboard meals versus sitting in a car through highway traffic. **Reliability** — trains run on schedule regardless of Delhi's unpredictable traffic jams, road construction, or accidents on the Yamuna Expressway. **Experience** — the Gatimaan Express itself is a uniquely Indian travel experience. The only advantage of driving is door-to-door convenience and flexibility for stops (such as Mathura or Sikandra en route). For driving options, consider the [Delhi to Agra private tour](/india/delhi/delhi-agra-private-tour)." },
      { question: "Is the Taj Mahal superfast tour suitable for families with children?", answer: "Yes — the train tour is excellent for families. Children enjoy the **Gatimaan Express experience** (India's fastest train), and the total walking at the Taj Mahal is approximately **1.5 km on flat ground** — manageable for children aged 5+. **Children under 5** travel free on Indian Railways (without a separate seat). Children aged **5–11** receive a **50% discount** on train tickets. Entry to the Taj Mahal is free for children under 15. The guide adjusts commentary for families, highlighting stories and details that engage younger visitors. The private vehicle in Agra can accommodate car seats if brought along. The day is long (12+ hours door to door) but punctuated by varied activities that keep children engaged." }
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'delhi-to-jaipur-same-day-tour-by-car') {
    return [
      { question: "How long is the drive from Delhi to Jaipur by car?", answer: "The drive from Delhi to Jaipur is approximately **270 km via NH48** (Delhi-Jaipur Expressway) and takes **4.5–5 hours** each way under normal conditions. The route is entirely on a well-maintained national highway and expressway, with smooth divided carriageway for most of the distance. Departure from your Delhi hotel is at **5:30–6:00 AM** to arrive in Jaipur by **10:30–11:00 AM**, allowing a full day of sightseeing before the return drive departing Jaipur at approximately **5:00–5:30 PM**, reaching Delhi by **10:00–10:30 PM**. A brief stop for tea/coffee and breakfast is made en route at a highway restaurant (approximately 30 minutes)." },
      { question: "What monuments and attractions are included in the Jaipur same-day tour?", answer: "The day covers Jaipur's top highlights: **Amber Fort** (the massive 16th-century hilltop fortress — the crown jewel of Jaipur, featuring Sheesh Mahal mirror palace, Diwan-i-Khas, and panoramic views; ~1.5 hours), **City Palace** (the Maharaja's residence with museums, courtyards, and the famous oversized silver urns; ~1 hour), **Jantar Mantar** (UNESCO-listed 18th-century astronomical observatory with the world's largest stone sundial; ~30 minutes), and **Hawa Mahal** (Palace of Winds — a photo stop at the iconic pink sandstone facade; ~15 minutes). Time permitting, Jal Mahal (Water Palace, photo stop) and the vibrant **Johari Bazaar** for shopping are also included. For a deeper Jaipur experience, consider our [Amber Fort guided tour](/india/jaipur/amber-fort-official-guided-tour)." },
      { question: "What type of AC car is provided for the Delhi to Jaipur tour?", answer: "A **private air-conditioned sedan** (Toyota Etios, Swift Dzire, or Honda Amaze) is provided for couples and solo travellers. For groups of 3–5, a **Toyota Innova Crysta** (6-seat SUV) is used. For groups of 6–12, a **tempo traveller** (12–15 seats) is arranged. All vehicles have functioning air conditioning (essential for the Rajasthan climate), comfortable seating, adequate luggage space, and USB charging ports. The same vehicle and driver are with you for the entire day — approximately **16–17 hours** from morning pickup to evening drop-off. Bottled water is provided in the vehicle. The vehicle is well-maintained and highway-rated for the high-speed expressway driving." },
      { question: "Does the driver speak English on the Delhi to Jaipur tour?", answer: "The driver speaks **basic conversational English** — sufficient for pickup coordination, route questions, and restaurant recommendations. For detailed monument commentary and historical narration, a **separate licensed guide in Jaipur** is included in the tour package. The Jaipur guide is a Rajasthan Tourism-certified professional who meets you at the first monument (typically Amber Fort) and accompanies you through all sites, providing in-depth English commentary on the Rajput history, architecture, and cultural significance of each location. Guides speaking **French, German, Spanish, Japanese, and other languages** are available on request at booking." },
      { question: "Where is the pickup and drop-off for the Jaipur same-day tour?", answer: "Pickup and drop-off are at **your Delhi hotel, guesthouse, or Airbnb** — anywhere within Delhi NCR (National Capital Region), including Gurgaon and Noida. Airport pickup from **Indira Gandhi International Airport (DEL)** is also available if you're arriving on an early morning flight. The driver arrives at your location at **5:30–6:00 AM** with a name board. On return, you're dropped at the same location (or a different Delhi address if needed) at approximately **10:00–10:30 PM**. If you have a late-night flight, the driver can drop you directly at Delhi airport instead of your hotel — confirm this arrangement at booking." },
      { question: "What time does the Jaipur same-day tour start and end?", answer: "The tour follows this timeline: **5:30–6:00 AM** — pickup from your Delhi hotel. **7:30 AM** — highway breakfast stop (~30 minutes). **10:30–11:00 AM** — arrival in Jaipur, begin sightseeing. **11:00 AM–4:30 PM** — Jaipur sightseeing with guided visits to Amber Fort, City Palace, Jantar Mantar, Hawa Mahal, and lunch. **5:00–5:30 PM** — depart Jaipur for Delhi. **10:00–10:30 PM** — arrive back at your Delhi hotel. Total tour duration is approximately **16–17 hours**, with **5.5–6 hours of active sightseeing** in Jaipur. The early start is essential to maximize time in Jaipur given the 5-hour drive each way." },
      { question: "Is a guide included in the Delhi to Jaipur same-day tour?", answer: "Yes — a **Rajasthan Tourism-certified licensed guide** is included for all Jaipur monuments. The guide meets you at the first site (typically Amber Fort around 10:30–11:00 AM) and accompanies you through the entire Jaipur sightseeing portion — approximately **5–6 hours** of guided touring. The guide provides expert commentary on Rajput history, the Kachwaha dynasty, Amber Fort's construction and design, the astronomical instruments at Jantar Mantar, and the significance of the Pink City designation. Without a guide, the historical depth and architectural details of Jaipur's monuments are largely inaccessible. The guide also assists with entry tickets and navigating crowded areas." },
      { question: "Are monument entry tickets included in the Jaipur day tour price?", answer: "Yes — **all entry tickets** are included: Amber Fort (**₹100 Indian / ₹500 foreign nationals**), City Palace (**₹200/₹500**), Jantar Mantar (**₹50/₹200**), and Hawa Mahal (**₹50/₹200**). A composite ticket covering Amber Fort, Jantar Mantar, Hawa Mahal, Nahargarh Fort, and Albert Hall Museum is available for **₹100/₹1,000** — your guide arranges whichever option provides the best value for your itinerary. Camera fees at all monuments are also included. The total ticket value for foreign nationals is approximately **₹1,400–₹2,000**, all pre-purchased so you skip the ticket queues." },
      { question: "Is one day enough to see Jaipur properly?", answer: "One day covers Jaipur's **essential highlights** comprehensively — Amber Fort, City Palace, Jantar Mantar, and Hawa Mahal are all within a compact area of the old city and can be visited thoroughly in **5–6 hours**. However, Jaipur has much more to offer beyond the core sites: Nahargarh Fort (sunset views), Albert Hall Museum, Jaigarh Fort (home to the world's largest cannon on wheels), the bazaars of Johari Bazaar and Bapu Bazaar, and the step wells of Chand Baori (95 km outside Jaipur). For travellers with more time, an overnight stay in Jaipur is recommended. For a multi-day experience, consider the [Golden Triangle tour from Delhi](/india/delhi/delhi-private-4-day-golden-triangle-luxury-tour)." },
      { question: "Are toll charges and parking fees included in the Delhi to Jaipur tour?", answer: "Yes — **all toll charges, parking fees, and fuel costs** are included in the tour price. The Delhi-Jaipur Expressway has multiple toll plazas totalling approximately **₹800–₹1,000** in toll charges (round trip). Parking fees at Amber Fort, City Palace, and other Jaipur monuments are approximately **₹100–₹200** per site. Driver meals, overnight allowance, and state permits (required for vehicles crossing state borders from Delhi to Rajasthan) are all included. There are **no hidden costs** — the quoted price is fully inclusive of vehicle, driver, fuel, tolls, parking, guide, entry tickets, and all applicable taxes. Lunch is the only item typically paid separately." },
      { question: "Can I combine the Jaipur day trip with other Delhi tours?", answer: "Absolutely — the Jaipur same-day tour pairs perfectly with Delhi sightseeing on adjacent days. Many travellers do the [Delhi full-day guided tour](/india/delhi/delhi-full-day-guided-tour) on Day 1 (covering Old Delhi and New Delhi) and the Jaipur same-day trip on Day 2. Another popular combination is this Jaipur tour followed by the [Taj Mahal superfast train tour](/india/delhi/taj-mahal-superfast-guided-tour) on the next day — covering all three Golden Triangle cities as day trips from Delhi without needing to change hotels. For a guided Delhi overview with shopping, see our [Delhi guided shopping tour](/india/delhi/delhi-guided-shopping-tour-female-expert)." }
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'delhi-same-day-sightseeing-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What monuments are covered on the Delhi same-day sightseeing tour?", answer: "The full-day Delhi sightseeing tour covers **8–10 major monuments** across both Old Delhi and New Delhi: **Old Delhi** — Red Fort (UNESCO, Mughal fortress), Jama Masjid (India's largest mosque, built 1656), Chandni Chowk (17th-century bazaar with cycle-rickshaw ride), and Raj Ghat (Mahatma Gandhi memorial). **New Delhi** — India Gate (war memorial), Rashtrapati Bhavan/Parliament House (drive-past), Humayun's Tomb (UNESCO, 1570 precursor to the Taj Mahal), Qutub Minar (UNESCO, 73-metre 12th-century minaret), and Lotus Temple (Bahá'í House of Worship). The tour provides a comprehensive sweep of Delhi's **3,000+ year history** from the medieval Delhi Sultanate through the Mughal Empire to British-era New Delhi and modern India." },
      { question: "Is the Delhi same-day sightseeing tour a full-day or half-day tour?", answer: "This is a **full-day tour** running approximately **9–10 hours** from hotel pickup to hotel drop-off. Typical timings are **8:30–9:00 AM pickup** and **6:00–7:00 PM return**. The full day is necessary to properly cover both Old Delhi and New Delhi, which are geographically separated and each contain multiple major sites. A sit-down lunch break (approximately 1 hour) is included midday. If you have limited time, we also offer a [Delhi half-day sightseeing tour](/india/delhi/delhi-sightseeing-half-day-tour) covering 4–5 highlights in approximately **4–5 hours**. The full-day tour is strongly recommended for first-time visitors who want a comprehensive Delhi experience." },
      { question: "What type of transport is used for the Delhi sightseeing tour?", answer: "A **private air-conditioned vehicle** is provided for the entire day — typically a Toyota Innova Crysta (6-seat SUV) for groups of 3–5, or a sedan (Swift Dzire/Honda Amaze) for couples and solo travellers. The vehicle remains exclusively with your group throughout the day, serving as a cool, comfortable base between monument visits. Delhi's distances between major sites are significant — **Qutub Minar to Red Fort is 18 km**, and navigating Delhi's traffic independently is challenging. The driver knows all monument parking areas, one-way restrictions, and traffic patterns. For a luxury vehicle upgrade (Mercedes, BMW, or Audi), see the [Explore Delhi luxury car tour](/india/delhi/explore-old-new-delhi-city-luxury-car-tour)." },
      { question: "Is a licensed guide included in the Delhi sightseeing tour?", answer: "Yes — a **Ministry of Tourism-licensed professional guide** accompanies you throughout the entire day. Delhi's complex layered history — 8 successive cities built on the same site, ruled by Turko-Afghan sultans, Mughals, the British Empire, and the Indian republic — requires expert narration to fully appreciate. Your guide provides the chronological thread connecting each site: how Humayun's Tomb (1570) directly inspired the Taj Mahal (1632), why the British moved the capital from Calcutta to Delhi in 1911, and how Shah Jahan's Red Fort became the symbol of Indian independence. Guides are available in **English, Hindi, French, German, Spanish, Japanese**, and other languages on request." },
      { question: "Are monument entry tickets included in the Delhi sightseeing tour?", answer: "Yes — **all monument entry tickets** are included in the tour price: Red Fort (₹35 Indian / ₹500 foreign), Humayun's Tomb (₹35/₹550), Qutub Minar (₹35/₹550), Jama Masjid (free entry, ₹300 camera fee included), and Lotus Temple (free entry). The total ticket value for foreign nationals is approximately **₹1,900–₹2,100**. All tickets are pre-purchased by your guide, so you **skip the ticket queues** at every monument — a significant time-saver, especially at popular sites like Humayun's Tomb and Qutub Minar where queues can exceed 20–30 minutes during peak season. India Gate and Raj Ghat are free to visit." },
      { question: "Does the tour cover both Old Delhi and New Delhi?", answer: "Yes — the tour comprehensively covers **both Old Delhi and New Delhi**, which are historically and geographically distinct parts of the city. **Old Delhi (Shahjahanabad)** — founded in 1638 by Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan — is a dense, vibrant, sensory-rich experience of narrow lanes, bazaars, mosques, and the Red Fort. **New Delhi** — designed by British architects Edwin Lutyens and Herbert Baker in the 1910s–1930s — is a spacious, tree-lined city of grand colonial boulevards, government buildings, and pre-Mughal monuments (Humayun's Tomb, Qutub Minar). The contrast between the two Delhis is extraordinary and forms one of the most fascinating aspects of the tour. Most tours begin in Old Delhi (morning energy) and move to New Delhi in the afternoon." },
      { question: "Is the Delhi sightseeing tour recommended for first-time visitors to India?", answer: "The Delhi same-day sightseeing tour is **specifically designed for first-time India visitors** and is the most popular Delhi tour for international travellers. Delhi can be overwhelming to navigate independently — the traffic, the distances between sites, the complexity of monument ticketing, and the intensity of Old Delhi's bazaars. A private guide and vehicle create a comfortable, managed framework that allows you to absorb and enjoy the city. Your guide provides cultural context, photography tips, and practical advice throughout the day. By evening, first-time visitors typically feel significantly more comfortable and confident about their India journey. For a detailed planning guide, see our [Delhi travel guide 2026](/india/delhi/delhi-travel-guide-2026)." },
      { question: "Does the Delhi sightseeing tour include hotel pickup?", answer: "Yes — **complimentary pickup and drop-off** at your Delhi hotel, guesthouse, or Airbnb is included. The driver arrives at your accommodation at **8:30–9:00 AM** with a name board displaying your name. Pickup is available from anywhere within Delhi NCR, including central Delhi (Connaught Place, Paharganj, Karol Bagh), south Delhi (Hauz Khas, Saket, Greater Kailash), and the airport area (Aerocity, Mahipalpur). Pickup from **Gurgaon and Noida** is also available with a small surcharge (₹300–₹500). If you're arriving at Delhi airport on a morning flight, airport pickup can be arranged — confirm your flight details at booking." },
      { question: "Is lunch included in the Delhi same-day sightseeing tour?", answer: "Yes — a **sit-down lunch** at a quality Delhi restaurant is included, typically served between the Old Delhi and New Delhi portions of the tour (around **1:00–2:00 PM**). The restaurant serves authentic North Indian and Mughlai cuisine — butter chicken, dal makhani, biryani, kebabs, fresh tandoori naan — with comprehensive vegetarian, vegan, Jain, halal, and allergen-aware options. Delhi is one of India's great food cities, and the lunch is a culinary highlight rather than a generic tourist meal. For travellers with specific food interests, your guide can recommend Old Delhi street food stops (Karim's, Paranthe Wali Gali) as additions to the standard lunch." },
      { question: "Is the Delhi same-day sightseeing tour suitable for families with children?", answer: "Yes — the tour is **excellent for families** and suitable for children aged **5 and above**. Highlights that particularly engage children include the **cycle-rickshaw ride** through Chandni Chowk (exciting and fun), the grand scale of the **Red Fort** (kids love exploring the ramparts), **India Gate** (open lawns for a brief run-around), and the futuristic **Lotus Temple** (the petal-shaped architecture fascinates children). The private vehicle allows for flexible breaks — ice cream stops, restroom breaks, and energy recharges as needed. **Children under 15** receive free entry at most Indian monuments. The guide adjusts commentary for families, using stories and legends that captivate younger visitors. For other family-friendly Delhi options, see [things to do in Delhi](/india/delhi/things-to-do-in-delhi)." },
      { question: "What is the best time of year to take the Delhi sightseeing tour?", answer: "**October to March** is the ideal season for Delhi sightseeing: cool, clear days with temperatures of **15–28°C**, comfortable for extensive outdoor walking at monuments. **November and February** are particularly pleasant. December–January can bring morning fog, which creates atmospheric photography conditions at Humayun's Tomb and Qutub Minar. **April–June** is hot (**35–46°C**) but manageable with early starts (7:00 AM pickup) and the air-conditioned vehicle providing relief between sites. **July–September** (monsoon) brings rain and humidity but also dramatic skies and lush green gardens — monuments are less crowded. The tour operates year-round; guides adjust the itinerary sequence to manage heat and weather conditions." }
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'delhi-sightseeing-half-day-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "Is the Delhi half-day sightseeing tour available in the morning or afternoon?", answer: "The half-day tour is available in **both morning and afternoon** slots. The **morning slot** (8:00–8:30 AM pickup, return by 1:00–1:30 PM) is the most popular and recommended — cooler temperatures, better light for photography, and more energetic bazaar scenes in Old Delhi. The **afternoon slot** (1:30–2:00 PM pickup, return by 6:30–7:00 PM) is ideal for travellers arriving on morning flights or those who want a relaxed morning before sightseeing. Both slots cover the same core monuments and run approximately **4.5–5 hours**. The morning slot is particularly recommended during the hotter months (April–June) to avoid peak afternoon heat." },
      { question: "Which monuments are included in the Delhi half-day tour?", answer: "The half-day tour covers **4–5 essential Delhi highlights**, strategically selected for maximum impact in limited time. **Option A (Old Delhi focus):** Jama Masjid, Red Fort (exterior and ramparts), Chandni Chowk cycle-rickshaw ride, Raj Ghat (Gandhi memorial), and a drive past India Gate. **Option B (New Delhi focus):** Humayun's Tomb (UNESCO), Qutub Minar (UNESCO), Lotus Temple, India Gate, and a drive past Parliament House and Rashtrapati Bhavan. You choose your preferred focus at booking, or your guide recommends based on your interests. Most first-time visitors choose **Option A** for its immersive, sensory-rich Old Delhi experience. For comprehensive coverage of both, upgrade to the [full-day Delhi sightseeing tour](/india/delhi/delhi-same-day-sightseeing-tour)." },
      { question: "How long is the Delhi half-day sightseeing tour?", answer: "The half-day tour runs approximately **4.5–5 hours** from hotel pickup to hotel drop-off. This allows **30–60 minutes** at each of the 4–5 included monuments, plus transit time between sites in your private air-conditioned vehicle. The pace is efficient but not rushed — your guide ensures you see the essential highlights, hear the key historical narratives, and have time for photography at each location. The half-day format is specifically designed for travellers with limited time — those on a layover, business travellers with a free half-day, or visitors who want to combine the tour with other activities (shopping, food tours, or a day trip to Agra or Jaipur)." },
      { question: "Is the Delhi half-day tour private or shared with other tourists?", answer: "This is a **fully private tour** — you travel exclusively with your own guide and driver, with no other tourists in your vehicle or group. The private format allows complete flexibility: if you want to spend extra time at Humayun's Tomb for photography, or skip a site to explore Chandni Chowk's lanes in more depth, the itinerary adjusts to your preferences. Private touring is particularly valuable in Delhi, where navigating the city, managing touts outside monuments, and finding optimal photography positions all benefit from a dedicated guide's attention. The private vehicle (air-conditioned sedan or SUV) is your mobile comfort base between sites." },
      { question: "What is the difference between the half-day and full-day Delhi tours?", answer: "The **half-day tour** (4.5–5 hours) covers **4–5 monuments** in either Old Delhi or New Delhi, while the [full-day tour](/india/delhi/delhi-same-day-sightseeing-tour) (9–10 hours) covers **8–10 monuments** across both Old and New Delhi plus includes lunch. Specifically, the full-day tour adds: the Chandni Chowk cycle-rickshaw ride AND the New Delhi UNESCO sites (or vice versa), Raj Ghat, a sit-down lunch, and more time at each monument. The full-day tour provides the complete Delhi narrative — the historical arc from the 12th-century Sultanate (Qutub Minar) through the Mughal Empire (Red Fort, Humayun's Tomb) to the British Raj (India Gate, Lutyens' Delhi) and modern India (Lotus Temple). The half-day is a highlight reel; the full day is the complete story." },
      { question: "Is the Delhi half-day tour suitable for airport layovers?", answer: "Yes — the half-day tour is **ideal for airport layovers** of **8 hours or more** (international-to-international or international-to-domestic connections). With a 4.5–5 hour tour duration plus approximately **1.5–2 hours** for airport transfers (Indira Gandhi International Airport is 15–25 km from central Delhi), you need a minimum of **7–8 hours** between flights. The driver picks you up at the airport arrivals terminal and returns you to the departures terminal. For layovers of 5–6 hours, a condensed 3-hour version covering 2–3 sites is available. Carry your passport, boarding pass, and checked luggage receipt — your guide assists with all logistics. For a detailed layover planning guide, see [Delhi 1-day itinerary](/india/delhi/delhi-1-day-itinerary)." },
      { question: "Can the Delhi half-day tour include airport pickup?", answer: "Yes — **airport pickup from Indira Gandhi International Airport (DEL)** is included at no extra charge. The driver meets you at the arrivals terminal with a name board after you clear immigration and customs. For the morning slot, pickup is typically at **8:00–8:30 AM** (suitable for travellers who arrived on an early morning or overnight flight). For afternoon tours, airport pickup at **1:30–2:00 PM** works for late-morning arrivals. The tour can also **end at the airport** rather than your hotel if you have an evening departure. One-way airport transfers (pickup at airport, drop at hotel, or vice versa) are seamlessly integrated into the tour routing." },
      { question: "What languages do the guides speak on the Delhi half-day tour?", answer: "All guides are **Ministry of Tourism-licensed professionals** with fluency in **English** as the default language. Guides speaking **French, German, Spanish, Italian, Portuguese, Japanese, Mandarin, Korean, Russian, and Arabic** are available on advance request (typically 48–72 hours notice for non-English languages). The English-speaking guides have extensive experience with international visitors and speak clearly and articulately — accustomed to adjusting pace and vocabulary for non-native English speakers. Guides provide not just monument narration but also cultural context — explaining local customs, religious practices, and daily life in Delhi, which is invaluable for short-stay visitors." },
      { question: "Is the vehicle air-conditioned on the Delhi half-day tour?", answer: "Yes — a **fully air-conditioned private vehicle** is provided for the entire tour. Vehicle types: **sedan** (Toyota Etios, Swift Dzire, or Honda Amaze) for 1–2 passengers, **SUV** (Toyota Innova Crysta, 6 seats) for groups of 3–5, or **tempo traveller** (12–15 seats) for larger groups. Delhi temperatures range from **5–15°C in winter** (December–January) to **40–46°C in summer** (May–June), making air conditioning essential for most of the year. The vehicle has tinted windows for privacy, USB charging ports, and bottled water. Between monument visits, the air-conditioned car provides a refreshing break, especially during the warmer months." },
      { question: "What are the must-see highlights if I only have half a day in Delhi?", answer: "If you have only half a day, prioritize these **4 unmissable Delhi highlights**: (1) **Humayun's Tomb** — the most architecturally significant monument in Delhi, a UNESCO World Heritage Site that directly inspired the Taj Mahal; (2) **Qutub Minar** — the 73-metre, 12th-century minaret, India's earliest Islamic monument; (3) **Chandni Chowk + Jama Masjid** — the sensory heart of Old Delhi, combining the 17th-century bazaar with India's largest mosque; and (4) **India Gate** — the iconic 1931 war memorial on Rajpath. These four sites provide a concentrated narrative of Delhi's major historical periods. Your guide recommends the optimal sequence based on your pickup location and traffic conditions. For a full exploration, book the [Old & New Delhi guided tour](/india/delhi/old-new-delhi-guided-tour)." },
      { question: "What should I wear on the Delhi half-day sightseeing tour?", answer: "Dress comfortably with these considerations: **Footwear** — comfortable walking shoes are essential (you walk approximately **2–3 km total** across monument visits, on stone and marble surfaces). **Clothing** — modest dress is recommended for religious sites: at Jama Masjid, women should cover shoulders and knees (scarves are available to borrow at the entrance). At Sikh gurudwaras (if included), both men and women must cover their heads (head coverings provided). **Weather** — light, breathable layers for October–March; sun hat, sunscreen, and loose-fitting clothes for April–September. **Sun protection** — sunglasses and SPF 50+ sunscreen are recommended year-round, as Delhi gets intense sun even in winter. Your guide carries a first-aid kit and extra water." }
    ];
  }

  // --- END ADDITIONAL DELHI TOUR FAQS (6 TOURS) ---

  // --- BANGKOK TOURS ---

  if (slug === 'bangkok-grand-palace-wat-pho-wat-arun-guided-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "How long is the Grand Palace, Wat Pho and Wat Arun tour?", answer: "The tour runs approximately **4 to 5 hours**, covering all three of Bangkok's most iconic temples in a single guided walk. You will spend about 90 minutes at the Grand Palace and Wat Phra Kaew, 75 minutes at Wat Pho (Reclining Buddha), and about 70 minutes at Wat Arun. The pace is comfortable and allows plenty of time for photos, questions and exploring each site thoroughly. Most tours begin at 9:00 AM, which helps you beat the worst of the midday heat." },
      { question: "Are the entrance fees included in the tour price?", answer: "Yes, **all entrance fees are included** in the tour price. This covers entry to the Grand Palace and Wat Phra Kaew (normally **500 baht** per person), Wat Pho (normally **300 baht**), and Wat Arun (normally **100 baht**). The traditional boat ride across the Chao Phraya River is also included. Having everything prepaid means you skip the ticket queues at each temple and walk straight in with your guide." },
      { question: "What is the dress code for visiting the Grand Palace?", answer: "All three temples require **modest dress**. Shoulders and knees must be covered for both men and women. No tank tops, shorts, miniskirts, see-through clothing or open-toed sandals. If you arrive underdressed, sarongs and cover-ups are available to borrow at the Grand Palace entrance, but they can be uncomfortable in the heat. We recommend wearing lightweight, breathable clothing that covers your shoulders and knees. Long cotton pants or a maxi skirt with a t-shirt works perfectly. Comfortable closed-toe shoes are also recommended since you will be walking on stone and marble surfaces for several hours." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup included in this tour?", answer: "No, this tour does not include hotel pickup. You meet the guide at the **Grand Palace main entrance**, near the Tha Chang Pier. The meeting point is easy to reach by taxi, Grab, tuk-tuk, or the Chao Phraya Express Boat. If you are staying near the river or in the Khaosan Road area, you can walk there in about 15 minutes. The guide will send you a WhatsApp message with exact meeting instructions the day before the tour." },
      { question: "What is the best time to visit the Grand Palace in Bangkok?", answer: "The best time is **early morning**, between 8:30 and 9:30 AM. The Grand Palace opens at 8:30 AM and the first hour is the least crowded. By 10:30 AM large tour bus groups start arriving and it gets significantly busier. Our tour is designed to start at 9:00 AM so you experience the Grand Palace during the cooler, quieter morning hours. If you are visiting between November and February (cool season), any morning time is pleasant. Between March and May (hot season), starting early is essential since temperatures can reach 35 to 38 degrees by midday." },
      { question: "Can children join this tour?", answer: "Yes, children of all ages are welcome. Children under **120 cm tall** get free entry to the Grand Palace. The walking distance is approximately 3 to 4 km total across all three temples, so children should be comfortable walking for a few hours with breaks in between. The boat ride across the river to Wat Arun is usually a highlight for younger visitors. Strollers are not practical at the temples due to steps and uneven surfaces. The guide adjusts the pace and commentary for families with children." },
      { question: "What is the difference between the Grand Palace and Wat Phra Kaew?", answer: "They are part of the same complex but serve different purposes. The **Grand Palace** is the former royal residence, a sprawling compound of throne halls, pavilions and government buildings built in 1782. **Wat Phra Kaew** (Temple of the Emerald Buddha) sits within the Grand Palace grounds and is Thailand's most sacred Buddhist temple. It houses the Emerald Buddha, a 66 cm jade statue dressed in gold seasonal robes that only the King of Thailand is permitted to change. You visit both with the same entrance ticket. The Grand Palace showcases royal and political history, while Wat Phra Kaew is the spiritual heart of the nation." },
      { question: "How do you get from Wat Pho to Wat Arun?", answer: "You cross the **Chao Phraya River** by traditional wooden boat. The crossing takes about 5 to 10 minutes and departs from the pier near Wat Pho (Tha Tien Pier). The boat ride is included in the tour price. It is a scenic crossing with views of the river, the Bangkok skyline and riverside temples. The boats run frequently so there is usually no waiting. Wat Arun sits on the opposite (west) bank of the river. After visiting Wat Arun, you can take the same boat back or continue exploring the Thonburi side independently." },
      { question: "Is the climb to the top of Wat Arun steep?", answer: "Yes, the steps at Wat Arun are **quite steep** and narrow. The main prang (spire) has steps at a roughly 45 degree angle leading to the upper terrace at about 30 metres high. There are handrails on both sides. The climb is optional and takes about 5 minutes each way. The reward is a panoramic 360 degree view over the Chao Phraya River, the Grand Palace across the water, and the Bangkok skyline. If you are uncomfortable with heights or steep stairs, you can skip the climb and still enjoy the beautiful mosaic work and grounds at the base level. Your guide will wait for you either way." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for first-time visitors to Bangkok?", answer: "This tour is **ideal for first-time visitors** and is the most popular guided tour in Bangkok. The Grand Palace, Wat Pho and Wat Arun are the three most important historical and cultural landmarks in the city. Visiting them with a knowledgeable local guide gives you essential context about Thai Buddhism, royal history and temple architecture that would be impossible to get on your own. Most visitors say it completely changed their understanding and appreciation of Bangkok. We recommend doing this tour on your first or second day to set the cultural foundation for the rest of your trip." },
      { question: "How big is the group on this tour?", answer: "This is a **small group tour** with a maximum of **10 people**. Most departures have between 4 and 8 guests. The small group size means you can actually hear the guide, ask questions freely, and move through the temples at a comfortable pace without getting lost in a crowd. It is a very different experience from the large bus tour groups of 30 to 40 people that you will see at the Grand Palace. The guide gives personalized attention and can adjust the pace and focus based on the group's interests." },
      { question: "What should I bring on the temple tour?", answer: "We recommend bringing: **comfortable walking shoes** (you will walk 3 to 4 km on stone and marble surfaces), **sunscreen and a hat** (most of the Grand Palace grounds have no shade), **a small bottle of water** (bottled water is provided but extra is helpful in the heat), **a camera or phone** (photography is allowed everywhere except inside the Emerald Buddha hall), and **a small amount of cash** for any personal purchases. Leave large bags at your hotel if possible. Lockers are available at the Grand Palace entrance for a small fee if needed." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'bangkok-maeklong-railway-damnoen-saduak-dragon-temple-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "How long is the Maeklong Railway Market, Floating Market and Dragon Temple tour?", answer: "The full day tour runs approximately **8 to 9 hours** from hotel pickup to hotel drop-off. Pickup starts between 7:00 and 8:50 AM depending on your hotel location, and you return to Bangkok by approximately 4:00 to 4:30 PM. The tour includes about 60 minutes at Maeklong Railway Market, 75 minutes at Damnoen Saduak Floating Market, and 60 minutes at the Dragon Temple, with driving time between stops." },
      { question: "What time does the train pass through Maeklong Market?", answer: "The train passes through Maeklong Railway Market **8 times per day**, roughly every 2 to 3 hours. The most common morning times are around **8:30 AM** and **11:10 AM**. Your guide times the visit to coincide with one of these train arrivals so you see the full spectacle of vendors folding up their stalls. The train horn sounds about 2 minutes before arrival, giving vendors time to pull back their awnings and goods. The whole process takes about 3 to 4 minutes and then everything goes back to normal within seconds. It is one of the most unique things you will see in Thailand." },
      { question: "Is the floating market at Damnoen Saduak touristy?", answer: "Damnoen Saduak is **Thailand's most famous floating market** and yes, it attracts tourists. That said, it is still a genuine, operating market where locals sell real produce, fresh food and handmade goods from their boats. The atmosphere is lively and colourful. The longtail boat ride through the narrow canals is the best way to experience it. Prices at the market are slightly higher than in Bangkok but you can bargain. Bring **Thai baht in small bills** (20, 50 and 100 baht notes) as vendors often cannot make change for large notes. The best food buys are fresh coconut ice cream, mango sticky rice, pad thai and grilled seafood." },
      { question: "What is the Dragon Temple and where is it?", answer: "Wat Samphran, commonly called the **Dragon Temple**, is located in Nakhon Pathom province about 40 km west of Bangkok. It features a striking **17-storey pink cylindrical tower** wrapped by a massive green and gold dragon sculpture that spirals from the base to the top. You can walk through a tunnel inside the dragon and climb the tower for panoramic countryside views. The temple was built in the 1980s and is one of the most unique and photogenic temples in Thailand. Unlike Bangkok's famous temples, the Dragon Temple is still relatively unknown to international tourists, so it is usually quiet and uncrowded." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup included in this tour?", answer: "Yes, **complimentary hotel pickup and drop-off** is included for hotels within 8 km driving distance of Khaosan Road and the Rambuttri area. If your hotel is outside this zone, you can meet the group at one of five central Bangkok pickup points: **Pratunam Market** (07:10 AM), **Chinatown Yaowarat** (07:30 AM), **MRT Sam Yot Station Exit 3** (08:00 AM), **Khaosan Road** at Lampoo Ocean Travel (08:30 AM), or **Central Pinklao** (08:50 AM). Your exact pickup time is confirmed via WhatsApp the day before the tour." },
      { question: "What type of vehicle is used for transportation?", answer: "The tour uses a **comfortable air-conditioned van** for all transportation. The drive from Bangkok to the first stop (Maeklong) takes approximately 60 to 90 minutes depending on morning traffic. The van has reclining seats, tinted windows and USB charging ports. Since this is a shared group tour with a maximum of 12 guests, the van makes several hotel pickup stops before leaving Bangkok. The total pickup process can take 60 to 90 minutes, so bring something to read or listen to for the morning drive." },
      { question: "Is the longtail boat ride at Damnoen Saduak included?", answer: "Yes, the **longtail boat ride** through the canals of Damnoen Saduak Floating Market is included in the tour price. The boat cruise lasts about 20 to 30 minutes and takes you through the narrow waterways lined with wooden houses and market stalls. Vendors paddle up alongside your boat selling fruits, drinks and souvenirs. The boat has a canopy for shade. This is the traditional and best way to experience the floating market, rather than walking the concrete paths along the canal edges." },
      { question: "Can you climb inside the Dragon Temple tower?", answer: "Yes, you can **walk through the dragon tunnel** inside the tower and climb the stairs toward the upper levels. The dragon's body forms a tunnel that wraps around the outside of the cylindrical tower. Some sections are steep and narrow, so wear comfortable shoes with good grip. The climb rewards you with **panoramic views** of the Nakhon Pathom countryside and surrounding temples. The climb is optional and suitable for anyone with reasonable fitness. There are rest points along the way. The interior also contains Buddha statues and small shrines at various levels." },
      { question: "Is lunch included in the tour?", answer: "Lunch is **not included** in the tour price, but both markets offer excellent and affordable food options. At Damnoen Saduak Floating Market you can buy fresh pad thai, grilled seafood, coconut ice cream, mango sticky rice and tropical fruit smoothies directly from the boat vendors, with most items costing **30 to 80 baht** (about $1 to $2.50). At Maeklong Railway Market there are also food stalls selling grilled meats, fresh fruit and Thai snacks. Your guide can recommend the best food vendors at each market. Bring at least **300 to 500 baht** in small bills for food and purchases." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children?", answer: "Yes, children aged **5 and above** will enjoy this tour. The train passing through Maeklong Market is exciting and memorable for kids. The longtail boat ride is fun and safe (life jackets available). The Dragon Temple's dragon tunnel feels like an adventure. That said, the tour involves an early morning start (7:00 to 8:50 AM pickup) and a long day with several hours of driving. Very young children or toddlers may find the day tiring. The van has air conditioning and there are restroom stops between each attraction. Children under 120 cm typically get free or reduced entry at the Dragon Temple." },
      { question: "What should I wear and bring on this tour?", answer: "Wear **comfortable walking shoes** and lightweight clothing. At the Dragon Temple, shoulders and knees should be covered (standard Thai temple dress code). Bring **sunscreen, a hat and sunglasses** as much of the tour is outdoors. Pack **Thai baht in small bills** (500 baht minimum recommended) for food and market purchases. A **light rain jacket** is advisable during rainy season (June to October). Bring your camera or phone as all three stops are extremely photogenic. A small backpack is more practical than a handbag since you will be getting in and out of boats and vans throughout the day." },
      { question: "What is the best time of year for this tour?", answer: "This tour operates year-round but the best months are **November to February** (cool season) when temperatures are comfortable at 25 to 32 degrees with low humidity and clear skies. **March to May** (hot season) is very hot, reaching 35 to 38 degrees, so bring extra water and sun protection. **June to October** (rainy season) can bring afternoon showers but mornings are usually dry and the markets are less crowded. The floating market and railway market are covered or have shade, so rain rarely cancels the tour. The Dragon Temple is best photographed in morning light regardless of season." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'bangkok-ancient-city-erawan-museum-tour' || slug === 'bangkok-ancient-city-erawan-museum-tickets') {
    return [
      { question: "What is Ancient City (Muang Boran) and is it worth visiting?", answer: "Ancient City, or Muang Boran, is the world's largest open-air museum — a 200-acre park in Samut Prakan, 33 km south of Bangkok. It contains 116 scaled replicas of Thailand's most important historical monuments, including the Grand Palace, Ayutthaya temples, and regional wonders from all 77 provinces. Many replicas are built on the same scale as the originals. It is genuinely worth visiting if you want to understand Thai history, architecture and culture without driving across the entire country. It takes between 4 and 6 hours to explore. Rent an electric buggy or bicycle at the entrance to get around comfortably." },
      { question: "What is the Erawan Museum and why is it famous?", answer: "The Erawan Museum houses a **giant three-headed bronze elephant** standing 43 metres tall — one of the most striking sculptures in Southeast Asia. Inside the elephant's body, a staircase leads to a domed chamber decorated with stunning Belgian stained glass and Rattanakosin-era antiques. The basement level contains a collection of ancient Thai, Chinese and European ceramics and artifacts. The museum is the personal vision of Thai businessman Lek Viriyaphant, who also created Ancient City. It is located in Bang Mueang, Samut Prakan, about 25 km from central Bangkok." },
      { question: "How do I get to Ancient City and Erawan Museum from Bangkok?", answer: "The easiest option is to take the **BTS Skytrain to Bearing station** (end of the Sukhumvit Line), then take a minivan or songthaew (shared pickup truck) from the bus stop outside the station — these run frequently and cost around 20 to 30 baht. Alternatively, take a **Grab taxi** directly from your hotel, which costs approximately 200 to 350 baht each way and takes 45 to 60 minutes depending on traffic. Do not attempt this journey on public transport from central Bangkok without at least a basic knowledge of the bus routes — the Bearing BTS route is far easier. Avoid rush hour (7:30 to 9:30 AM and 5:00 to 7:30 PM)." },
      { question: "How long should I allocate for Ancient City?", answer: "Plan for **at least 4 hours**, and up to 6 hours if you want to explore thoroughly. The park is enormous — 200 acres in a shape roughly resembling Thailand's geographical outline. On foot, you cannot see everything in one visit. Renting an **electric buggy** (around 500 baht per hour) or a **bicycle** (150 baht for the day) is highly recommended. Most visitors rent a buggy for 2 hours to cover the highlights, then walk through specific areas. Start with the central and southern zones, which contain the most impressive structures, then work outward depending on your time and energy." },
      { question: "Can I visit both Ancient City and Erawan Museum on the same day?", answer: "Yes, most visitors combine both on the same day. Erawan Museum is located about **8 km from Ancient City** and is easy to reach by Grab taxi in 15 to 20 minutes. The typical order is to visit **Erawan Museum first** (it is closer to Bangkok and takes about 1.5 to 2 hours), then continue to Ancient City for the rest of the day. If you start at 9:00 AM, you can easily do both and return to Bangkok by 4:00 to 5:00 PM. Some visitors prefer Ancient City first since it requires more energy. Ask your driver or guide to plan the most efficient route given Bangkok's traffic patterns." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children?", answer: "Yes, both attractions are excellent for families with children. Ancient City feels like an adventure park with open spaces, ponds, and impressive structures to explore. Children enjoy the giant elephant at Erawan Museum and the stained glass dome inside it. The park is flat and easy to navigate with younger children. Bring **sunscreen, hats and water** as both sites are largely outdoors. Feeding fish in the ponds at Ancient City is a popular activity for kids. Note that the entrance ticket does not include electric buggy rental, which costs extra but is highly recommended for families with young children or anyone who wants to cover more ground." },
      { question: "What are the opening hours and ticket prices?", answer: "**Ancient City** is open daily from 9:00 AM to 7:00 PM. Adult tickets are 700 baht and children's tickets (under 14) are 350 baht. Buggy rental is 500 baht per hour and bicycles are 150 baht for the day. **Erawan Museum** is open daily from 9:00 AM to 6:00 PM (last entry 5:30 PM). Adult tickets are 450 baht and children's tickets (under 12) are 200 baht. Ticket prices may change, so confirm on the day. Both sites accept credit cards and cash. English-language audio guides are available at both attractions." },
      { question: "Are the replicas at Ancient City accurate representations of real temples?", answer: "Yes, the replicas are constructed with remarkable historical accuracy by Thai architects, artisans and craftsmen using traditional materials and techniques. Many were built using original blueprints and specifications. Some structures at Ancient City are actually **relocated originals** that were rescued from demolition elsewhere in Thailand, not replicas at all. The project was commissioned by Lek Viriyaphant starting in 1963 and construction continued for decades. The attention to decorative detail — particularly the tile work, gilded spires and carved stonework — is exceptional. Art and architecture scholars visit specifically to study the collection." },
      { question: "What should I wear and bring on this day trip?", answer: "Wear **comfortable walking or closed-toe shoes** as you will cover significant distances on paths, steps and sometimes uneven terrain. Both sites require modest dress — **shoulders and knees covered** for temple replicas and the Erawan Museum interior. Bring a hat and sunscreen as Ancient City offers limited shade. Pack water (though stalls inside sell drinks and snacks). Bring **cash in Thai baht** for buggy or bicycle rental, food stalls and souvenir shops. A fully charged phone or camera is essential — both sites offer extraordinary photography opportunities. A lightweight rain jacket is advisable from June to October." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'bangkok-photo-walk-hidden-gems-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What makes this Bangkok photo walk different from a standard sightseeing tour?", answer: "This is a **photography-focused experience** led by a professional photographer who knows Bangkok's most compelling visual locations — many of which are hidden from the standard tourist map. Instead of rushing between famous landmarks, you spend time in the right light at the right angles. The guide teaches you practical composition, light reading, and storytelling through images. You leave with a curated set of photographs from Bangkok's most atmospheric lanes, markets and temple alleyways rather than the same crowded shots everyone else takes at the Grand Palace. It suits both beginners and experienced photographers." },
      { question: "Which neighbourhoods and locations does the photo walk cover?", answer: "The exact route varies by session and time of day, but typically includes **Chinatown (Yaowarat)**, the historic Talat Noi riverside quarter, Chinese shrines tucked behind market streets, the Bang Rak neighbourhood, and hidden canal-side communities on Khlong Ong Ang. Each location offers a completely different visual palette — the layered neon and gold of Chinatown contrasts sharply with the crumbling wooden shophouses of Talat Noi. The guide selects routes based on current light conditions and avoids overlapping with tour bus crowds. [Explore more Bangkok hidden gems](/thailand/bangkok/things-to-do-in-bangkok) beyond the standard tourist trail." },
      { question: "What camera equipment do I need?", answer: "You do not need expensive equipment. The guide works with participants using **any camera, including a smartphone**. Many guests shoot entirely on iPhone or Android and produce stunning results. That said, if you own a mirrorless or DSLR camera, bring it along with a standard zoom lens (24–70mm equivalent) and a fast prime if you have one (50mm f/1.8 is ideal for low-light markets). Tripods are not generally practical in Bangkok's narrow laneways but compact flexible tripods can help in specific spots. The guide adjusts tips and techniques based on what equipment you are actually using." },
      { question: "What time does the photo walk start and how long does it last?", answer: "The walk runs in either a **morning session (7:00 to 10:00 AM)** or an **evening session (4:00 to 7:00 PM)**. Both are timed to capture golden hour light — the warm, soft light just after sunrise or just before sunset that makes Bangkok's weathered walls, gold temples and street scenes look extraordinary. The midday session is generally avoided as harsh overhead light flattens texture and creates unflattering shadows. The walk lasts approximately **3 hours** and covers 2 to 3 km on foot at a relaxed pace with frequent stops." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for complete beginners in photography?", answer: "Absolutely. The guide is experienced at adapting the session for photographers at every level. For beginners, the focus is on **composition fundamentals** — framing, leading lines, rule of thirds, foreground interest — using whatever camera you own. You do not need to understand ISO, aperture or shutter speed to benefit from the tour. The guide makes sure every participant gets photographs they are genuinely proud of. For more advanced photographers, the session goes deeper into light reading, street photography technique, and post-processing recommendations tailored to Bangkok's colour palette." },
      { question: "How big is the group on this photo walk?", answer: "This is an **intentionally small group** experience with a maximum of 6 participants. This matters significantly for a photography tour — if the group is too large, you cannot all position yourselves at the same angle, the guide cannot give individual feedback, and the presence of a large group changes how locals respond to the camera. Small groups move more discreetly through narrow lanes and produce more authentic images. Most sessions have 2 to 4 guests, which allows for highly personalized guidance and genuine creative exploration." },
      { question: "Is it appropriate to photograph local people and markets?", answer: "Yes, but always with respect. Your guide teaches you the ethics of **street photography in Bangkok** — how to read body language, when to ask permission versus when to shoot candidly, and how to interact positively with market vendors and residents. This is part of the creative and cultural education the tour provides. Bangkok's street life is extraordinarily photogenic and most people are comfortable being photographed, especially when you smile and show them the result. The guide acts as a cultural translator, helping you navigate neighbourhoods where a foreign photographer alone might feel uncertain." },
      { question: "What areas does the tour help me discover beyond famous Bangkok landmarks?", answer: "Most visitors to Bangkok spend the majority of their time at the [Grand Palace](/thailand/bangkok/grand-palace-bangkok), [Wat Pho](/thailand/bangkok/wat-pho) and Chatuchak Market. This photo walk intentionally takes you away from those areas into **Talat Noi**, Bangkok's oldest riverside quarter where Portuguese traders settled in the 18th century, and into community alleys where daily life continues largely unchanged. You photograph vendors sleeping beside their stalls at dawn, monks collecting alms, boat operators navigating the klongs, and the layered visual history of a city that has absorbed Chinese, European, and Siamese influences for 250 years." },
      { question: "What should I wear and bring?", answer: "Wear **comfortable walking shoes** and lightweight breathable clothing. The walk covers 2 to 3 km, sometimes through narrow, humid market lanes. Avoid white clothing as it can blow out in harsh sunlight. Dark, muted colours are also less conspicuous for street photography. Bring **a fully charged camera or phone** with enough storage space — you will want to shoot several hundred frames. A portable battery bank is useful. Bring **cash in small Thai baht** for any street food you want to try along the way (the guide knows the best stalls). Sunscreen and a light hat are useful for morning sessions." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'bangkok-scavenger-hunt-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What is the Khlong Toei Market Scavenger Hunt and how does it work?", answer: "This is an **interactive urban exploration game** set inside and around Khlong Toei Market — Bangkok's largest fresh food market and one of the most vibrant, unfiltered local markets in Southeast Asia. Your guide leads you through a series of clues, challenges and discovery tasks that take you deeper into the market than any standard tour would. You solve riddles, find specific vendors, identify unusual ingredients, and interact with market regulars along the way. It is part game, part cultural deep-dive, and a completely fresh way to experience a part of Bangkok that most tourists never see." },
      { question: "What is Khlong Toei Market and why is it significant?", answer: "Khlong Toei Market is Bangkok's **largest wet market**, operating since the 1940s in the port district near the river. It supplies much of Bangkok's restaurant industry with fresh seafood, meat, tropical fruit, vegetables, and specialty ingredients. It is not a tourist market — it is where chefs, restaurant owners, hotel kitchens and local families actually shop. The atmosphere is raw, chaotic and completely authentic. The smells, sounds and visual spectacle of thousands of vendors operating at full capacity makes it one of the most photographically and culturally rich environments in the city. [See more Bangkok markets](/thailand/bangkok/chatuchak-weekend-market)." },
      { question: "Is the tuk-tuk ride included?", answer: "Yes, a **tuk-tuk ride** is included as part of the experience. You use Bangkok's iconic three-wheeled taxi to navigate between different sections of the area, which adds both speed and excitement to the scavenger hunt format. Your guide coordinates the tuk-tuk logistics so you can focus on the challenges. The tuk-tuk portion is a highlight in itself — Khlong Toei is a working neighbourhood where these vehicles are used by locals daily, rather than just for tourists, so the ride feels genuinely embedded in the local experience." },
      { question: "How long does the scavenger hunt last?", answer: "The tour runs for approximately **3 hours**. The scavenger hunt format keeps the energy high throughout, so it feels more dynamic than a standard guided walk of the same length. There are natural breaks built in — moments to taste something from a vendor, pause and talk to a market character, or take photographs. The challenges are designed to be achievable but genuinely engaging, building in difficulty as the session progresses. The tour typically ends with a final challenge that brings together everything you have discovered about the market and its community." },
      { question: "Is this tour family-friendly and suitable for children?", answer: "Yes, this tour is excellent for families with children aged **8 and above**. The scavenger hunt format naturally engages children in a way that conventional guided tours do not. Children often outperform adults at the observational challenges. The tasks are written to be interesting for mixed-age groups. Younger children (under 8) can participate but may need more support with reading challenges. The pace is relaxed and the guide adjusts challenge difficulty based on group composition. Families report this as one of the most memorable experiences they had in Bangkok. [Plan a Bangkok day with kids](/thailand/bangkok/1-day-bangkok-itinerary)." },
      { question: "What kind of challenges are included in the hunt?", answer: "The challenges are a mix of **observation, interaction and discovery**. Examples include: identifying specific Thai fruits or vegetables from a visual clue card, finding a particular type of vendor within a specific zone, counting something unusual, photographing a hidden detail in the market architecture, asking a vendor a specific question (with translation help from the guide), and tasting a challenge item. The hunt reveals genuinely interesting facts about the market — its history, the community living in the neighbourhood behind it, and the role of markets in Bangkok's food culture." },
      { question: "Is it safe to eat food in Khlong Toei Market?", answer: "Yes. Your guide knows which vendors have operated for years and maintains the highest standards. Khlong Toei Market supplies professional kitchens, which means hygiene standards are generally high even though the environment looks chaotic. **Cooked foods** from established stalls are always safe. Fresh seafood and meat are sold rapidly due to high turnover — nothing sits around long. The guide will direct you to specific vendors for tasting as part of the experience. Bring a small amount of cash (100 to 200 baht) for any additional purchases you want to make beyond the included tastings." },
      { question: "What makes this different from a standard Bangkok food tour?", answer: "Standard [Bangkok food tours](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-chinatown-food-tour-15-tastings-michelin-stops) follow a set route with pre-arranged tasting stops. The scavenger hunt format makes you an **active participant** rather than a passive follower. You make decisions, solve problems, navigate unfamiliar spaces and interact with the market on its own terms. The guide is a facilitator and cultural translator rather than a lecturer. Because you are discovering things yourself through the challenges, the information genuinely sticks. Participants consistently say the hunt changed their understanding of Bangkok's food culture in a way that a restaurant-hopping food tour never could." },
      { question: "What should I wear and bring?", answer: "Wear **comfortable closed-toe shoes** — the market floors can be wet and slippery, especially in the fresh meat and seafood sections. Light, breathable clothing is essential in Bangkok's humidity. Bring a small amount of **cash in Thai baht** (200 to 300 baht) for any purchases beyond the included content. Your smartphone is useful for the photographic challenges. Leave large bags or backpacks at your hotel if possible — the market lanes are narrow and busy. Wear clothing you do not mind getting a little damp or receiving market odours — this is an immersive, real-world experience, not a sanitised attraction." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'learn-muay-thai-bangkok-private-training-certified-fighter') {
    return [
      { question: "Do I need any Muay Thai or martial arts experience to join this class?", answer: "No experience is required. This private session is designed to accommodate **complete beginners** through to experienced practitioners. The certified trainer assesses your level at the start and structures the entire session around your current ability and goals. Beginners learn the fundamental eight weapons of Muay Thai — punches, kicks, elbows and knees — with heavy focus on correct technique, body mechanics and defensive positioning. If you have prior boxing or martial arts experience, the trainer adjusts the pace and complexity accordingly, introducing Thai boxing-specific combinations, pad work and ring awareness." },
      { question: "What does the private training session include?", answer: "The session includes a **full warm-up**, technique instruction covering Muay Thai strikes and defence, supervised pad work with the trainer, optional light sparring or partner drills depending on your comfort level, a cool-down stretch, and a briefing on Thai boxing culture and its history. **Free Muay Thai shorts are provided** on arrival so you can train in traditional style. Water, hand wraps and gloves are provided at the academy. The session runs for approximately **60 to 90 minutes** depending on the option booked. You also get a chance to see the gym, meet the fighters in training, and ask the trainer about their career." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup available and what are the options?", answer: "Yes, **three pricing options** are available: training only with no pickup ($60), training with one-way hotel pickup ($72), and training with round-trip hotel pickup ($85). Hotel pickup is available for most areas of Bangkok and covers all major tourist districts including Sukhumvit, Silom, Khaosan Road and Siam. The round-trip option is recommended if this is your first time visiting the gym, as it removes any navigation stress and ensures you arrive on time and relaxed. Pickup is in a comfortable air-conditioned vehicle." },
      { question: "What is FITFAC Muay Thai Academy and where are they located?", answer: "FITFAC is a professional Muay Thai academy with **11 branches across Bangkok**, making it one of the city's most accessible training facilities. The academy trains both competitive fighters and recreational learners. Your trainer is a certified and experienced fighter, not just a fitness instructor — meaning the technical instruction is genuinely high quality. The specific branch used for your session depends on your hotel location and preferred pickup point. FITFAC is a legitimate gym where real Thai boxers train daily alongside tourist participants." },
      { question: "Is Muay Thai training physically demanding?", answer: "A 60 to 90 minute Muay Thai session is a **significant cardiovascular workout** even at a beginner level. You will be sweating heavily by the 20-minute mark. The trainer monitors your energy and adjusts the intensity, ensuring you are challenged without being pushed to exhaustion or injury. Most participants describe the class as the most physical activity they do during their Thailand trip — which makes it even more satisfying. If you have any injuries, joint issues or health conditions, inform the trainer before the session begins. Modifications are always available." },
      { question: "What should I wear and bring to the Muay Thai class?", answer: "Wear **lightweight athletic clothing** you can move freely in — shorts and a t-shirt are ideal. Free Muay Thai shorts are provided so you can change into them at the gym. Bring a **small towel and a change of clothes** for after the session. Athletic trainers or bare feet are both acceptable for training. Bring a **water bottle** — though water is available at the gym, having your own ensures you stay hydrated throughout the session. Remove all jewellery and watches before training. A camera or phone is welcome — the trainer and staff are happy for you to photograph and video the session." },
      { question: "How does Muay Thai compare to Western boxing?", answer: "Muay Thai is far more comprehensive than Western boxing. Known as the **Art of Eight Limbs**, it uses fists (like boxing), elbows, knees and kicks — giving fighters eight points of attack compared to boxing's two. Clinch work (holding and striking at close range) is also a major element of Muay Thai that has no equivalent in Western boxing. Thai boxing also has a strong spiritual dimension — traditional fighters perform the Wai Kru ceremony before fights to honour their teachers. Your trainer will explain the cultural and religious significance of these rituals during your session." },
      { question: "Can I watch professional Muay Thai fights while in Bangkok?", answer: "Yes — Bangkok has two world-famous Muay Thai stadiums: **Rajadamnern Stadium** (established 1945) and **Lumpinee Stadium** (established 1956). Both hold regular fight nights featuring professional Thai and international fighters. Taking a Muay Thai training session before attending a live fight transforms the experience — you understand the techniques, the strategy and the difficulty of what the fighters are doing at a completely different level. Your trainer can recommend upcoming fight nights and explain how to buy tickets. See our [Bangkok travel guide](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-travel-guide-2026) for event listings and tips." },
      { question: "Is the training session suitable for children?", answer: "Yes, children aged **10 and above** can participate in the private session. The trainer adapts the session entirely for younger participants — focusing on coordination, basic strikes and pad work at an appropriate intensity. Many families book this as one of their most memorable Bangkok activities. Younger children enjoy hitting the pads and wearing the traditional Muay Thai shorts. Parents can participate alongside children in a shared session. Muay Thai is an important part of Thai culture, and learning even the basics gives children a genuine window into Thai martial tradition that they will talk about for years." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'bangkok-floating-market-railway-market-day-trip-boat-ride') {
    return [
      { question: "What floating market is visited on this day trip?", answer: "This tour visits **Damnoen Saduak Floating Market** — the most famous and visually spectacular floating market in Thailand. The market operates in a network of narrow canals in Ratchaburi province, about 100 km southwest of Bangkok. Vendors in traditional straw hats paddle their wooden boats loaded with tropical fruit, fresh coconut, pad thai, grilled seafood and souvenirs through the waterways. The longtail boat ride through the canals is included in this tour, giving you the best possible perspective on the market. [See more about Bangkok's floating markets](/thailand/bangkok/floating-market-bangkok)." },
      { question: "Are there two options — meet at a point or hotel pickup?", answer: "Yes, this tour offers **two clear options**: a meet-at-point option for **$21 per person** (meet independently at a central Bangkok location) or a one-way hotel pickup option for **$43 per person**. The meet-at-point option is significantly cheaper and suits travellers comfortable with Bangkok's public transport or Grab app. The hotel pickup option is more convenient and includes a direct pickup from your accommodation. Note that this is a shared group tour with a maximum of **43 passengers** — a comfortable coach-sized group rather than a large bus." },
      { question: "What time does the tour start and how long does it take?", answer: "The tour typically departs from Bangkok between **7:30 and 8:30 AM** depending on your pickup point. The drive to Damnoen Saduak takes approximately 1.5 to 2 hours depending on traffic. You spend around 1 to 1.5 hours at the floating market, then visit Maeklong Railway Market, and return to Bangkok in the early afternoon, arriving back by approximately **2:00 to 3:00 PM**. The full tour duration is approximately 6 to 7 hours. Starting early is important — both markets are most active in the morning and the floating market is significantly more crowded after 10:00 AM." },
      { question: "Is the longtail boat ride included?", answer: "Yes, the **longtail boat ride** through the canals at Damnoen Saduak is included in the tour price. The boat takes you through the narrow waterways alongside the market vendors, giving you close access to the boats and stalls. The ride lasts approximately 20 to 30 minutes. This is the authentic way to experience the floating market — walking along the canal paths gives a much less immersive experience. The boat has a canopy for shade and vendors paddle up alongside you throughout the ride. Bring cash in small Thai baht bills for any purchases you want to make from boat vendors." },
      { question: "What happens at Maeklong Railway Market?", answer: "**Maeklong Railway Market** (Talad Rom Hub) is one of Thailand's most unique and photographed attractions. A fully operational train line runs directly through the middle of the market, with vendors setting up their stalls along — and sometimes directly on — the railway tracks. When the train arrives, they retract their awnings and pull in their goods, then reset everything within seconds of the train passing. The train passes through **8 times per day**, and your guide times the visit to coincide with one of these arrivals. The spectacle lasts 3 to 5 minutes and is utterly unforgettable. [Plan your full Bangkok itinerary](/thailand/bangkok/1-day-bangkok-itinerary) around these experiences." },
      { question: "Is the tour suitable for people who get seasick or uncomfortable on boats?", answer: "The longtail boat ride at Damnoen Saduak is on a **calm, narrow canal** — not the open sea. The water is completely still and the ride is smooth, similar to a canal boat or punt. Almost anyone can handle it comfortably. The boat does make a loud engine noise (longtail engines are notoriously loud) and the wake can splash slightly, so keep this in mind if you carry expensive camera equipment. Earplugs are worth bringing if you are noise-sensitive. The boat ride lasts only 20 to 30 minutes. People with severe claustrophobia should note the canals are narrow and bordered by market stalls on both sides." },
      { question: "What food can I buy at the floating market?", answer: "Damnoen Saduak offers some of Thailand's most photogenic and delicious market food. From the boat vendors, you can buy **coconut ice cream** served in a half coconut shell, **mango sticky rice**, fresh tropical fruits, **pad thai** cooked to order in a wok on the boat, grilled corn, and coconut pancakes. At the market stalls along the canal edge, you will find grilled seafood, fresh noodle soups, and Thai desserts. Prices are slightly above Bangkok street food prices but the quality is generally good. Bring **200 to 400 baht** in small bills (20, 50 and 100 baht notes) as vendors often cannot change large bills." },
      { question: "How is this tour different from the Maeklong Railway & Dragon Temple tour?", answer: "This tour focuses on the **floating market experience** with the included longtail boat ride as its centrepiece, while the [Maeklong Railway, Damnoen Saduak & Dragon Temple tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-maeklong-railway-damnoen-saduak-dragon-temple-tour) adds the remarkable Wat Samphran Dragon Temple to the itinerary. Both tours visit Maeklong Railway Market. The key difference is this tour includes a longer market boat ride and is better suited to guests who want more time at the floating market. The Dragon Temple tour is better for guests interested in offbeat Bangkok architecture and temple photography. Both are worthwhile if you have multiple days." },
      { question: "What should I bring and wear on this tour?", answer: "Wear **comfortable, lightweight clothing** with shoes you can slip off easily if needed (longtail boats sometimes require removing shoes before boarding). Bring a **hat and sunscreen** — the floating market is almost entirely outdoors with limited shade. Pack **cash in small Thai baht** (400 to 600 baht recommended) for food, boat purchases and souvenirs. A **waterproof case** for your phone is worth having on the longtail boat. Bring a light rain jacket if visiting during rainy season (June to October). A small day bag is more practical than a large backpack on the boat. Arrive at your pickup point 5 minutes early — the tour departs on schedule." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'bangkok-street-food-tuk-tuk-night-tour' || slug === 'pak-khlong-talat-flower-market-evening-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What is included in the Bangkok After Dark Street Food and Tuk-Tuk Night Tour?", answer: "This tour includes **10+ food tastings** across Bangkok's most atmospheric night-time street food districts, all transportation by tuk-tuk between stops, a professional local guide who has deep knowledge of Bangkok's street food scene, and water throughout the tour. The tour is operated by a licensed local partner — one of Thailand's first 100% locally owned food tour companies, founded by a team of Bangkok-born food experts. You taste a genuinely diverse range of Thai dishes — not just pad thai and spring rolls, but regional specialities, fermented flavours and dishes that would be difficult to find or identify without a guide." },
      { question: "What type of food is included in the tastings?", answer: "The tastings are carefully curated to represent the full spectrum of Bangkok's street food culture. Expect dishes such as **boat noodles** (a rich pork or beef broth noodle soup), **khao moo daeng** (red pork on rice), **kanom krok** (coconut milk pancakes), fresh tropical fruit, **mango sticky rice**, various satays and grilled meats, **pad see ew** (stir-fried flat noodles), and dessert items from traditional Thai sweet vendors. The guide explains the cultural and regional origin of each dish. Vegetarian accommodations are available with advance notice — inform the operator when booking." },
      { question: "Where does the tour start and what are the departure times?", answer: "The tour meets at **Sam Yan MRT Station, Exit 2** — a central and easy-to-find location accessible by the MRT underground line from most parts of Bangkok. Three departure times are available: **6:00 PM, 6:30 PM, and 7:00 PM**. Arriving by MRT is strongly recommended as Bangkok traffic is heaviest between 5:30 and 7:30 PM. The nearest MRT stations to major tourist areas are: Silom (1 stop), Lumphini (2 stops), and Sukhumvit (interchange to BTS). The tour lasts approximately **4 hours**, returning by approximately 10:00 to 11:00 PM." },
      { question: "Is the tuk-tuk ride safe and comfortable?", answer: "Yes, tuk-tuks are a **safe and iconic** way to experience Bangkok at night. The operator uses licensed tuk-tuk drivers who know the routes well and navigate Bangkok's night-time traffic efficiently. The vehicles are open-sided, which is actually perfect for Bangkok's warm evenings and allows you to take in the city sights, smells and atmosphere as you move between food stops. The night-time tuk-tuk ride past illuminated temples, wet markets and neon-lit food alleys is a highlight in itself. Wear a light layer if you feel the breeze at speed, and hold your bags inside the vehicle." },
      { question: "How many food stops are there on the tour?", answer: "The tour makes **at least 10 tasting stops** across different Bangkok neighbourhoods and street food districts. Each stop introduces a different type of vendor, cuisine style or ingredient. The mix includes longstanding iconic vendors that have operated for decades, family-run stalls, fresh market producers, and dessert specialists. The guide provides context at each stop about the food's history, the vendor's story and the cultural significance of the dish. The combination of tuk-tuk rides and frequent short stops keeps the tour dynamic and prevents the food fatigue that can come from sitting in one restaurant for an extended period." },
      { question: "Is this tour operated by a local Thai company?", answer: "Yes. Your operating partner is one of Thailand's **first 100% locally owned food tour company**. The team is made up of Bangkok-born food enthusiasts, not an international tourism operator. This matters for authenticity — the vendor relationships, neighbourhood knowledge and cultural insights come from people who grew up eating this food, not from a foreign company that researched Bangkok from a guidebook. Supporting local operators also directly benefits Bangkok's street food vendors and communities. The company has been operating since 2010 and has a strong track record." },
      { question: "Is this suitable for people with dietary restrictions?", answer: "The guide can accommodate **vegetarians and pescatarians** with advance notice, as Bangkok's street food scene has many excellent plant-based and seafood-only options. Vegan accommodation is more limited but possible for most stops. **Gluten-free** travellers should note that soy sauce and fish sauce (both contain wheat or seafood) are common in Thai street cooking and complete avoidance is difficult. Nut allergies should be clearly communicated — peanuts are commonly used in Thai dishes. Those with shellfish allergies should also inform the guide. The tour is not suitable for guests with severe food allergies as cross-contamination in street food environments cannot be guaranteed." },
      { question: "What is Bangkok's street food scene like compared to other Asian cities?", answer: "Bangkok is consistently ranked among the **top 3 street food cities in the world**, alongside Singapore and Hong Kong. What distinguishes Bangkok is the extraordinary diversity within a single city — you can eat Chinese-influenced roast duck and noodle soups in Chinatown, fiery Isan grilled meats and papaya salads in the northeast Thai community areas, and refined Royal Thai cuisine refinements in historic neighbourhoods, all within a few kilometres of each other. The street food scene operates 24 hours and ranges from 30-baht noodle bowls to Michelin Bib Gourmand vendors. [Explore all Bangkok food experiences](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-chinatown-food-tour-15-tastings-michelin-stops)." },
      { question: "What should I wear and bring on the night food tour?", answer: "Bangkok evenings are warm but occasionally breezy during tuk-tuk rides. Wear **light, breathable clothing** and comfortable walking shoes (you will walk between stops at each tasting location). The dress code is casual — no temple visits are included so modest covering is not required. Bring **a small amount of Thai baht** (200 to 400 baht) in case you want to purchase extra portions or souvenirs from vendors. Your smartphone is essential for photographs — Bangkok's street food stalls look spectacular under neon and market lighting at night. Arrive at Sam Yan MRT Exit 2 at least 5 minutes before your chosen departure time." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'bangkok-chinatown-food-tour-15-tastings-michelin-stops') {
    return [
      { question: "What is included in the Bangkok Chinatown Food Tour?", answer: "This tour includes **15 or more food tastings** at Bangkok's most celebrated Chinatown street food vendors, including multiple stops that have received **Michelin recognition** (Bib Gourmand or Michelin Guide listed). Your guide is a food expert who grew up eating in Yaowarat and maintains genuine relationships with the vendors. The price of $70 per person includes all food, water throughout the tour, and a professional culinary guide. Maximum 8 guests per tour ensures personal attention at every stop and means you can actually speak with vendors rather than watching from a crowd." },
      { question: "Which Michelin-recognised vendors are included on the tour?", answer: "The specific vendors rotate slightly based on availability and seasonality, but the operator consistently includes stops at vendors that appear in the **Michelin Guide Thailand**, including internationally recognized Chinatown institutions. Bangkok's Yaowarat neighbourhood has the highest concentration of Michelin-recommended street food in Thailand — a remarkable achievement for open-air food stalls. Your guide explains the history and significance of each Michelin-listed vendor and helps you understand what the inspectors recognized. Note that Michelin Bib Gourmand and Michelin Guide listings are awarded to vendors based on quality-to-price ratio and consistency, not just fame." },
      { question: "Where does the tour start and what are the departure times?", answer: "The tour meets at **Shanghai Mansion Bangkok**, a landmark heritage hotel in the heart of Yaowarat (Bangkok's Chinatown). This is a consistent and easy meeting point accessible by MRT (Hua Lamphong station, 10-minute walk) or by taxi or Grab from any part of Bangkok. Four departure times are available: **4:00 PM, 4:30 PM, 4:45 PM, and 5:00 PM**. The early evening timing is deliberate — Chinatown's famous street food scene fires up in the late afternoon, with the peak atmosphere and freshest cooking between 5:00 and 9:00 PM. The tour lasts **4 hours**, finishing by approximately 8:00 to 9:00 PM." },
      { question: "What types of food will I taste on the Chinatown food tour?", answer: "Bangkok's Chinatown is a uniquely Sino-Thai culinary world with dishes you will not find elsewhere in Thailand. Expect tastings of **roast duck and red roast pork** from Chinese-style BBQ operators, **boat noodles** with pork blood broth, **oyster omelette** (hoi thod) cooked on a massive flat-top wok, **crab fried rice** from wok specialists, traditional **Chinese herbal teas**, **durian**, fresh tropical fruit, Thai-Chinese desserts including tong yip (egg threads in syrup) and bua loi (rice flour balls in coconut milk), and various grilled seafood from the famous Yaowarat vendor stretch." },
      { question: "Is the tour suitable for people who do not eat pork?", answer: "The operator can accommodate **halal or pork-free** dietary requirements with advance notice. Chinatown's food scene is heavily pork-influenced (roast pork and pork-based broths are central to Sino-Thai cooking), but the guide can select alternative stops and dishes that provide an equally rich experience without pork. Tell us when booking and they will customise the route. Shellfish-free and vegetarian accommodations are also possible with advance notice, though some creative substitution is required to maintain the full tasting experience." },
      { question: "Why is Bangkok's Chinatown considered one of the best street food areas in the world?", answer: "Bangkok's Yaowarat district has been a hub of Chinese immigrant commerce and culture since the early 19th century. The community brought Teochew, Cantonese and Hokkien cooking traditions that fused over two centuries with Thai ingredients and flavours to create a **uniquely Sino-Thai culinary identity**. The result is extraordinary — dishes that do not exist anywhere else in the world. The density of high-quality vendors per block is unmatched anywhere in Bangkok. Many current vendors are third or fourth generation operators, meaning the recipes have been refined and perfected across decades. The [Michelin Guide Thailand](https://guide.michelin.com/th/en) consistently awards more recognition to Yaowarat vendors than any other Bangkok district." },
      { question: "How is this tour different from going to Yaowarat on my own?", answer: "Chinatown's vendor landscape is vast, confusing and constantly changing. Without local knowledge, you will likely end up at the most tourist-visible stalls (which are not always the best) and miss the extraordinary vendors hidden in side lanes, wet market corridors and unmarked shophouses. Your guide's personal relationships with vendors often means **better portions and special preparations** not available to walk-in customers. The guide also provides cultural and culinary context that transforms eating into understanding — you learn why these dishes exist, how they evolved, and what they reveal about Bangkok's Chinese community. [Explore more Bangkok food experiences](/thailand/bangkok/things-to-do-in-bangkok)." },
      { question: "Is durian included and what if I cannot eat it?", answer: "Durian is often included as one of the tasting experiences in Chinatown, where you can find the finest and freshest examples in Bangkok. If you genuinely cannot tolerate the smell or taste, your guide will have an alternative ready — usually a different seasonal fruit or a sweet item from a nearby vendor. No one is ever forced to eat anything on the tour. That said, the guides are skilled at introducing durian in a way that helps even sceptics appreciate it. Trying **fresh, ripe Monthong durian** from a quality Chinatown vendor is a completely different experience from encountering processed durian products, and many first-timers are pleasantly surprised." },
      { question: "Can I join this tour solo and will I be paired with others?", answer: "Yes, solo travellers are very welcome and frequently join. Because the maximum group size is **8 guests**, even if you are the only solo guest, the group dynamic is intimate enough to meet people naturally. The small group format encourages conversation between guests, and the shared experience of discovering extraordinary food together is a natural social catalyst. Many solo travellers report meeting other guests they stayed in touch with after the tour. The guide also facilitates introductions and conversations. This is one of the most recommended solo traveller experiences in Bangkok on TripAdvisor and Google." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'ayutthaya-ancient-temples-day-trip-bangkok-thai-lunch') {
    return [
      { question: "How far is Ayutthaya from Bangkok and how long is the journey?", answer: "Ayutthaya is located approximately **80 km north of Bangkok** and the journey takes about **1.5 to 2 hours** by air-conditioned van, depending on traffic. The route follows the main highway and is generally smooth. Morning departures between 7:00 and 8:00 AM avoid the worst Bangkok traffic. Ayutthaya is the closest major UNESCO World Heritage Site to Bangkok, making it ideal for a day trip. The ancient city was the capital of the Kingdom of Ayutthaya from 1351 to 1767 — over 400 years — before being destroyed by Burmese forces. It is one of the most important and moving historical destinations in Southeast Asia." },
      { question: "What are the four temples visited on this tour?", answer: "The tour covers four of Ayutthaya's most significant ancient temples: **Wat Phra Sri Sanphet**, the grandest royal temple of the old palace complex featuring three restored chedis containing the ashes of Ayutthayan kings; **Wat Mahathat**, where you see the iconic Buddha head entwined in the roots of a Bodhi tree — one of the most photographed images in Thailand; **Wat Ratchaburana**, built by King Borom Rachathirat II to honour his brothers who died in an elephant duel, with the original frescoes still visible inside the prang; and **Wat Phra Ram**, built on the cremation site of King Ramathibodi I, the founder of the Ayutthaya Kingdom." },
      { question: "Is the Thai buffet lunch included and what type of food is served?", answer: "Yes, an **authentic Thai buffet lunch at a local restaurant** near the historical park is included on standard options. The buffet features central Thai cuisine — dishes such as green and red curry, jasmine rice, stir-fried vegetables, grilled meats, soup, fresh fruit and Thai desserts. The restaurant is used by local tour operators specifically for its quality and air-conditioned comfort after a morning of outdoor temple walking. Note that the **English Tour without Lunch and Entrance Ticket** option (the cheapest option at $27.21) does not include lunch or entrance fees — these are paid separately on site at 80 baht per temple." },
      { question: "What are the different tour options and meeting points?", answer: "Four meeting-point options are available at different price points. The **English Tour without Lunch and Entrance Ticket** ($27.21) meets at River City Bangkok with start times of 7:30 AM, 9:00 AM or 12:00 PM — entrance fees and lunch are not included. The **River City Bangkok Meeting Point** ($37.10) meets at River City Bangkok with entrance fees and Thai lunch included. The **Old Siam Plaza Meeting Point** ($37.10) meets at The Old Siam Plaza, next to Starbucks, with everything included. The **Samsen Meeting Point** ($37.10) meets at Samsen Center Restaurant on Samsen Soi 2. All standard options drop off at **MBK Mall** near the BTS Skytrain at the end of the day." },
      { question: "What is the dress code for visiting Ayutthaya's temples?", answer: "All Ayutthaya temples require **modest dress** — shoulders and knees must be covered for both men and women. This applies to everyone without exception, including children. Bring a **lightweight scarf or sarong** to wrap around your shoulders or waist if needed — these can also provide some protection from the sun. Comfortable **closed-toe walking shoes** are highly recommended as the temple grounds involve walking on uneven stone, brick and grass surfaces. The sites are largely open-air and exposed, so bring **sunscreen, a hat and sunglasses**. The sun is intense year-round in Ayutthaya, and there is very limited shade between the ruins." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved in the Ayutthaya temples tour?", answer: "The tour involves **moderate to significant walking** across all four temple sites, covering approximately 4 to 6 km total over the day. The ground is uneven in places — ancient brick paving, grass, and stone steps are common. Temple stairs can be steep at some sites. The tour is not suitable for guests with serious mobility issues or those who cannot manage prolonged outdoor walking in heat. There are rest periods built in (lunch, vehicle transfers between temples) and the guide sets a comfortable pace. Most participants with average fitness complete the tour comfortably. Children aged 6 and above generally manage well with appropriate footwear." },
      { question: "Is Ayutthaya better to visit in the morning or afternoon?", answer: "**Morning is significantly better**. Ayutthaya is largely open-air and exposed, and by midday the heat and direct sunlight can be intense — particularly between March and May when temperatures reach 37 to 40 degrees. The morning light is also far better for photography of the ancient prangs, headless Buddha statues and crumbling brick walls. Starting between 7:30 and 9:00 AM means you experience the most atmospheric parts of the historical park before the day-tripping crowds arrive from Bangkok. The lunch break at midday provides a natural rest from the heat before any afternoon temple visits. [Plan your Bangkok trip timing](/thailand/bangkok/1-day-bangkok-itinerary) accordingly." },
      { question: "Can I extend my time in Ayutthaya and return independently?", answer: "Yes. If you want to spend longer in Ayutthaya, you can return to Bangkok independently by **train, minivan or bus**. The Ayutthaya train station is a short tuk-tuk ride from the historical park and trains run frequently to Bangkok's Hua Lamphong and Bang Sue stations (journey time 1.5 hours, cost 20 to 100 baht). Minivans from Ayutthaya's minivan terminal at Chao Prom Market run to Victory Monument in Bangkok (1.5 to 2 hours, around 70 baht). This option suits guests who want to explore beyond the four main temples — Ayutthaya has over 30 ruined temples, floating markets, and boat tours worth extending a day for." },
      { question: "What is the historical significance of Ayutthaya and why did it fall?", answer: "The Kingdom of Ayutthaya was one of **Southeast Asia's most powerful and wealthy states**, attracting diplomats, traders and missionaries from across Asia, Europe and Persia. At its peak in the 17th century, Ayutthaya was one of the largest cities in the world with over one million inhabitants. It maintained extensive trade relationships with China, Japan, Portugal, the Netherlands and France. The city fell in 1767 when Burmese forces breached its defences after a prolonged siege. The Burmese systematically destroyed the temples, melted down gold Buddhist statues, and burned the royal palace and archives. The destruction was so complete that much of Ayutthaya's history was permanently lost. Your guide brings these events to life throughout the tour." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'bangkok-temples-canals-local-life-bike-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "Do I need cycling experience to do this tour?", answer: "The only requirement is the ability to **ride a bicycle independently**. The 15–18 km route is completely flat with no hills, designed for all fitness levels. Guests aged 8 to 75 complete it comfortably. You do not need to be a regular cyclist — if you can ride a bike, you can do this tour. The guide keeps a relaxed, unhurried pace with frequent stops for photos, stories and street snacks. There is no pressure to keep up or push through." },
      { question: "How big is the group on this bike tour?", answer: "Maximum **8 riders per tour** — this is strictly enforced. Candbike deliberately keeps groups small to ensure a genuinely personal experience rather than a large convoy. With 8 people maximum, the guide can stop at any point, answer questions individually, and take detours that interest the group. The small group also makes it possible to access narrow canal paths and temple lanes that a larger group would disrupt or block." },
      { question: "What is included in the tour price?", answer: "The $23 per person price includes a **quality city bike with a sized-and-adjusted helmet**, bottled water throughout the tour, a rain poncho if needed, and your English-speaking local guide. Child seats and small frames are available on request — notify Candbike of children's heights when booking. The tour does not include hotel pickup (self-make-way to BTS Pho Nimit), food and drinks at market stops, personal purchases, or optional gratuities for the guide." },
      { question: "What is the Thonburi district and why is it special?", answer: "Thonburi is Bangkok's **west bank** — the older, quieter, less-touristed side of the city across the Chao Phraya from the Grand Palace. Before Bangkok became the capital, Thonburi was briefly the capital of Thailand under King Taksin (1767–1782). Today it retains a slower pace: wooden shophouses, community temples, canal-side homes and local markets that haven't been developed for tourism. Most visitors never cross the river to explore it, which is exactly what makes this bike tour so different from the standard Bangkok experience." },
      { question: "What time do the tours depart?", answer: "Tours depart twice daily: **09:00 (morning)** and **14:00 (afternoon)**. The morning tour is cooler and calmer — the canals are at their most atmospheric and the temples are quieter. The afternoon tour has slightly warmer temperatures but beautiful golden light, particularly along the Chao Phraya riverside towards the end of the route. Both tours run for 3 hours and return to the Candbike base (morning tour returns by 12:00, afternoon by 17:00). Tours operate **rain or shine** — rain ponchos are provided." },
      { question: "Where exactly do I meet the guide?", answer: "Meet at the **Candbike base near BTS Pho Nimit station, Exit 4**. After exiting, walk 50 metres and take the first left. The address is 373/2 Soi Somdet Phra Chao Tak Sin 8 (Soi Sae Sim), Khwaeng Bukkhalo, Khet Thonburi. Search **'Candbikebangkoktours'** on Google Maps for the exact pin. Arrive at least **10 minutes before your start time** for bike fitting and the safety briefing. Hotel pickup is not included — the BTS Skytrain (Silom Line) connects directly to Pho Nimit station." },
      { question: "What should I wear and bring?", answer: "Wear **light, comfortable clothing** and closed-toe shoes or sandals with a back strap. A hat is recommended for the afternoon tour, and sunscreen is essential — much of the route is exposed. Bring a **small bag or backpack** as the bikes have no storage. Carry **cash for optional market snacks and street food stops** — the local market midway through the tour is cash-only and vendors speak no English. The guide can translate and recommend. No special cycling gear is needed." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children?", answer: "Yes — guests aged **8 to 75** complete the tour comfortably. **Child seats are available** for small children who cannot ride independently; notify Candbike of children's heights in advance when booking. Small frames are also available. The flat, easy route is well-suited for families. The route through local neighbourhoods, canal paths and a market stop tends to engage children more than conventional museum or temple tours. The maximum 8-person group size means the guide can always keep track of the whole family." },
      { question: "How is this different from a longtail boat canal tour?", answer: "A longtail boat tour shows you Bangkok's canals **from the water** — a beautiful perspective but one where you are a passenger looking in. The bike tour puts you **inside** the communities alongside the canals, riding through the same lanes as residents, stopping at their market, and reaching temple courtyards that no boat can access. You interact with the neighbourhood rather than observing it from a distance. The two experiences complement each other well if you have multiple days — see our [Bangkok Canal Tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-canal-tour) for the on-water perspective." },
      { question: "Who leads the tours — are the guides local to Thonburi?", answer: "Tours are typically led by **Dan or Filip**, both long-term Bangkok residents with genuine knowledge of the Thonburi neighbourhood. They are not scripted guides reading from a fixed commentary — they know the families who have lived in these lanes for generations, which temples are worth stopping at (and which to pass), and where to find the best street snacks. The small group format means conversation flows naturally throughout the ride. Both guides speak fluent English and are happy to answer questions about Bangkok life beyond the standard tourist topics." },
    ];
  }

  if (slug === 'bangkok-chao-phraya-dinner-cruise-luxury-river') {
    return [
      { question: "What time does the dinner cruise depart and return?", answer: "The cruise departs at **8:00 PM sharp** from ICONSIAM Pier and returns at approximately **10:00 PM**. Check-in opens at 7:30 PM — arrive at least 30 minutes early to board, receive your welcome drink, and find your table before the ship leaves the dock. Late arrivals cannot board once the ship has departed, so plan your transportation accordingly. The pier is directly accessible via the BTS Gold Line (Charoen Nakhon station) or by taxi." },
      { question: "What food is served on the cruise?", answer: "The cruise features a **premium international and Thai buffet** with a wide spread: fresh seafood (prawns, squid, fish), grilled meats, sushi and sashimi, Thai curries (green, red, massaman), pad thai, fried rice, cold salads, fresh tropical fruit, and a full dessert station with cakes, pastries, and ice cream. The buffet is continuously replenished throughout the cruise. Vegetarian options are available. If you have specific dietary requirements or allergies, notify us at booking and the kitchen will accommodate." },
      { question: "Are alcoholic drinks included in the price?", answer: "The ticket includes a **welcome drink on arrival** and soft drinks, water, tea, and coffee throughout the cruise. **Alcoholic beverages are not included** but are available for purchase onboard at the bar — expect beer, wine, cocktails, and spirits. Prices are reasonable by Bangkok standards. You cannot bring your own alcohol onboard. If you want unlimited drinks, check whether a drinks package add-on is available at the time of booking." },
      { question: "Is there a dress code for the dinner cruise?", answer: "**Smart casual** is recommended. Most guests dress up slightly for the evening — collared shirts, nice dresses, closed-toe shoes. You won't be turned away in casual clothes, but flip-flops, tank tops, and beachwear will feel out of place on a luxury cruise. If you're coming from a day of sightseeing, a quick change at your hotel before heading to the pier is worth it. The atmosphere is elegant but relaxed — think nice dinner out, not black tie." },
      { question: "Can I choose between indoor and outdoor seating?", answer: "Yes. The cruise offers **air-conditioned indoor seating** on the 2nd floor with panoramic windows and **open-air seating** on the upper deck. Indoor is more comfortable (especially during rainy season June–October), while the upper deck gives unobstructed views and better photo opportunities as you pass Wat Arun and the Grand Palace. Both areas have full access to the buffet and entertainment. Seating preference can be indicated at booking but is subject to availability." },
      { question: "What landmarks will I see from the cruise?", answer: "The cruise route passes Bangkok's most iconic riverside landmarks, all illuminated at night: the **Grand Palace** complex, **Wat Pho** (Temple of the Reclining Buddha), **Wat Arun** (Temple of Dawn) with its porcelain-covered spire glowing against the sky, the **Rama VIII Bridge** with its dramatic cable lighting, historic riverside warehouses, and the skyline of modern Bangkok. The stretch past Wat Arun is the highlight — the temple lit up from the river at night is one of the most photographed views in Bangkok." },
      { question: "Is the cruise suitable for families with children?", answer: "Yes, the cruise is family-friendly and **children of all ages are welcome**. The buffet has options that kids enjoy (fried rice, chicken, desserts, ice cream), and the live entertainment with traditional Thai dancers in colorful costumes tends to hold children's attention. The ship is stable and spacious with railings on all decks. Children under a certain age may qualify for discounted or free tickets — check pricing options at booking. The cruise is also popular for **birthday celebrations and anniversaries** — notify us in advance for special arrangements." },
      { question: "What happens if it rains during the cruise?", answer: "The cruise **operates rain or shine** — it runs every night regardless of weather. The main dining area on the 2nd floor is fully enclosed and air-conditioned, so rain has zero impact on dinner or entertainment. The upper deck has partial cover but can get wet during heavy rain. During rainy season (June–October), a light jacket or wrap is useful for the air-conditioned interior. Bangkok rain showers are typically short and dramatic — many guests find a passing storm actually enhances the atmosphere on the river." },
      { question: "How do I get to ICONSIAM Pier?", answer: "**ICONSIAM Pier** is on Charoen Nakhon Road, next to the ICONSIAM shopping mall. The easiest options: **BTS Skytrain** — take the Gold Line to Charoen Nakhon station, then a short walk to the pier. **Taxi or Grab** — tell the driver 'ICONSIAM pier' (every driver knows it). **Free shuttle boat** — ICONSIAM operates free boats from Sathorn Pier (next to BTS Saphan Taksin). Allow 30–45 minutes from central Bangkok. Arrive by 7:30 PM for check-in." },
      { question: "Can I book this cruise for a special occasion?", answer: "Absolutely — the dinner cruise is one of the most popular choices in Bangkok for **birthdays, anniversaries, honeymoons, and proposals**. The combination of luxury dining, river views, and live entertainment creates a genuinely memorable evening. Let us know your occasion at booking and the crew can arrange special touches — a dedicated table, cake, or announcement during the entertainment. Group bookings for celebrations are welcome. For very large parties, private deck sections may be available." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Bangkok Longtail Boat Canal Cruise ---
  if (slug === 'bangkok-longtail-boat-canal-cruise-hidden-temples') {
    return [
      { question: "What should I wear for the longtail boat canal tour?", answer: "Wear **comfortable, casual clothing** you don't mind getting slightly splashed. The longtail boats move quickly and canal water can spray — avoid white or delicate fabrics. **Closed-toe sandals or water-friendly shoes** are ideal. Bring a hat and sunscreen for sun protection. If visiting temples during the tour, shoulders and knees should be covered (bring a light shawl)." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children?", answer: "Yes — children **ages 3 and up** are welcome and generally love the longtail boat experience. Life jackets are provided for all passengers. The boat ride is exciting but safe. Children under 3 can join but must sit on a parent's lap. The tour is a great way to show kids a different side of Bangkok beyond the busy streets." },
      { question: "How many people are on each boat?", answer: "This is a **small-group tour** with a maximum of **6-8 passengers per longtail boat**. This keeps the experience intimate and allows the guide to share stories and point out details you'd miss on a larger vessel. Private boat options may be available on request for families or small groups." },
      { question: "What temples and landmarks will we see?", answer: "The tour passes through Bangkok's historic canal network (khlongs) visiting **Wat Arun** (Temple of Dawn) and several lesser-known canal-side temples. You'll see traditional Thai stilt houses, floating markets, orchid farms, and local communities that still live along the waterways — a Bangkok that most tourists never discover." },
      { question: "Will I get wet on the boat?", answer: "You may get **light splashes** from canal water, especially when the boat accelerates or passes other vessels. It's usually minor — just a few drops. The boat has a canopy for shade but sides are open. If you're concerned, keep electronics in a waterproof bag or ziplock. The guide will warn you before any splashy sections." },
      { question: "What's included in the tour price?", answer: "The price includes: **longtail boat charter**, English-speaking guide, temple entrance fees, bottled water, and hotel pickup in central Bangkok. Lunch or snacks are NOT included but there are opportunities to buy street food along the route. Tips for the guide and boat driver are appreciated but not required." },
      { question: "How long is the actual boat ride?", answer: "The total boat time is approximately **1.5-2 hours**, with stops at temples and points of interest along the way. The full tour including pickup, boat ride, temple visits, and drop-off takes about **3-4 hours**. It's a well-paced experience with a good mix of cruising and exploring on foot." },
      { question: "Is there a best time of day for this tour?", answer: "**Morning departures (8-9 AM)** are ideal — the light is beautiful for photography, temperatures are cooler, and the canals are quieter. Afternoon tours work too but can be hotter. The canals have a different character in the morning when local life is most active — monks collecting alms, vendors setting up, kids heading to school." },
      { question: "What happens if it rains?", answer: "Bangkok rain is usually short tropical bursts. The boat has a **canopy cover** and the tour operates rain or shine. If there's a heavy downpour, the guide may adjust timing or route slightly. Honestly, a light rain on the canals can be atmospheric and beautiful. Ponchos may be provided, but bringing a light rain jacket is smart during monsoon season (June-October)." },
      { question: "Where does the tour depart from?", answer: "The tour includes **hotel pickup** from central Bangkok hotels (Sukhumvit, Silom, Khao San Road areas). The boat departs from a pier near the Chao Phraya River — your guide will take you there after pickup. If you prefer to meet directly at the pier, let us know at booking and we can arrange. Allow time for Bangkok morning traffic." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Ethical Elephant Sanctuary Day Trip ---
  if (slug === 'ethical-elephant-sanctuary-day-trip') {
    return [
      { question: "Is this an ethical no-riding sanctuary?", answer: "**Yes — 100% no riding, no chains, no hooks.** This is a genuine rescue sanctuary where elephants roam freely in natural forest habitat. You'll walk alongside them, feed them, and observe natural behaviors. The sanctuary follows international ethical standards — no performances, no bathing forced for tourists, and no bullhook training. Your visit fee directly funds elephant care and rescue operations." },
      { question: "How far is the sanctuary from Bangkok?", answer: "The sanctuary is in **Chonburi province**, approximately **2-2.5 hours from central Bangkok** by private van. The drive passes through Thai countryside and is comfortable in an air-conditioned vehicle. Pickup from Bangkok hotels is included. You can also join from Pattaya (about 1 hour drive)." },
      { question: "What should I wear?", answer: "Wear **comfortable clothes you don't mind getting dirty** — you'll be walking in nature and may get muddy. **Closed-toe shoes** with grip are essential (no flip-flops or heels). Long pants are recommended to protect against insects. Bring a change of clothes if you want. The sanctuary provides traditional mahout clothing to change into." },
      { question: "Is this suitable for young children?", answer: "Children **ages 4 and up** are welcome and usually have an unforgettable experience. The elephants are gentle and accustomed to visitors. Very young children (under 4) may find the full day too long and tiring. Children must be accompanied by an adult at all times near the elephants. This is one of the most educational wildlife experiences available for families." },
      { question: "What activities are included in the day?", answer: "Your day includes: **feeding elephants** their favorite fruits and vegetables, **walking with elephants** through the forest, learning about each elephant's rescue story, a **traditional Thai lunch**, and observing elephants in their natural mud baths and swimming holes. Some sanctuaries also include herbal medicine preparation for the elephants. It's hands-on and deeply personal." },
      { question: "How many elephants are at the sanctuary?", answer: "The sanctuary is home to approximately **8-12 rescued elephants**, each with their own name and rescue story. Many were rescued from logging camps, street begging, or tourist riding operations. The guides know every elephant personally and will share their individual histories — some stories are heartbreaking but the recovery is inspiring." },
      { question: "Is lunch included?", answer: "**Yes** — a traditional Thai lunch is included, served at the sanctuary. The meal is freshly prepared and features local Thai dishes. Vegetarian and vegan options are available — just let us know at booking. Water and refreshments are provided throughout the day. No alcohol is served at the sanctuary." },
      { question: "What time does the tour start and end?", answer: "Pickup from Bangkok hotels is typically **6:30-7:30 AM** depending on your location. You'll arrive at the sanctuary around 9:30 AM. The sanctuary program runs until approximately 2:30-3:00 PM. Return to Bangkok is around **5:00-5:30 PM**. It's a full day trip — plan accordingly and get a good night's sleep beforehand." },
      { question: "Can I touch and interact with the elephants?", answer: "You'll have **close, respectful interaction** — feeding elephants by hand, walking beside them, and being near them in their natural habitat. You can touch them gently when the guides indicate it's safe. Photography is encouraged. The key difference from unethical operations: **the elephants choose** to interact with you, they're never forced. If an elephant walks away, that's respected." },
      { question: "What's the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before the tour. This is important because the sanctuary needs to prepare food for both visitors and elephants in advance. Same-day cancellations or no-shows are non-refundable. If weather is severe (rare), the sanctuary may modify the program but tours proceed rain or shine — elephants love the rain!" },
    ];
  }

  // --- Death Railway & Hellfire Pass Day Tour ---
  if (slug === 'death-railway-bridge-river-kwai-hellfire-pass') {
    return [
      { question: "How far is Kanchanaburi from Bangkok?", answer: "Kanchanaburi is approximately **2.5-3 hours west of Bangkok** by road. The drive is scenic, passing through Thai countryside and sugar cane fields. Travel is in an **air-conditioned minivan or coach** with a comfort stop en route. Hotel pickup from central Bangkok is included — the early start is worth it for this deeply moving historical experience." },
      { question: "What is the Death Railway?", answer: "The **Thailand-Burma Railway** (known as the Death Railway) was built by Japan during WWII using forced labor — over **100,000 Asian laborers and 12,000 Allied POWs died** during its construction in 1942-43. The 415km railway was built in just 16 months through incredibly harsh jungle and mountain terrain. Today it stands as a memorial to their sacrifice. Walking Hellfire Pass and crossing the Bridge on the River Kwai makes this history tangible." },
      { question: "Is Hellfire Pass difficult to walk?", answer: "The main Hellfire Pass cutting is a **moderate walk** — about 30-40 minutes through the original rock cutting carved by POW laborers. The path is uneven with some steps and slopes, so **sturdy shoes are essential**. There's an optional longer trail (2-3 hours) for fit walkers. The main pass section is accessible to most fitness levels, including older visitors who take it slowly. It's emotional more than physical." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children?", answer: "Children are welcome but **parents should consider the content**. The war cemeteries and Hellfire Pass tell stories of suffering and death. Children under 10 may not fully understand the significance. Teenagers often find it deeply educational. The train ride and river scenery are enjoyable for all ages. Use your judgment based on your child's maturity and sensitivity." },
      { question: "What's included in the tour?", answer: "The tour includes: **hotel pickup and drop-off**, air-conditioned transport, English-speaking guide, **Hellfire Pass Memorial Museum** entry, walk through Hellfire Pass, visit to the **Bridge on the River Kwai**, **Death Railway train ride** (short scenic section), **Kanchanaburi War Cemetery** visit, and **Thai lunch**. Essentially everything except personal purchases and tips." },
      { question: "Is the train ride safe?", answer: "**Yes** — the scenic train ride covers a short, well-maintained section of the original railway. You'll cross the famous wooden trestle viaduct (Wang Po Viaduct) clinging to the cliff face above the river — it's dramatic but perfectly safe. The train moves slowly, allowing incredible photo opportunities. It's one of the most memorable train journeys in Southeast Asia." },
      { question: "Is lunch included?", answer: "**Yes** — a Thai lunch is included, typically served at a riverside restaurant in Kanchanaburi with views of the River Kwai. The meal includes several Thai dishes. Vegetarian options are available with advance notice. Water is provided throughout the day. You can also buy snacks and drinks at various stops." },
      { question: "What should I bring?", answer: "Bring: **comfortable walking shoes** (essential for Hellfire Pass), sunscreen, hat, insect repellent, water bottle, camera, and a light rain jacket during wet season. Dress respectfully for the war cemeteries — no beachwear. A small backpack is useful. The museum and some parts are air-conditioned, but most of the day is outdoors in tropical heat." },
      { question: "How long is the full tour?", answer: "This is a **full-day tour**: pickup around **6:30-7:00 AM**, return to Bangkok around **7:00-8:00 PM**. The day includes about 5-6 hours of driving total (broken into segments) and 5-6 hours at the various sites. It's a long but incredibly rewarding day. The guide keeps things moving at a good pace with enough time at each stop." },
      { question: "Is the Hellfire Pass Museum worth seeing?", answer: "**Absolutely** — the Hellfire Pass Memorial Museum (built and maintained by the Australian government) is one of the best WWII museums in Asia. It features personal accounts, original tools, photographs, and an excellent audio guide narrated by POW survivors. Allow at least **45-60 minutes** here. The museum provides crucial context before you walk the actual pass. Free entry is included in your tour." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Tuk-Tuk Chinatown Street Food Night Tour ---
  if (slug === 'tuk-tuk-chinatown-street-food-temple-night-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "How much food is included in the tour?", answer: "You'll sample **8-10 different dishes** across multiple street food stalls and local restaurants in Chinatown. The portions are tasting-sized so you can try everything without getting too full too fast. Expect a mix of savory, sweet, and unusual items — pad thai, mango sticky rice, roasted duck, dim sum, and specialties you'd never find on your own. Most people leave very full. Come hungry!" },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for vegetarians?", answer: "**Yes** — let us know at booking and the guide will swap meat dishes for vegetarian alternatives. Bangkok's Chinatown has excellent vegetarian options including tofu dishes, vegetable dumplings, fruit desserts, and noodle soups. Vegan can also be accommodated with advance notice. The guide knows every stall and can navigate dietary restrictions expertly." },
      { question: "How long is the tuk-tuk ride?", answer: "The tuk-tuk is your **transport between food stops**, not a sightseeing ride. Total tuk-tuk time is about **30-40 minutes** spread across the evening, with rides of 5-10 minutes between neighborhoods. The rest is walking through Chinatown's narrow streets and alleys on foot. The tuk-tuk experience itself is thrilling — open-air, weaving through Bangkok's neon-lit streets at night." },
      { question: "Is this safe for tourists?", answer: "**Absolutely** — you're with a local guide who knows every alley and stall. The tuk-tuk drivers are vetted and experienced. Chinatown is one of Bangkok's liveliest and safest neighborhoods at night with heavy foot traffic and well-lit streets. Keep valuables secure (as in any busy area) and you'll be fine. The guide handles all ordering and payments at food stalls." },
      { question: "What time does the tour start and end?", answer: "The tour starts at **6:00-6:30 PM** when Chinatown comes alive. You'll meet near a central Chinatown landmark (details provided after booking). The tour ends around **9:30-10:00 PM**. This is the perfect time slot — the streets are electric with neon signs, steam rising from food stalls, and the energy of Bangkok's oldest neighborhood in full swing." },
      { question: "Do I need to bring cash?", answer: "**No extra cash needed for food** — all tastings are included in the tour price. However, bring some Thai baht (**200-500 THB**) if you want to buy extra snacks, drinks, or souvenirs from the markets you'll pass through. Some stalls you'll want to return to! ATMs are available in Chinatown if needed. Credit cards are NOT accepted at street stalls." },
      { question: "Is this suitable for children?", answer: "Children **ages 5 and up** enjoy the tour — the tuk-tuk ride is exciting, the food is fun to try, and the atmosphere is memorable. Younger kids may find the late evening timing challenging. The guide can recommend milder dishes for kids who don't like spicy food. Kids pay a reduced rate. It's a fantastic way to introduce children to street food culture safely." },
      { question: "What temples will we visit?", answer: "The tour includes a visit to **Wat Traimit** (Temple of the Golden Buddha), home to the world's largest solid gold Buddha statue — 5.5 tons of pure gold, worth over $250 million. You'll also pass by several illuminated Chinese-Thai temples and shrines that are spectacular at night. The temple visits add cultural depth beyond just the food experience." },
      { question: "What if I have food allergies?", answer: "**Inform us at booking** about any allergies — especially shellfish, nuts, and gluten, which are common in Thai-Chinese cuisine. The guide will select alternative dishes and communicate with stall vendors in Thai to ensure safety. Severe allergies can be accommodated but please be specific. The guide carries the allergy information and checks every dish before you eat." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved?", answer: "Expect about **2-3 km of walking** over the evening, mostly flat through Chinatown streets and covered market areas. The pace is relaxed with frequent stops for food and photos. Wear **comfortable shoes** — Chinatown streets can be uneven and sometimes wet from food stalls. The tuk-tuk handles the longer distances between neighborhoods. Not strenuous but you'll be on your feet." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Muay Thai Boxing Tickets at Rajadamnern Stadium ---
  if (slug === 'muay-thai-boxing-tickets-at-rajadamnern-stadium') {
    return [
      { question: "What is included in the Muay Thai Boxing ticket?", answer: "Your ticket includes **reserved seating at Rajadamnern Stadium**, one of Bangkok's two legendary Muay Thai venues operating since 1945. You get access to the full fight card — typically **8-10 bouts** ranging from undercard matches to championship-level fights. The atmosphere is electric with live commentary, cheering crowds, and traditional Wai Kru dance rituals before each bout. No food or drinks are included but vendors inside the stadium sell Thai snacks and beverages." },
      { question: "What time do the fights start and end?", answer: "Fight nights at Rajadamnern Stadium typically begin at **6:00 PM** with the first bout, and the main event finishes around **10:00-10:30 PM**. Doors open approximately **30 minutes** before the first fight. We recommend arriving early to secure the best views and soak in the pre-fight atmosphere. Combine it with a late-night [Chinatown street food tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-tuk-tuk-chinatown-street-food-temple-night-tour) afterward — the stadium is just 10 minutes from Chinatown." },
      { question: "Which seating class should I choose?", answer: "**Ringside seats** put you within arm's reach of the action — you'll feel the impact of every kick and elbow. **Second class** offers elevated stadium seating with excellent sightlines. **Third class** is the cheapest and places you among the most passionate Thai bettors — an authentic cultural experience in itself. For first-time visitors, we recommend second class for the best balance of proximity and comfort. All classes have unobstructed views of the ring." },
      { question: "Is Muay Thai suitable for families with children?", answer: "Children **ages 10 and up** generally enjoy the spectacle — the Wai Kru dance, live music, and crowd energy make it exciting. However, Muay Thai is a **full-contact combat sport** with real strikes, so younger or sensitive children may find it intense. There's no age restriction but use your judgment. For a more family-friendly Thai cultural evening, consider the [river dinner cruise with cultural show](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-night-river-experience-with-dinner-cultural-show) instead." },
      { question: "How do I get to Rajadamnern Stadium?", answer: "Rajadamnern Stadium is located on **Ratchadamnoen Nok Road** near the Khao San Road area. The easiest way to get there is by **taxi or Grab** (about 100-200 THB from central Bangkok). The nearest BTS/MRT stations are a 15-20 minute walk. After the fights, taxis and tuk-tuks line up outside the venue. If you're staying near Khao San Road, it's a pleasant **10-minute walk**." },
      { question: "Can I bet on the fights?", answer: "Betting is a massive part of Muay Thai culture and you'll see Thai spectators making frantic hand signals throughout every bout. However, **organized betting is technically restricted to Thai nationals**. As a tourist, you should enjoy the spectacle rather than try to participate in the betting pools. The energy of the betting crowd — especially in third-class seats — is part of what makes Rajadamnern an unforgettable experience." },
      { question: "What's the difference between Rajadamnern and Lumpinee Stadium?", answer: "Both are premier Muay Thai venues but Rajadamnern (est. 1945) is the **older and more traditional** of the two. It's located in the historic district near Khao San Road, making it more convenient for tourists. Lumpinee recently moved to a newer facility in the Ram Intra area, further from central Bangkok. Fight quality is comparable at both venues — Rajadamnern hosts championship bouts on most fight nights and the atmosphere is widely considered more authentic." },
      { question: "What should I wear to the fights?", answer: "There's **no strict dress code** — casual clothing and comfortable shoes are fine. The stadium is air-conditioned in the seated sections but can still get warm with the crowd energy. Avoid wearing red or blue as these are the fighter corner colors and may cause confusion. Bring a light jacket as the AC can be strong in ringside areas. Photography is allowed but **flash photography is prohibited** during active bouts." },
      { question: "Can I leave and re-enter the stadium?", answer: "**No** — once you exit the stadium, re-entry is not permitted with the same ticket. Make sure you've eaten beforehand or plan to buy snacks inside. Restrooms are available inside the venue. The full fight card takes about **4 hours**, so come prepared for an evening-long experience. If you want dinner before, the [backstreets food tour with 15 tastings](/thailand/bangkok/backstreets-food-tour-with-15-tastings) is a great pre-fight option." },
      { question: "Is there a cancellation policy?", answer: "Tickets are **non-refundable once confirmed** as they are allocated seating passes for a specific fight night. However, date changes may be possible with **48 hours' notice** depending on availability. Contact us via WhatsApp immediately if your plans change. Fight cards are occasionally rescheduled by the stadium — in that case, your ticket transfers automatically to the new date or a full refund is issued." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Floating Market and Train Market Experience ---
  if (slug === 'floating-market-and-train-market-experience') {
    return [
      { question: "What time does the tour depart from Bangkok?", answer: "The tour departs from your Bangkok hotel at **6:00-6:30 AM** to beat the crowds and the heat. The early start is essential — Damnoen Saduak floating market is most active and photogenic in the **early morning** before 10 AM. The drive takes approximately **90 minutes** from central Bangkok. You'll return to Bangkok by **2:00-3:00 PM**, leaving your evening free for a [tuk-tuk Chinatown food tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-tuk-tuk-chinatown-street-food-temple-night-tour)." },
      { question: "Is the boat ride included in the price?", answer: "The **longtail boat ride through the floating market canals** is included in your tour price. This is a private boat for your group — not a shared vessel. The ride lasts approximately **30-45 minutes** and navigates through the narrow canals where vendors sell from wooden boats piled with tropical fruits, pad thai, coconut pancakes, and handmade souvenirs. Buying food and souvenirs from the boat vendors is at your own expense." },
      { question: "What happens at the Maeklong Railway Market?", answer: "Maeklong (also called the 'umbrella pulldown market') is a market built directly **on active railway tracks**. Eight times daily, a train passes straight through the market and vendors calmly fold their awnings and pull back their produce — then reset everything within seconds after the train passes. It's one of the most jaw-dropping sights in Thailand. Your guide ensures you're positioned for **the best photos and videos** of the train passing." },
      { question: "What are the train times at Maeklong?", answer: "Trains pass through Maeklong Railway Market at approximately **8:30 AM, 11:10 AM, 11:30 AM, and 2:30 PM** (return trains at 6:20 AM, 9:00 AM, 11:30 AM, 3:30 PM). Your tour is timed to catch the **most dramatic morning passage**. Arrive at least 15 minutes early to get a good viewing position — the guide will find the best spot. The market operates daily but the train times are the highlight, so the schedule is carefully planned." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved?", answer: "Expect **light to moderate walking** — about 2-3 km total across both markets on flat ground. At the floating market, much of the experience is from the boat. At Maeklong, you'll walk along the railway tracks through the market stalls. Wear **comfortable shoes** that can get slightly wet or muddy. The tour is suitable for all fitness levels including elderly travelers and children. Shaded areas are available at both markets." },
      { question: "Is lunch included?", answer: "**Lunch is not included** but you'll have ample opportunity to eat at both markets. The floating market has incredible boat-cooked pad thai, coconut ice cream, grilled prawns, and tropical fruit — all extremely affordable (**30-100 THB per dish**). Maeklong market has excellent Thai desserts and fresh seafood. Bring **300-500 THB** in small notes for food and purchases. Your guide can recommend the best stalls at each market." },
      { question: "How does this differ from the Damnoen Saduak standalone tour?", answer: "This tour combines **both the floating market AND the railway market** in a single trip, which is the most efficient way to see both since they're in the same region. The [Damnoen Saduak and Maeklong Railway Market tour](/thailand/bangkok/damnoen-saduak-market-and-maeklong-railway-market) from another operator covers the same two destinations with a slightly different itinerary and guide style. Both are full-day excursions from Bangkok — choose based on group size preference and pricing." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children?", answer: "**Yes** — children love this tour. The longtail boat ride is exciting, the floating market is colorful and full of treats, and watching the train roll through Maeklong market is unforgettable for kids. **Life jackets are provided** on the boat for all ages. The pace is relaxed enough for families. We recommend this as one of the top family activities from Bangkok alongside the [ethical elephant sanctuary day trip](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-ethical-elephant-sanctuary-day-trip)." },
      { question: "What should I bring?", answer: "Bring **sunscreen, a hat, and sunglasses** — the floating market is mostly open-air and it gets hot by mid-morning. A **small waterproof bag** for your phone is smart on the boat. Wear lightweight, breathable clothing. Cash in small denominations (**20 and 50 THB notes**) is essential for buying from market vendors — they don't accept cards. A portable fan or cooling towel is helpful during Thailand's hot season (March-May)." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before the tour start time for a full refund. Cancellations within 24 hours are non-refundable as transport and boat are pre-arranged. Weather rarely affects this tour — the markets operate rain or shine, and the boat has a canopy cover. In the unlikely event of severe flooding, we'll reschedule at no extra cost or issue a full refund." },
    ];
  }

  // --- White Lotus Thai Cooking Class with Market Tour ---
  if (slug === 'white-lotus-thai-cooking-class-with-market-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What dishes will I learn to cook?", answer: "You'll prepare **4-5 authentic Thai dishes** from scratch, typically including **Pad Thai, Tom Yum soup, Green or Red Curry, and a Thai dessert** like mango sticky rice. The exact menu varies by session and you may get to choose from a selection. Every dish is made using fresh ingredients you help select at the local market. You eat everything you cook — it serves as a complete, delicious lunch or dinner. Recipe cards are provided to take home." },
      { question: "What happens during the market tour?", answer: "The class begins with a **guided tour of a local Thai market** where your chef-instructor explains the key ingredients in Thai cuisine — lemongrass, galangal, kaffir lime leaves, palm sugar, and different chili varieties. You'll learn how to **select fresh produce** like a local and understand the difference between varieties of fish sauce and curry paste. This market visit typically lasts **30-45 minutes** and is included in the price. It transforms your cooking experience from just following recipes to truly understanding Thai flavors." },
      { question: "Can the class accommodate dietary restrictions?", answer: "**Yes** — vegetarian, vegan, gluten-free, and halal options are available with advance notice. The chef substitutes tofu or vegetables for meat, uses vegetable-based sauces, and adapts recipes without losing authentic flavor. **Inform us at least 24 hours before** your class so the kitchen can prepare appropriate ingredients. Severe nut or shellfish allergies can also be managed — Thai cuisine uses both extensively, so early communication is important." },
      { question: "How long is the entire experience?", answer: "The complete experience — market tour plus cooking class plus eating — lasts approximately **4-5 hours**. The market tour is **30-45 minutes**, followed by **2.5-3 hours** of hands-on cooking, and **30-45 minutes** to enjoy your creations. The pace is relaxed and social. If you want a shorter format, the [Tingly Thai Cooking School half-day class](/thailand/bangkok/tingly-thai-cooking-school-half-day-cooking-class) offers a more compact session." },
      { question: "Do I need any cooking experience?", answer: "**Absolutely not** — the class is designed for complete beginners. The chef demonstrates every technique step-by-step before you do it yourself. Each participant has their own **individual cooking station** with a wok, burner, and pre-measured ingredients. Whether you can barely boil water or you're an experienced home cook, the class adapts to your skill level. The instructor circulates to help everyone personally." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup included?", answer: "**Yes** — complimentary pickup is available from hotels in central Bangkok (Sukhumvit, Silom, Khao San Road, Riverside areas). Pickup typically happens **45-60 minutes** before the class starts. Drop-off after the class is also included. If your hotel is outside the standard pickup zone, the operator will suggest a convenient meeting point. Details are confirmed via WhatsApp after booking." },
      { question: "How many people are in each class?", answer: "Classes are kept **small — typically 6-12 participants** — to ensure personal attention from the chef. This is not a factory cooking class with 50 tourists. The intimate setting means you can ask questions, get real-time feedback on your technique, and interact with other travelers. Private classes for couples or families can be arranged upon request at a small surcharge." },
      { question: "Can children participate?", answer: "Children **ages 8 and up** can fully participate in the cooking. Younger kids are welcome to watch and taste but may find the full class too long. The chef is experienced with families and makes it fun for younger participants. Wok cooking involves high heat and hot oil, so children must be supervised. This is a wonderful alternative to a [Bangkok food walking tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-authentic-tasting-thai-chinatown-walking-food-tour) for families who prefer a hands-on experience." },
      { question: "Do I get to take home recipes?", answer: "**Yes** — every participant receives a printed or digital **recipe booklet** with all dishes from the session, including ingredient lists, measurements, and the chef's personal tips. The booklet also lists common Western supermarket substitutes for hard-to-find Thai ingredients. Many guests tell us these recipes become their go-to dinner party dishes back home. The recipes are exclusive to the cooking school and not available online." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before the class for a full refund. Cancellations within 24 hours are non-refundable as ingredients are purchased fresh that morning specifically for your session. If you need to change dates, contact us as early as possible and we'll do our best to accommodate. The class runs rain or shine — it's held in a covered kitchen space." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Damnoen Saduak Market and Maeklong Railway Market ---
  if (slug === 'damnoen-saduak-market-and-maeklong-railway-market') {
    return [
      { question: "How is this tour different from other floating market tours?", answer: "This tour combines **two of Thailand's most iconic markets** in a single day trip — the Damnoen Saduak floating market (where vendors sell from boats on canals) and the Maeklong railway market (where a train passes through the stalls). The combination is the most efficient way to experience both since they're only **30 minutes apart** by road. We also visit a coconut sugar farm along the way. For a similar itinerary from a different operator, see our [Floating Market and Train Market Experience](/thailand/bangkok/floating-market-and-train-market-experience)." },
      { question: "What time should I expect to return to Bangkok?", answer: "You'll return to your Bangkok hotel by approximately **2:00-3:00 PM**, giving you the entire afternoon and evening free. This makes it perfect to pair with an evening activity like the [Muay Thai boxing at Rajadamnern Stadium](/thailand/bangkok/muay-thai-boxing-tickets-at-rajadamnern-stadium) or a [tuk-tuk Chinatown night food tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-tuk-tuk-chinatown-street-food-temple-night-tour). The early morning departure (**6:00-6:30 AM**) ensures you experience the markets at their most vibrant." },
      { question: "Is the longtail boat ride included?", answer: "**Yes** — a private longtail boat ride through the floating market canals is included in the tour price. The boat ride lasts **30-45 minutes** and takes you through narrow waterways where vendors paddle wooden boats laden with fruits, noodles, and souvenirs. The boat has a canopy for shade. Purchasing food or souvenirs from boat vendors is at your own expense — bring **200-500 THB** in small notes." },
      { question: "What is the coconut sugar farm visit?", answer: "En route between the two markets, you'll stop at a **traditional coconut sugar farm** where you watch the entire process — from tapping the coconut palm flowers for sap, to boiling the sap into rich caramel-colored sugar. You'll get to **taste fresh coconut sugar** and buy some to take home. This authentic rural experience adds cultural depth beyond just the two markets and is unique to this tour itinerary." },
      { question: "How crowded are the markets?", answer: "Damnoen Saduak is busiest between **9:00-11:00 AM** when large tour groups arrive. Our early departure means you arrive around **8:00 AM** when it's quieter and more photogenic. Maeklong is less crowded overall but the area near the tracks gets packed 15 minutes before each train passes. Your guide knows the **best positions for photos** and navigates around the crowds expertly. Weekdays are generally less busy than weekends." },
      { question: "What should I wear?", answer: "Wear **lightweight, breathable clothing** and comfortable shoes that can handle wet surfaces. The floating market has splashing water and the boat can be slightly damp. A hat and sunscreen are essential — most of the markets are open-air. Avoid flip-flops as walking on railway tracks requires stable footwear. Bring a **small waterproof bag** or pouch for your phone during the boat ride." },
      { question: "Is this tour wheelchair accessible?", answer: "Unfortunately, **this tour is not wheelchair accessible**. The floating market requires boarding a longtail boat from a dock, and the Maeklong railway market involves walking along uneven train tracks. The markets have narrow passages and no designated accessible paths. Travelers with limited mobility should consider alternatives like the [Bangkok canal longtail boat cruise](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-longtail-boat-canal-cruise-hidden-temples) which has easier boarding and a more relaxed pace." },
      { question: "Can I book this as a private tour?", answer: "**Yes** — this tour is available as both a small group (max 12 people) and a private option. The private version allows flexible timing, extra stops, and personalized attention. Private tours cost approximately **30-40% more** but provide a significantly more intimate experience. Contact us via WhatsApp to arrange a private departure. The private version uses the same route and guide but at your own pace." },
      { question: "Is food included in the tour?", answer: "A **light breakfast snack** is typically provided during the drive, but the main appeal is buying fresh food at the markets yourself. The floating market has boat-cooked pad thai, grilled seafood, coconut ice cream, and tropical fruits at extremely low prices (**30-80 THB per dish**). At Maeklong you'll find dried seafood, Thai desserts, and fresh produce. Your guide recommends the best stalls — bring **300-500 THB** for food and snacks." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before departure for a full refund. Late cancellations are non-refundable as vehicle and boat are pre-booked. The tour operates rain or shine — markets function normally in light rain and the boat has a canopy. In case of severe weather warnings, we'll offer a free reschedule or full refund. Changes to your booking date are free with 48 hours' notice." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Soi Cowboy Nana Soi 11 Rooftops Clubs Go-Gos ---
  if (slug === 'soi-cowboy-nana-soi-11-rooftops-clubs-go-gos') {
    return [
      { question: "What does this nightlife tour include?", answer: "The tour covers Bangkok's **three main nightlife zones** — Soi Cowboy, Nana Plaza, and Soi 11 — plus a visit to a **rooftop bar** with panoramic city views. Your local guide navigates the scene, handles cover charges at select venues, and ensures you experience the best of each area safely. The tour typically includes **2-3 complimentary drinks** at partner venues. You'll visit go-go bars, rooftop cocktail lounges, live music venues, and club dance floors." },
      { question: "What time does the tour start and end?", answer: "The tour starts at **8:30-9:00 PM** when Bangkok's nightlife begins warming up, and ends around **1:00-2:00 AM**. You'll meet at a central BTS station (typically Asok or Nana). The timing covers the sweet spot of Bangkok's nightlife — rooftop bars at golden hour fading into the electric energy of the entertainment districts. After the tour ends, you're free to continue on your own or head back to your hotel." },
      { question: "Is this tour safe?", answer: "**Absolutely** — having a local guide is the smartest way to experience Bangkok nightlife. Your guide knows which venues are reputable, helps you avoid common tourist scams (inflated drink bills, aggressive touts), and ensures you're in safe areas at all times. The tour sticks to well-established, high-traffic entertainment districts. Bangkok's main nightlife areas are **heavily policed** and generally very safe for tourists who exercise basic common sense." },
      { question: "What's the dress code?", answer: "Most venues require **smart casual** — closed-toe shoes, long pants or smart jeans, and a collared shirt or neat top. Rooftop bars are the strictest — **no shorts, flip-flops, or tank tops**. Some clubs enforce a similar dress code. Your guide will confirm specific requirements before the tour. Ladies have more flexibility but avoid overly casual beachwear. Dressing well also gets you better treatment at VIP areas and rooftop venues." },
      { question: "Are drinks included in the tour price?", answer: "**2-3 welcome drinks** at partner venues are included. Beyond that, drinks are at your own expense. Budget approximately **200-500 THB per drink** at rooftop bars and **100-200 THB** at street-level bars and clubs. Some go-go bars require a minimum drink purchase — your guide will explain pricing upfront before entering any venue. Bring at least **2,000-3,000 THB** in cash for the evening for drinks and optional food." },
      { question: "Is this suitable for couples?", answer: "**Yes** — many couples take this tour to safely experience Bangkok's legendary nightlife scene. The tour is designed as a cultural experience, not a sleazy pub crawl. You'll see go-go bars as a cultural phenomenon (Thailand's unique entertainment culture) alongside upscale rooftop cocktail bars and live music venues. Couples particularly enjoy the rooftop bar segment. If you prefer food-focused evening entertainment, consider the [Chinatown food tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-authentic-tasting-thai-chinatown-walking-food-tour) instead." },
      { question: "What age restrictions apply?", answer: "All participants must be **at least 20 years old** — this is Thailand's legal drinking age and strictly enforced at entertainment venues. Bring your **passport or a photocopy** as ID may be checked at club entrances. There is no upper age limit — the tour attracts travelers from their 20s through their 60s. The guide adjusts the pace and venue selection based on the group's energy and preferences." },
      { question: "Which rooftop bar will we visit?", answer: "The specific rooftop bar varies by night and availability, but options include some of Bangkok's best — **Octave at the Marriott Sukhumvit**, **Above Eleven**, or **Zoom at the Anantara Sathorn**. All feature stunning **360-degree panoramic views** of Bangkok's glittering skyline. The rooftop segment is scheduled for early in the evening when the sunset views are most spectacular. Your guide chooses the venue with the best availability and atmosphere that night." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved?", answer: "Expect **moderate walking** — approximately 3-4 km over the evening across the three nightlife zones. The areas are relatively compact and connected by short BTS rides or tuk-tuk hops. You'll be on your feet inside venues too. Wear comfortable shoes — Bangkok's sidewalks can be uneven. The pace includes plenty of sitting time at bars and venues. If you have mobility concerns, let us know and the guide can arrange more taxi transfers between zones." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before the tour for a full refund. Late cancellations are non-refundable as venue reservations and guide time are pre-arranged. The tour runs every night regardless of weather since all venues are indoors or covered. Group size is kept to **4-8 people** for an intimate experience — book early for weekend nights (Friday and Saturday) as these fill up fastest." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Tingly Thai Cooking School Half-Day Cooking Class ---
  if (slug === 'tingly-thai-cooking-school-half-day-cooking-class') {
    return [
      { question: "How many dishes will I learn to cook?", answer: "You'll prepare **3-4 dishes** during the half-day session, typically a starter (like spring rolls or tom yum soup), a main course (pad thai or curry), a stir-fry, and a dessert (mango sticky rice). The chef demonstrates each technique before you replicate it at your **own individual cooking station**. You eat everything you make at the end — it's a generous meal. The Tingly school is known for its clear, patient instruction style." },
      { question: "What's the difference between morning and afternoon sessions?", answer: "The **morning session (9:00 AM - 1:00 PM)** includes a market visit where you shop for ingredients with the chef — highly recommended for food lovers and photographers. The **afternoon session (2:00 PM - 6:00 PM)** skips the market tour and goes straight to cooking. Both sessions teach the same number of dishes. Morning is more popular, so book early. The afternoon session works well if you've done a [morning floating market trip](/thailand/bangkok/floating-market-and-train-market-experience)." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup included?", answer: "**Yes** — free pickup and drop-off is included from central Bangkok hotels in the Sukhumvit, Silom, and Khao San Road areas. Pickup is approximately **30-45 minutes** before class start time. If your hotel is outside the standard zone, the school will suggest a nearby meeting point (usually a BTS station). Drop-off takes you back to the same pickup location after the class." },
      { question: "Can dietary restrictions be accommodated?", answer: "**Yes** — vegetarian, vegan, halal, and gluten-free diets are fully supported with **24 hours' advance notice**. The chef substitutes ingredients without compromising on authentic Thai flavor — tofu replaces meat, vegetable stock replaces fish sauce, and tamari replaces soy sauce for gluten-free. **Nut and shellfish allergies** require special attention as Thai cuisine uses both extensively. Communicate all restrictions clearly when booking." },
      { question: "How big are the class sizes?", answer: "Classes are limited to **8-14 participants** to ensure personal attention. Each person gets their own cooking station with wok, burner, utensils, and ingredients. The chef circulates throughout the class to help individuals. The small size means you can ask questions freely and get hands-on corrections to your technique. For an even more intimate experience, the [White Lotus cooking class](/thailand/bangkok/white-lotus-thai-cooking-class-with-market-tour) offers smaller groups of 6-12." },
      { question: "Do I need any cooking experience?", answer: "**None at all** — the class is designed for absolute beginners. The chef breaks down every dish into simple, repeatable steps. You'll learn fundamental Thai cooking techniques like balancing sweet-sour-salty-spicy flavors, proper wok handling, and curry paste preparation. Even experienced home cooks learn new techniques. The goal is that you can confidently recreate these dishes in your own kitchen back home." },
      { question: "What do I take home from the class?", answer: "Every participant receives a **recipe booklet** with all dishes from the session, including measurements adapted for Western kitchens and substitute ingredient suggestions. You also take home the satisfaction of a delicious meal you cooked yourself! Some sessions include a small bag of Thai spices or curry paste as a bonus takeaway. The recipes use common ingredients you can find in most international supermarkets." },
      { question: "Is this class good for couples?", answer: "**Extremely popular with couples** — cooking together is a fun, interactive date experience in Bangkok. You'll have side-by-side cooking stations and can compare your dishes. Many couples book this as a morning activity and follow it with an afternoon at a [rooftop bar or Mahanakhon SkyWalk](/thailand/bangkok/mahanakhon-skywalk-entry-ticket-with-options). The relaxed, social atmosphere means you'll also meet other travelers. Private couple sessions are available on request." },
      { question: "What if I'm a picky eater?", answer: "The chef is flexible with **spice levels and ingredients**. If you don't eat certain things, alternatives are available. Chili can be reduced or omitted, and protein choices can be adjusted. Thai cuisine is naturally diverse — even within the standard menu, there's something for everyone. The chef explains every ingredient before adding it, so you always know what's in your food. This class actually converts many picky eaters into Thai food lovers!" },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before the class. Late cancellations are non-refundable as fresh ingredients are purchased that morning. Date changes are free with **48 hours' notice** subject to availability. The class runs in all weather — the kitchen is fully covered. Maximum capacity is strictly enforced, so we recommend booking **2-3 days in advance** during peak season (November-February)." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Mahanakhon SkyWalk Entry Ticket with Options ---
  if (slug === 'mahanakhon-skywalk-entry-ticket-with-options') {
    return [
      { question: "What is the Mahanakhon SkyWalk?", answer: "The Mahanakhon SkyWalk is Bangkok's **highest observation deck** at **314 meters** atop the King Power Mahanakhon building — Thailand's tallest skyscraper. The highlight is the **glass floor tray** that extends from the rooftop, letting you stand on transparent glass and look straight down 78 floors to the street below. The 360-degree open-air rooftop offers unobstructed views of the entire Bangkok skyline, the Chao Phraya River, and on clear days, all the way to the Gulf of Thailand." },
      { question: "What ticket options are available?", answer: "There are typically **two main ticket options**: the standard SkyWalk ticket (rooftop observation deck + glass tray access), and the premium package that adds a **cocktail at the SkyBar** on the 76th floor. The standard ticket is the best value — the glass tray and rooftop views are the main attraction. The cocktail upgrade is worth it if you want a memorable drink with the view. Both options include the indoor observation deck on the 74th floor with interactive digital displays." },
      { question: "When is the best time to visit?", answer: "The **golden hour — 30-60 minutes before sunset** — is the most spectacular time. You'll see Bangkok transition from daylight to a glittering sea of lights. Sunset times vary by season: approximately **6:00 PM** (November-February) and **6:30 PM** (March-October). Book your ticket for **1 hour before sunset** to enjoy both daylight and nighttime views. Weekday evenings are less crowded than weekends. Combine it with a [river dinner cruise](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-night-river-experience-with-dinner-cultural-show) afterward." },
      { question: "Is the glass floor safe?", answer: "**Completely safe** — the glass tray is made of **multi-layered tempered glass** rated to hold far more weight than it will ever carry. It feels terrifying standing 314 meters above the street on transparent glass, but it's engineered to the highest safety standards. You must wear provided shoe covers to protect the glass surface. Bags and loose items must be secured. If you're afraid of heights, you can still enjoy the panoramic views from the solid-floor sections of the rooftop." },
      { question: "How long should I spend there?", answer: "Plan for **60-90 minutes** to fully enjoy the experience. This gives you time for the indoor observation deck (74th floor) with its digital screens, the outdoor rooftop (78th floor), photos on the glass tray, and simply absorbing the view. If you add the cocktail package, allow an extra **30-45 minutes** at the SkyBar. There's no time limit on your ticket — stay as long as you like." },
      { question: "How do I get there?", answer: "Mahanakhon is directly connected to **BTS Chong Nonsi station** — the entrance is literally at the base of the building when you exit the BTS. This makes it one of the most accessible attractions in Bangkok. No taxi or tuk-tuk needed if you're anywhere near the BTS line. The BTS runs until midnight, so evening visits are easy. From Khao San Road, take a taxi (**100-150 THB**) or grab a tuk-tuk." },
      { question: "Is there a dress code?", answer: "There's **no strict dress code** for the observation deck — casual wear is fine. However, if you upgrade to the SkyBar cocktail package, smart casual is recommended (no shorts or flip-flops). Shoe covers are provided and mandatory for the glass tray to protect the glass surface. Selfie sticks and tripods are **not allowed** on the rooftop for safety reasons, but phone photography is unlimited." },
      { question: "Can I bring children?", answer: "**Yes** — children of all ages are welcome and kids under 3 enter free. The glass tray is thrilling for older kids and safe for all ages. The indoor observation deck has interactive digital displays that children enjoy. However, the open-air rooftop has **strong winds** at 314 meters — hold onto young children and hats. For a child-friendly daytime activity, combine this with the [Ancient City and Erawan Museum](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-ancient-city-erawan-museum-tour)." },
      { question: "What if the weather is bad?", answer: "On **overcast or rainy days**, the rooftop may be temporarily closed for safety while the indoor 74th-floor observation deck remains open. Visibility is reduced but the city lights through clouds can be dramatic at night. If the rooftop is closed for your entire visit, a partial refund or reschedule is offered. Check the weather forecast before booking — the dry season (**November-February**) offers the clearest skies." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "Tickets are valid for your **selected date and time slot** only. **Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before your booked time. Late cancellations are non-refundable. If you can't make your exact time, contact us — we can often shift to a later slot on the same day. Tickets cannot be transferred to another date without cancellation and rebooking. We recommend booking the sunset slot **2-3 days in advance** as it's the most popular." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Backstreets Food Tour with 15+ Tastings ---
  if (slug === 'backstreets-food-tour-with-15-tastings') {
    return [
      { question: "How many food tastings are actually included?", answer: "You'll sample **15 or more distinct dishes** across multiple neighborhoods — this is not an exaggeration. The guide takes you to hidden stalls, family-run shops, and local markets that tourists never find on their own. Expect pad thai, grilled satay, som tum (papaya salad), boat noodles, roti, mango sticky rice, Thai iced tea, and specialty items unique to each neighborhood. Portions are tasting-sized so you can genuinely try everything without tapping out after dish five." },
      { question: "How long is the tour?", answer: "The tour runs approximately **4 hours** covering about **3-4 km of walking** through Bangkok's backstreets. The pace is relaxed — you're never rushed between stops. There's plenty of time to ask questions about each dish, take photos, and chat with vendors (through your guide). It's a walking tour, not a bus tour, so wear **comfortable shoes**. For a shorter evening food experience, consider the [tuk-tuk Chinatown night tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-tuk-tuk-chinatown-street-food-temple-night-tour)." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for vegetarians?", answer: "**Yes** — with advance notice, the guide swaps meat dishes for excellent vegetarian alternatives. Bangkok has abundant vegetarian street food including tofu dishes, vegetable curries, fruit desserts, and rice dishes. **Vegan** can also be accommodated. The guide communicates dietary needs to vendors in Thai to ensure no hidden fish sauce or shrimp paste. Specify your requirements when booking so the route can be adjusted." },
      { question: "What neighborhoods does the tour cover?", answer: "The exact route varies but typically includes **Old Town Bangkok, Chinatown (Yaowarat), and hidden residential neighborhoods** where locals eat — not tourist strips. You'll walk through narrow alleys, covered markets, and residential sois that don't appear in any guidebook. The guide selects vendors based on quality, not tourist convenience. Some stalls have been serving the same family recipe for **over 50 years**. The route is carefully designed to show you the real Bangkok food scene." },
      { question: "How does this compare to the Chinatown food tour?", answer: "This tour covers **more ground and more tastings** (15+ vs 10-12) across multiple neighborhoods, while the [Chinatown walking food tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-authentic-tasting-thai-chinatown-walking-food-tour) focuses specifically on Yaowarat's Chinese-Thai food scene in greater depth. Think of it this way: the backstreets tour is the wide-angle lens of Bangkok street food; the Chinatown tour is the zoom lens on one incredible neighborhood. Both are excellent — do both if you have time." },
      { question: "Are drinks included?", answer: "**Thai iced tea and water** are included throughout the tour. At some stops, you'll try unique local beverages like fresh coconut water, chrysanthemum tea, or longan juice — these are included as part of the tastings. Alcoholic drinks are not included but the guide can stop at a convenience store if you want to grab a beer. The tour keeps you well-hydrated — Thailand's heat and spicy food demand it." },
      { question: "What if I have food allergies?", answer: "**Inform us at booking** about all allergies, especially **shellfish, peanuts, and gluten** which are prevalent in Thai street food. The guide has detailed knowledge of ingredients at every stop and communicates in Thai with each vendor. For severe allergies, some stops may be observation-only while alternatives are provided. Thai cuisine can be tricky with hidden allergens (shrimp paste, fish sauce, peanuts), so honest and complete disclosure is essential for your safety." },
      { question: "Is this suitable for children?", answer: "Children **ages 6 and up** generally enjoy the tour — the food is fun and varied, and kids love trying new things in an adventurous setting. Younger children may find the 4-hour walking duration tiring. The guide can recommend milder dishes for kids who don't like spicy food. **Strollers are not practical** for the narrow backstreet alleys. Children eat at a reduced price. For a shorter family-friendly option, try the [Chinatown tuk-tuk night tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-tuk-tuk-chinatown-street-food-temple-night-tour)." },
      { question: "What should I bring?", answer: "Bring **comfortable walking shoes** (no heels or flip-flops), **cash for optional purchases** (200-300 THB), and **wet wipes or hand sanitizer** for eating at street stalls. A small **water bottle** is helpful though the guide provides drinks. Wear lightweight, breathable clothing — you'll be walking outdoors in Bangkok's heat. A portable fan or neck towel is a lifesaver during the hot season. Don't bring a huge backpack — a small crossbody bag is ideal." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before the tour. Late cancellations are non-refundable as food pre-orders are placed with vendors. Group size is **maximum 12 people** to maintain an intimate experience at small stalls. The tour runs in light rain — vendors operate rain or shine under covered stalls. In heavy downpour conditions, some route adjustments may be made but the tour proceeds. Book **2-3 days ahead** in peak season." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Thai Cooking Class in Sukhumvit / Market Tour - Mango ---
  if (slug === 'thai-cooking-class-in-sukhumvit-market-tour-mango') {
    return [
      { question: "Why is this called the 'Mango' cooking class?", answer: "The **Mango Thai Cooking Class** is the school's brand name, known for its vibrant, fun approach to Thai cuisine. Mango sticky rice (Khao Niaow Ma Muang) is their signature dessert dish and a guaranteed part of every session. The name reflects their philosophy — making Thai cooking **sweet, approachable, and memorable**. The school is one of Bangkok's highest-rated cooking experiences and has taught thousands of international visitors." },
      { question: "What dishes will I cook?", answer: "You'll prepare **4-5 dishes** from a selection that typically includes **Pad Thai, Green Curry, Tom Yum Goong, Spring Rolls, and Mango Sticky Rice**. Some sessions let you choose your menu from a wider list. Every dish is made completely from scratch — including pounding your own curry paste in a mortar and pestle. The class is comprehensive enough that you leave with genuine Thai cooking skills, not just tourist photos. Compare with the [Tingly Thai half-day class](/thailand/bangkok/tingly-thai-cooking-school-half-day-cooking-class) for a slightly different format." },
      { question: "Where exactly in Sukhumvit is the school?", answer: "The Mango school is located in the **Sukhumvit area**, Bangkok's most convenient neighborhood for tourists. It's within walking distance of a BTS station, making it easily accessible from anywhere on the Skytrain line. The exact address and Google Maps pin are provided after booking confirmation. The Sukhumvit location means you can combine the class with shopping at Terminal 21 or afternoon drinks at a nearby rooftop bar like [Mahanakhon SkyWalk](/thailand/bangkok/mahanakhon-skywalk-entry-ticket-with-options)." },
      { question: "Does the class include a market tour?", answer: "**Yes** — the morning session begins with a guided tour of a **local Thai fresh market** where you learn about key ingredients: lemongrass, galangal, kaffir lime, chili varieties, palm sugar, and Thai basil. Your chef explains how to select fresh produce and identify quality ingredients. The market tour lasts **30-40 minutes** and is included in the price. The afternoon session skips the market and starts directly with cooking." },
      { question: "How large are the class sizes?", answer: "Classes have a maximum of **10-14 participants**, each with their own individual cooking station. The intimate size ensures the chef can give personal attention, correct your technique, and answer questions. It's social enough to meet other travelers but small enough that you don't feel lost in a crowd. Private classes for couples, families, or groups can be arranged — contact us via WhatsApp for pricing." },
      { question: "Can dietary restrictions be accommodated?", answer: "**Absolutely** — the school handles vegetarian, vegan, gluten-free, halal, and nut-free diets regularly. **Notify us at least 24 hours before** your session so the correct ingredients are prepared. Vegetarian versions use tofu and mushrooms; vegan omits all animal products; gluten-free uses tamari and rice-based ingredients. The chef is experienced in adapting recipes while maintaining authentic flavor. This is one of the most allergy-friendly cooking schools in Bangkok." },
      { question: "Is hotel transfer included?", answer: "**Pickup is included** from hotels in central Bangkok (Sukhumvit, Silom, Sathorn, Khao San areas). The van collects you approximately **30-45 minutes** before class time. Drop-off is also included after the class ends. For hotels outside the standard pickup zone, a convenient BTS meeting point will be suggested. The Sukhumvit location means most hotels are within a short transfer distance." },
      { question: "What do I take home?", answer: "Every participant receives a **printed recipe booklet** with all dishes taught, complete with ingredient lists, measurements, and the chef's pro tips. The booklet includes **Western supermarket substitutes** for Thai ingredients that may be hard to find at home. You'll also leave with the muscle memory of proper wok technique, curry paste preparation, and Thai flavor balancing. Some sessions include a small gift of Thai spices." },
      { question: "Is this suitable for children?", answer: "Children **ages 7 and up** can fully participate in the cooking with supervision. The chef makes the class fun and engaging for younger participants. Wok cooking involves high heat, so adult supervision is required. Kids especially love making spring rolls and mango sticky rice. Younger children can watch and taste. This is a more hands-on alternative to a [Bangkok food walking tour](/thailand/bangkok/backstreets-food-tour-with-15-tastings) for families." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before the class. Cancellations within 24 hours are non-refundable as ingredients are freshly purchased each morning. Date changes are free with **48 hours' notice** subject to availability. The class runs rain or shine in a fully covered kitchen. Peak season (**November-February**) fills up fast — book at least 3 days ahead. Maximum capacity is strictly maintained for quality." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Bangkok Authentic Tasting Thai-Chinatown Walking Food Tour ---
  if (slug === 'bangkok-authentic-tasting-thai-chinatown-walking-food-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "How is this different from the tuk-tuk Chinatown night tour?", answer: "This is a **daytime walking-focused food tour** that goes deeper into Chinatown's hidden alleys and family-run stalls, with **10-12 tastings** and more cultural context. The [tuk-tuk Chinatown night tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-tuk-tuk-chinatown-street-food-temple-night-tour) is an evening experience combining food with tuk-tuk rides and temple visits in a more high-energy, photogenic atmosphere. This walking tour is better for serious food lovers; the night tour is better for first-time visitors wanting the full Bangkok sensory experience. Ideally, do both!" },
      { question: "What food will I try?", answer: "Expect **10-12 dishes** including Chinatown specialties you won't find elsewhere in Bangkok: Chinese-style roasted duck, dim sum, hand-pulled noodles, shark fin soup alternatives, fish maw soup, Chinese pastries, grilled seafood, mango sticky rice, and Thai-Chinese fusion dishes. The guide focuses on stalls that have been in the same family for **decades** — some for three generations. Each stop tells a story about Bangkok's Chinese immigrant heritage and how it shaped Thai cuisine." },
      { question: "What time does the tour run?", answer: "The walking tour runs during **late morning to early afternoon** (typically 10:00 AM - 2:00 PM) when Chinatown's day markets and lunch stalls are at their peak. This timing avoids the extreme midday heat while catching the best food stalls. Some are open only during lunch hours. For evening Chinatown food experiences, the [tuk-tuk night food tour](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-tuk-tuk-chinatown-street-food-temple-night-tour) is the better option." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved?", answer: "The tour covers approximately **3-4 km** over 3-4 hours through Chinatown's streets, alleys, and covered markets. The pace is leisurely with frequent stops for food and explanations. You'll walk through narrow **soi (lanes)** and market corridors — some are tight and bustling. Wear comfortable, closed-toe shoes as the ground can be wet and uneven. The tour is manageable for most fitness levels but not suitable for wheelchairs or strollers." },
      { question: "Is this suitable for vegetarians?", answer: "**With advance notice**, vegetarian alternatives are available at most stops. Chinatown has Buddhist vegetarian restaurants (look for the yellow flag) and many tofu and vegetable dishes. However, Chinatown's signature dishes are heavily meat and seafood-focused — vegetarians will miss some iconic tastings. **Vegan** is more challenging but possible. For a food tour with better vegetarian options, consider the [backstreets food tour with 15 tastings](/thailand/bangkok/backstreets-food-tour-with-15-tastings) which covers diverse neighborhoods." },
      { question: "Is this tour good for solo travelers?", answer: "**Excellent for solo travelers** — the small group format (maximum 10-12 people) means you'll bond with other food-loving travelers over shared dishes. Many solo travelers join this tour and make friends by the second stop. The guide facilitates introductions and the communal nature of Thai eating makes it naturally social. Solo travelers also benefit from the guide's navigation — Chinatown's labyrinthine alleys are easy to get lost in alone." },
      { question: "What should I bring?", answer: "Bring **comfortable walking shoes**, a **small water bottle**, **wet wipes** for street food eating, and **300-500 THB cash** for any extra purchases or drinks beyond the included tastings. A hat and sunscreen are essential if the tour runs during sunny hours. Keep your bag small and secure — Chinatown is crowded and a crossbody bag works best. Camera or phone for photos — Chinatown is incredibly photogenic with its neon signs and bustling stalls." },
      { question: "Are food allergies accommodated?", answer: "**Inform us at booking** about all allergies. The guide speaks Thai and communicates directly with vendors about ingredients. **Shellfish and nut allergies** are the most critical in Chinatown as many dishes use shrimp paste, oyster sauce, and peanuts. For severe allergies, some stops may be observation-only with safe alternatives at other stalls. The guide carries a card with allergy information in Thai and Chinese to show vendors." },
      { question: "Will I need lunch after the tour?", answer: "**No** — the 10-12 tastings add up to a very full meal. Most people are completely satisfied (or overstuffed) by the end. Portions are designed so you can try everything without getting too full too early. Come with an **empty stomach** — skip breakfast or eat very lightly. If you're still hungry after 12 tastings, the guide can point you to their personal favorite Chinatown restaurants for a sit-down meal on your own." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before the tour for a full refund. Late cancellations are non-refundable. The tour runs in light rain — most stalls are covered and Chinatown has plenty of sheltered walking areas. Maximum group size is **10-12 people** to keep the experience intimate at small stalls. Book **2-3 days ahead** during peak season (November-March) as this is one of Bangkok's most popular food tours." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Khao Yai National Park Small-Group Day Trip ---
  if (slug === 'khao-yai-national-park-small-group-day-trip') {
    return [
      { question: "How far is Khao Yai from Bangkok?", answer: "Khao Yai National Park is approximately **180 km northeast of Bangkok** — about a **2.5-3 hour drive** each way. The tour includes comfortable van transport with hotel pickup and drop-off. The drive passes through scenic Thai countryside. The total day trip runs approximately **12-14 hours** door to door, making it a long but rewarding day. If you prefer a closer day trip, the [floating market and railway market tour](/thailand/bangkok/floating-market-and-train-market-experience) is only 90 minutes from Bangkok." },
      { question: "What wildlife might I see?", answer: "Khao Yai is home to **wild elephants, gibbons, hornbills, macaques, deer, and over 300 bird species**. Wildlife sightings are never guaranteed in a national park, but your experienced guide knows the best trails and timing. **Wild elephants** are commonly spotted near salt licks, especially during the rainy season (June-October). Hornbills are nearly guaranteed. The park is also home to Asian black bears and clouded leopards, though these are rarely seen. Binoculars are recommended." },
      { question: "What waterfalls will we visit?", answer: "The tour typically visits **Haew Narok waterfall** (the park's tallest at 150 meters — and yes, the name means 'Hell's Gorge') and **Haew Suwat waterfall**, which gained fame from the movie 'The Beach' with Leonardo DiCaprio. Both are spectacular, especially during and after the rainy season (July-November) when water flow is strongest. During the dry season (February-April), some waterfalls may have reduced flow. The guide adjusts the route based on current conditions." },
      { question: "How difficult are the hiking trails?", answer: "The trails visited on this day trip are **easy to moderate** — well-maintained paths through tropical forest with some gentle inclines. Total hiking distance is approximately **4-6 km** spread across multiple short trails. No technical climbing or scrambling is required. Sturdy shoes with grip are essential as trails can be **muddy and slippery**, especially during rainy season. The guide sets a comfortable pace suitable for average fitness levels." },
      { question: "What should I bring?", answer: "Essential items: **sturdy walking shoes** (hiking boots ideal, not sandals), **rain jacket** (weather changes quickly in the mountains), **mosquito repellent** (DEET-based recommended), **sunscreen**, a **refillable water bottle**, and **a light long-sleeve layer**. Khao Yai is at **elevation** so it's cooler than Bangkok by 5-8°C. Bring your camera — the scenery is stunning. A small daypack for your belongings on the trails is useful. Leeches can be present during rainy season — tuck pants into socks." },
      { question: "Is lunch included?", answer: "**Yes** — a Thai lunch at a local restaurant near the park is included. The restaurant serves authentic Thai dishes — not tourist food — and the meal is generous. Vegetarian options are available with advance notice. Bring extra cash (**200-300 THB**) for drinks, snacks, and convenience store stops during the drive. The guide usually stops at a roadside fruit stall on the way home for fresh tropical fruits." },
      { question: "What's the group size?", answer: "Groups are kept **small — maximum 10-12 people** — to minimize impact on the park environment and maximize wildlife spotting chances. Large groups scare wildlife away, so small groups have better chances of encounters. The guide can give personal attention and adjust the pace to the group's fitness level. For a completely private experience, contact us for custom pricing. The small van is comfortable for the 3-hour drive each way." },
      { question: "Is this suitable for children?", answer: "Children **ages 6 and up** enjoy this trip — the wildlife, waterfalls, and jungle setting are exciting for kids. The hiking trails are manageable for active children. However, the **14-hour total day length** (including 6 hours of driving) can be exhausting for younger children. Bring snacks and entertainment for the van. For a shorter nature day trip from Bangkok, the [ethical elephant sanctuary](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-ethical-elephant-sanctuary-day-trip) is a half-day alternative that kids love." },
      { question: "When is the best season to visit Khao Yai?", answer: "Each season offers different experiences: **Dry season (November-February)** has the best weather, clearest trails, and most comfortable temperatures. **Rainy season (June-October)** has the most dramatic waterfalls, lush green forest, and the highest chance of wild elephant sightings — but trails are muddy and leeches are active. **Hot season (March-May)** is the quietest but hottest. For a first visit, **November-January** is ideal — cool, dry, and green from the just-ended rains." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 48 hours** before departure (longer notice required than city tours due to van and park permit pre-booking). Cancellations within 48 hours receive a 50% refund. The tour operates in light rain — the guide brings ponchos and adjusts the route to safer trails. Only severe weather (tropical storm warning) cancels the trip, in which case a full refund or reschedule is offered. Book early in peak season as this tour fills up fast." },
    ];
  }

  // --- Bangkok Night River Experience with Dinner & Cultural Show ---
  if (slug === 'bangkok-night-river-experience-with-dinner-cultural-show') {
    return [
      { question: "What does the dinner cruise include?", answer: "The cruise includes a **full Thai dinner buffet** with live cooking stations, traditional Thai cultural performances, and a **2-hour cruise** along the Chao Phraya River passing Bangkok's illuminated landmarks — Wat Arun, the Grand Palace, Rama VIII Bridge, and the riverside temples. Drinks are typically included (a welcome cocktail plus soft drinks) with premium alcoholic beverages available for purchase. Live traditional music accompanies the dinner service throughout the evening." },
      { question: "What kind of cultural show is performed?", answer: "The show features **traditional Thai classical dance** (Khon and Ram Thai) in elaborate costumes with gold headdresses, live traditional Thai music, and sometimes a Muay Thai demonstration. The performances tell stories from Thai mythology and the Ramakien (Thai version of the Ramayana). Shows run approximately **45-60 minutes** in segments between dinner courses. It's a beautiful introduction to Thai performing arts — far more authentic than hotel lobby shows." },
      { question: "What time does the cruise depart?", answer: "The cruise typically departs at **7:00-7:30 PM** and returns by **9:00-9:30 PM**. Check-in and boarding begins **30 minutes before departure** at the designated pier. The timing is perfect — you'll see Bangkok's riverside landmarks transition from sunset glow to full nighttime illumination during the cruise. For a daytime river experience, combine this with a morning [longtail boat canal cruise](/thailand/bangkok/bangkok-longtail-boat-canal-cruise-hidden-temples) for a completely different perspective of Bangkok's waterways." },
      { question: "Which pier does the cruise depart from?", answer: "The departure pier is typically at **Asiatique The Riverfront** or **River City Pier**, both easily accessible by BTS + ferry or taxi. The exact pier is confirmed after booking. Both piers have waiting areas, restrooms, and nearby restaurants if you arrive early. From Sukhumvit, take the BTS to Saphan Taksin and transfer to the free Asiatique shuttle boat. From Khao San Road, a taxi takes **20-30 minutes**." },
      { question: "What's the dress code?", answer: "**Smart casual** is recommended — collared shirt or blouse, long pants or dress, and closed shoes. Most cruises don't allow shorts, flip-flops, or sleeveless tops for men. Women have more flexibility but beachwear is inappropriate. You're dining on a premium vessel with entertainment — think 'nice dinner out' attire. The boat is **air-conditioned inside** with an open-air upper deck, so bring a light jacket for the breeze on the river." },
      { question: "Is the food quality good?", answer: "The dinner buffet offers **above-average quality** for a cruise experience — fresh seafood (prawns, fish, squid), Thai curries, stir-fries, noodle stations, tropical fruit, and Thai desserts. It's not Michelin-star dining, but it's genuinely tasty Thai food with variety. Live cooking stations ensure hot, fresh dishes throughout the cruise. The river views and cultural show elevate the experience far beyond just the food. If you're primarily a foodie, supplement with a [daytime street food tour](/thailand/bangkok/backstreets-food-tour-with-15-tastings) for authentic local flavors." },
      { question: "Can dietary restrictions be accommodated?", answer: "**Yes** — the buffet format naturally offers options for most diets. Vegetarian, halal, and seafood-free dishes are typically available. **Vegan and severe allergy requirements** should be communicated at least **48 hours before** so the kitchen can prepare specific options. The buffet labels major allergens. For very strict dietary needs, eat beforehand and enjoy the cruise for the views and show — the experience is worth it regardless of the food." },
      { question: "Is this suitable for children?", answer: "**Yes** — children enjoy the boat ride, colorful cultural performances, and the excitement of dining on the river with Bangkok glowing around them. Children under 3 typically ride free; ages 3-11 receive a reduced rate. The performances are visually stunning and hold kids' attention. The boat has **safety railings** on all decks. For families, this is one of Bangkok's most memorable evening activities alongside the [Muay Thai boxing experience](/thailand/bangkok/muay-thai-boxing-tickets-at-rajadamnern-stadium)." },
      { question: "What if it rains?", answer: "The cruise operates **rain or shine** — the dining area and performance stage are under a covered deck. Only the upper observation deck is open-air. Light rain actually adds atmosphere with the city lights reflecting off the water. The cruise only cancels in **extreme weather conditions** (very rare), in which case a full refund or reschedule is offered. The dry season (November-February) has the lowest chance of rain and the most comfortable evening temperatures." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "**Free cancellation up to 24 hours** before the cruise departure time. Late cancellations are non-refundable as your seat and dinner are pre-reserved. Date changes are possible with **48 hours' notice** subject to availability. The cruise runs nightly except during special maintenance periods (extremely rare). Book **3-5 days ahead** during peak season (December-January) as the sunset and dinner cruises are among Bangkok's most popular evening activities." },
    ];
  }

