  // --- JAIPUR: jaipur-city-night-tour-with-guide (auto-added 2026-07-02) ---
  if (slug === 'jaipur-city-night-tour-with-guide') {
    return [
      { question: "What time does the Jaipur Night City Tour start and how long does it last?", answer: "The tour begins at **7:30 PM** and runs for a full **4 hours**, wrapping up around 11:30 PM. This timing is deliberate: it lets you catch Jaipur's monuments once they're lit up after dark, when Hawa Mahal, Patrika Gate and the Rajasthan Legislative Assembly glow against the night sky. It's an unhurried evening with time for photos at each stop." },
      { question: "What exactly is included in the price?", answer: "Your booking covers a **private air-conditioned car for the entire 4 hours**, a professional local guide, and hotel pickup and drop-off within Jaipur. Fuel, parking, tolls and complimentary water bottles are all included too, so there are no surprise add-ons. The standard **Jaipur City Night Tour with Guide is $79**, and you're never sharing the car with strangers." },
      { question: "Does the tour include entry tickets to the monuments?", answer: "This is primarily an **illuminated photo-stop and drive-past tour**, so it doesn't include paid interior entry tickets. In the evening most sites like Amber Fort and Hawa Mahal are best admired lit up from outside, which is exactly what this tour is designed for. If you'd like to actually go inside the forts and palaces during opening hours, our [Private Full Day Sightseeing Tour By Car with Guide](/india/jaipur/jaipur-full-day-sightseeing-tour-by-car) covers the interiors properly." },
      { question: "Which landmarks will I see on this night tour?", answer: "You'll take in the **Albert Hall Museum, Hawa Mahal (Palace of Winds), Jal Mahal floating on the lake, Amber Fort, and Patrika Gate**, plus a scenic drive past the illuminated Rajasthan Legislative Assembly. Each stop is chosen for how photogenic it looks after dark. For the same icons explored in daylight with time to walk around, see our [City Highlights Tour with Amber Fort & Hawa Mahal](/india/jaipur/jaipur-city-highlights-tour-with-amber-fort-hawa-mahal)." },
      { question: "Is there a bike version of this tour?", answer: "Yes. Alongside the standard car tour, we offer the **Jaipur Night Tour On a Bike – Feel like a local at $90** for the same 4-hour duration. It's a more open-air, adventurous way to weave through the Pink City's evening streets. The car option is the better pick if you're travelling with young kids, older relatives, or simply want climate-controlled comfort between stops." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup really included, and how does it work?", answer: "Yes, **complimentary pickup and drop-off is included anywhere within Jaipur city limits**. After you book, share your hotel name and we'll confirm the pickup point; your guide and driver will meet you in the lobby at 7:30 PM. If you're staying somewhat outside the main city, just let us know in advance so we can confirm the location works." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children and elderly travelers?", answer: "Absolutely. Because most of the experience happens from the comfort of a private air-conditioned car with short photo stops, there's very little walking involved, making it **ideal for families with kids and older travelers**. There's no steep climbing at night, unlike a daytime Amber Fort visit where you'd tackle the ramp up to the fort. Everyone can enjoy the lights at a relaxed pace." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved?", answer: "Very little. This is a **car-based tour with brief photo stops**, so you'll only be on your feet for a few minutes at each spot, such as at Patrika Gate or in front of Hawa Mahal. If you're after a more active, on-foot experience of the old city, our [Heritage Walk & Street Food Tasting of Jaipur Pink City](/india/jaipur/jaipur-heritage-walk-street-food-tour) is a great daytime complement." },
      { question: "What should I wear for the evening tour?", answer: "Jaipur evenings are far more comfortable than the daytime heat, but it's still worth dressing lightly in breathable cotton. In winter months (December to February) nights can get genuinely chilly, so **bring a light jacket or shawl**. Comfortable shoes are enough since you won't be doing serious walking, and modest dress is appreciated when stopping near any religious or heritage sites." },
      { question: "Is it safe to tour Jaipur at night?", answer: "Yes. You'll be with a **professional local guide and private driver the whole time**, travelling in a well-lit car between well-visited landmarks. Jaipur's main monument areas are busy and welcoming in the evening. Your guide handles all navigation, parking and logistics, so you can simply relax and take in the illuminated Pink City." },
      { question: "When is the best season to do this night tour?", answer: "The tour runs year-round, but **October to March offers the most pleasant evenings** with cool, clear air perfect for photography and being outdoors at each stop. Monsoon months (July to September) can bring occasional showers but the lights reflecting off wet streets look wonderful. Summer nights are warm yet still far kinder than the fierce daytime sun." },
      { question: "How good is this tour for photography?", answer: "Excellent, it's built around it. **Patrika Gate is one of Jaipur's most photogenic spots**, and Hawa Mahal, Jal Mahal and Amber Fort all take on a completely different, dramatic character under night lighting. Your guide knows the best angles and vantage points at each stop, and the unhurried 4-hour pace means you're never rushed to get the shot you want." },
      { question: "Is this a private tour or will I share the car with others?", answer: "It's **fully private**. The air-conditioned car is exclusively for you and your group, and the guide's attention is all yours throughout the evening. That means you set the pace, can linger longer at your favourite spots, and enjoy a personalised experience. If you'd like a private guide for daytime exploring too, consider our [Explore With Official Tour Guide](/india/jaipur/jaipur-city-tour-with-official-guide) option." },
      { question: "Can I combine this with daytime sightseeing?", answer: "Definitely, and many travelers do exactly that. Spend the day going inside the forts and palaces, then let this night tour show you the same skyline transformed by lights. Our [Private Full Day Sightseeing with Local Expert Guide](/india/jaipur/jaipur-private-full-day-sightseeing-tour) pairs perfectly with an evening night tour to give you a complete two-part picture of the Pink City." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "You can **cancel free of charge up to 24 hours before** your 7:30 PM start time for a full refund. Cancellations made within 24 hours are generally non-refundable, as the guide and private car are reserved specifically for you. If your plans are uncertain, we recommend booking flexibly and contacting us as early as possible for any changes." },
      { question: "How do I book and confirm my night tour?", answer: "Simply select your preferred option (the **$79 car tour** or the **$90 bike experience**) and complete your booking online. You'll receive a confirmation, and we'll follow up to arrange your exact hotel pickup details. Booking ahead is wise during peak season (October to March), when evening slots fill quickly." },
    ];
  }

  // --- JAIPUR: hawa-mahal-landmarks-guided-tour (auto-added 2026-07-02) ---
  if (slug === 'hawa-mahal-landmarks-guided-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "Which landmarks does this 6-hour tour actually cover?", answer: "This tour focuses on three of Jaipur's most iconic sites: the grand **City Palace** with its royal courtyards and blend of Rajasthani and Mughal architecture, the lattice-façade **Hawa Mahal** (the famous 'Palace of Winds'), and the UNESCO-listed **Jantar Mantar** astronomical observatory. The pace is relaxed enough to explore each properly with your guide over roughly six hours. If you'd rather add Amber Fort and more sites, consider the [Jaipur City Highlights Tour with Amber Fort & Hawa Mahal](/india/jaipur/jaipur-city-highlights-tour-with-amber-fort-hawa-mahal)." },
      { question: "Are the monument entry tickets included in the price?", answer: "Yes. The **$201 Iconic Landmarks Tour** includes entry tickets for City Palace, Hawa Mahal and Jantar Mantar, so there are no surprise queues at the ticket counters. It also covers your private AC car, driver, a government-approved guide, all toll taxes and parking, plus a mineral water bottle. You won't need to pay anything extra on the ground for the covered monuments." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup and drop-off provided?", answer: "Yes, **hotel or airport pick-up and drop-off are included** in the price. Your driver will collect you directly from your accommodation in Jaipur at the agreed time, so there's no need to arrange separate transport or meet at a central point. Just share your hotel details when you book and confirm your preferred start time." },
      { question: "Is this a private tour or will I share the car with strangers?", answer: "This is a fully **private tour** — the air-conditioned car, driver and guide are yours alone for the day. You set the pace, linger longer at Jantar Mantar's sundials if you like, or spend extra time photographing Hawa Mahal. If you're travelling as a bigger group and want a longer itinerary, the [Private Full Day Sightseeing Tour By Car with Guide](/india/jaipur/jaipur-full-day-sightseeing-tour-by-car) is a natural next step." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved, and is it suitable for older travellers?", answer: "The walking is moderate and manageable for most fitness levels. City Palace and Jantar Mantar involve strolling around courtyards and open grounds, while Hawa Mahal is largely enjoyed from the street-facing plaza for photos. There are some stairs at City Palace, but nothing like the steep ramp climb at Amber Fort. With the private car ferrying you between sites, it suits **older travellers and families** well." },
      { question: "What is the best time of day to start this tour?", answer: "An early start, around **8:30–9:00 am**, is ideal. You'll photograph Hawa Mahal in the soft morning light when its pink sandstone glows and the street is quieter, and you'll cover the outdoor sections of Jantar Mantar before the midday sun. Because it's a private tour, your start time is flexible — just confirm it when booking." },
      { question: "How hot does Jaipur get, and what should I wear?", answer: "Jaipur can be genuinely hot from April to June, often exceeding 40°C, so light, breathable cotton clothing, a hat and sunglasses are strongly recommended. Your car is air-conditioned and a **mineral water bottle is included**, but bring extra water in summer. October to March is far more comfortable for sightseeing. Modest dress that covers shoulders and knees is respectful at the palace sites." },
      { question: "Can I take photos at all three monuments?", answer: "Absolutely — this is one of the most photogenic routes in Jaipur. **Hawa Mahal's** honeycomb façade is the classic postcard shot, best from the café rooftops opposite, while City Palace offers colourful gates and courtyards and Jantar Mantar's giant stone instruments are wonderfully geometric. Some interior sections at City Palace may carry a small camera fee or restrictions, which your guide will point out." },
      { question: "Is the tour good for kids?", answer: "Yes, it works well for families. The private AC car keeps children cool and comfortable between stops, and Jantar Mantar in particular fascinates kids with its enormous sundials and astronomy instruments. The guide can pitch the storytelling to keep younger travellers engaged. If your children love animals too, you might pair it another day with the [Jaipur Elephant Village Tour](/india/jaipur/elephant-village-tour-jaipur)." },
      { question: "Will the guide speak English, and are they qualified?", answer: "Yes. Your guide is a **government-approved, expert local guide** who brings Jaipur's royal history and architecture to life in fluent English. They tailor the commentary to your interests, whether that's the Mughal design details at City Palace or the science behind Jantar Mantar's instruments. If you want a guide-only booking without the car and monuments, the [official Jaipur city guide option](/india/jaipur/jaipur-city-tour-with-official-guide) is a good alternative." },
      { question: "Does the tour include lunch or meals?", answer: "Meals are not included beyond the complimentary mineral water bottle, leaving you free to eat wherever you like. Your guide and driver can recommend an authentic Rajasthani restaurant along the way and wait while you dine. If food is a priority, many travellers combine sightseeing with the [Heritage Walk & Street Food Tasting](/india/jaipur/jaipur-heritage-walk-street-food-tour) on a separate day." },
      { question: "Can I add extra stops like Amber Fort or Jal Mahal?", answer: "Because the car and guide are private, you can often add a photo stop at spots like **Jal Mahal** (the water palace) or a drive-past of Albert Hall, subject to the six-hour window. If you specifically want Amber Fort's ramparts, Nahargarh Fort and a fuller day, we'd recommend the [full-day sightseeing tour by car](/india/jaipur/jaipur-private-full-day-sightseeing-tour) instead, which is built for that scope." },
      { question: "How long do we spend at each site?", answer: "The six hours are split comfortably across the three landmarks plus travel time between them, which is short as they're all in the old walled city. Expect roughly the most time inside **City Palace** given its museum and courtyards, a focused photo-and-history stop at Hawa Mahal, and an unhurried exploration of Jantar Mantar. Your guide adjusts timings to your interests." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "You can typically cancel free of charge up to 24 hours before the tour's start time for a full refund; cancellations inside 24 hours are generally non-refundable. Because this is a private tour with a dedicated car and government-approved guide reserved for you, we appreciate as much notice as possible. Check the exact terms shown at checkout before you confirm." },
      { question: "How do I book and confirm my tour?", answer: "Simply select your date and the **$201 Iconic Landmarks Tour** option and complete your booking online. Share your hotel name and preferred pickup time, and you'll receive a confirmation with your guide and driver details. We arrange everything in advance, so all you need to do on the day is be ready for pickup." },
      { question: "Is this tour a good introduction to Jaipur if it's my first visit?", answer: "Yes — it's one of the best first-day itineraries in the Pink City, hitting three unmissable landmarks with an expert guide and zero logistical hassle. It gives you a strong overview of Jaipur's royal heritage and architecture. Many first-timers follow it with a shopping day at Johari and Bapu Bazaar via the [Jaipur Craft & Shopping Experience](/india/jaipur/jaipur-shopping-tour) to round out their trip." },
    ];
  }


  // --- REMAINING: khajuraho-group-of-monuments-guided-tour (auto-added 2026-07-02) ---
  if (slug === 'khajuraho-group-of-monuments-guided-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What exactly is included in the two booking options, and which one has the car?", answer: "There are two options. The **\"Book Official Tour Guide for Khajuraho Tour\" at $40** covers a licensed, government-approved guide for the full 4 hours only, with no vehicle. The **\"Half Day Khajuraho Tour With Tour Guide & Car\" at $100** adds a private air-conditioned car with hotel pick-up and drop-off. Choose the $100 option if you'd rather not arrange your own auto-rickshaw between the temple groups." },
      { question: "Are the temple entry tickets included in the price?", answer: "No. **Both options cover the guide (and car, on the $100 option) but not the ASI entrance ticket to the Western Group of Monuments.** The Archaeological Survey of India ticket is paid separately at the gate and is very inexpensive for international visitors. Your guide will point you to the correct counter so you don't queue in the wrong line." },
      { question: "Where does the guide meet me if I book the guide-only option?", answer: "For the $40 guide-only option, **your guide meets you at the entry gate of the Western Group of Temples, or along the way if you're arriving from a hotel nearby.** Khajuraho is small and walkable, so most hotels are only a few minutes from the temple complex. If you book the $100 car option instead, the guide and driver collect you directly from your hotel lobby." },
      { question: "How long do we spend at the Western Group and Kandariya Mahadev Temple?", answer: "The tour is 4 hours in total, with **roughly 2 hours dedicated to the Western Group**, the most famous and best-preserved section. You'll spend meaningful time at the towering **Kandariya Mahadev Temple dedicated to Lord Shiva**, admiring its spire and the intricate carvings, before moving through the surrounding shrines. The pace is unhurried so you can actually absorb the sculpture detail." },
      { question: "Will the guide explain the famous erotic carvings, and is the tour family-appropriate?", answer: "Yes. The Khajuraho sculptures are world-renowned, and a good guide places the erotic panels in their proper Chandela-era cultural and spiritual context rather than sensationalising them. **The tour is suitable for families**, and guides are experienced at reading the group and keeping explanations tasteful. If you're travelling with children, just let your guide know your preference at the start." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved and are there steps to climb?", answer: "Expect a fair amount of walking across the landscaped temple grounds, plus **steep stone steps up to the raised platforms of the main temples like Kandariya Mahadev**. The surfaces are uneven old stone in places. It's manageable for most reasonably mobile travellers, but anyone with knee or mobility issues should mention it so the guide can pace things and skip the highest climbs where needed." },
      { question: "What's the best time of day to take this tour?", answer: "**Early morning is ideal**, when the light is soft on the sandstone, the crowds are thinner, and temperatures are lowest. Late afternoon is a good second choice for warm golden light on the carvings. Because the tour lasts 4 hours, starting early also means you finish before the midday heat peaks, which matters greatly in the warmer months." },
      { question: "When is the best season to visit Khajuraho weather-wise?", answer: "**October to March is by far the most comfortable window**, with pleasant days and cool mornings. Summers from April to June are very hot and dry in this part of Madhya Pradesh, so an early start and plenty of water are essential. If you're visiting in late February, your trip may coincide with the famous Khajuraho Dance Festival held against the temple backdrop." },
      { question: "What should I wear and bring for the tour?", answer: "Wear **comfortable closed walking shoes for the uneven stone and steps**, light breathable clothing, and modest attire that's respectful for a religious heritage site. Bring a hat, sunscreen, sunglasses and a water bottle, as much of the site is open with little shade. In cooler months a light layer for the early morning is worth packing." },
      { question: "Is this a private tour or will I share it with strangers?", answer: "**This is a private tour** dedicated to your own party, so the guide's attention and pace are entirely yours. That means you can ask as many questions as you like, linger at the temples that fascinate you, and move on quickly from ones that don't. It's a far more personal experience than joining a large group at the gate." },
      { question: "Can I take photographs during the tour?", answer: "Yes, **photography of the exteriors and the celebrated carvings is allowed and encouraged**, and Khajuraho is one of the most photogenic heritage sites in India. Your guide knows the best angles and lighting for the Kandariya Mahadev spire and the sculpted panels. Note that some inner sanctums may have restrictions, and tripods or professional gear can require a separate permit." },
      { question: "Is the guide officially licensed and approved?", answer: "Yes. **The tour is led by a local, government-approved official tour guide**, which is exactly what the site's heritage status calls for. These guides are trained in the history of the Chandela dynasty and the UNESCO-listed monuments, so you get accurate context rather than hearsay. It also means you're not relying on unofficial touts who gather near the gate." },
      { question: "Is the tour suitable for older travellers?", answer: "It can be, with a little planning. The grounds are flat and pleasant to stroll, but **the climbs onto the temple platforms involve steep steps** that some older visitors prefer to skip. Because it's private, your guide can tailor the route, allow rest stops in shaded spots, and focus on the ground-level carvings and viewpoints that are just as rewarding." },
      { question: "How do I confirm my booking and what if my hotel isn't listed?", answer: "Book online and you'll receive confirmation with the meeting details. **For the $100 car option, simply share your Khajuraho hotel name and we'll arrange lobby pick-up**; almost all local hotels are within easy reach of the temples. If your accommodation is slightly outside the main cluster, let us know in advance and we'll confirm the exact pick-up arrangement." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "We offer flexible cancellation on this tour, so plans that change needn't cost you. **We recommend cancelling at least 24 hours before your start time for a full refund.** Because a licensed guide (and driver, on the car option) is reserved specifically for you, very late cancellations may not be refundable. Check the exact terms shown at checkout before you confirm." },
      { question: "Can the tour cover more than the Western Group in 4 hours?", answer: "The 4 hours are built around the **Western Group as the centrepiece, with about 2 hours there**, and there's scope to see other highlights depending on your pace and interests. If you'd like to add the Eastern or Southern temple clusters, the $100 option with the car makes moving between them far easier. Discuss your priorities with the guide at the start so the time is used the way you want." },
    ];
  }

  // --- REMAINING: jaisalmer-fort-walking-tour (auto-added 2026-07-02) ---
  if (slug === 'jaisalmer-fort-walking-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What exactly does this Jaisalmer Heritage Walk cover in 3 hours?", answer: "Over roughly **3 hours** you'll explore the living **Jaisalmer Fort** (a UNESCO World Heritage Site), wind through its narrow lanes past centuries-old Jain temples and royal palaces, admire the intricately carved **Patwon Ki Haveli**, and stroll the colourful bazaars where artisans still work. The walk also takes in the scenic calm of **Gadisar Lake**. Your official local guide brings the stories and history to life along the way." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup included in the tour price?", answer: "This is a **walking tour on foot within the old city**, so it does not include vehicle transfers. Most guests meet the guide at an agreed point near the fort's first gate, and since many hotels and havelis sit inside or just below the fort, it's usually a short stroll to the start. Once you book, we'll confirm the exact meeting point and time with you directly." },
      { question: "Does the $20 price include entry tickets to the sites?", answer: "The **Jaisalmer Heritage Walk ($20 per person)** includes your **walking tour with an official local guide** only. Any optional entry fees, such as the Jain temples inside the fort or the interior of Patwon Ki Haveli, are paid separately and are modest. Your guide will tell you which sites have a ticket so you can decide what to enter as you go." },
      { question: "What's the best time of day to do this walk?", answer: "**Early morning** is the favourite slot, when the sandstone glows gold in soft light, the lanes are quiet, and the heat is bearable. **Late afternoon into sunset** is also lovely, especially for the views from the fort ramparts and over Gadisar Lake. We'll help you pick a start time that suits the season and your schedule." },
      { question: "How hot does Jaisalmer get, and what should I wear?", answer: "Jaisalmer sits in the **Thar Desert**, so days can be very hot from April to September, while November to February are pleasantly mild. Wear light, breathable clothing, a hat and sunglasses, and comfortable closed shoes for the uneven cobbled lanes. Bring water and sunscreen. As you'll pass Jain temples, modest dress that covers shoulders and knees is appreciated." },
      { question: "Is the walk physically demanding? How much walking and how many steps?", answer: "It's a **gentle-to-moderate walk** at an easy, story-led pace rather than a hike. Expect a gradual climb up to the fort, cobbled and sometimes uneven lanes, and a few staircases inside the fort and havelis. There's plenty of pausing to look and listen, so most reasonably mobile travellers manage comfortably. If you'd rather see the highlights by vehicle, the [Highlights of Jaisalmer half-day tour by car](/india/jaisalmer/jaisalmer-fort-half-day-tour) is a good alternative." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children and older travellers?", answer: "Yes, families and older guests are very welcome. Children usually enjoy the fort's maze of lanes, the cows and shops, and the artisan stalls, while the relaxed pace suits grandparents too. The main things to note are the climb to the fort and some steps and uneven ground. Comfortable shoes and a slower morning start make it easy for all ages." },
      { question: "Is this a private tour or a shared group?", answer: "The tour is built around a **dedicated official local guide** leading your walk through the fort and old city. Group size is kept small and personal, so you can ask questions and set the pace. If you'd like a purely private, credentials-verified guide for the fort, you may also consider our [licensed and certified Jaisalmer Fort guide](/india/jaisalmer/jaisalmer-fort-certified-guided-tour) option." },
      { question: "How does this compare to your other Jaisalmer walking tour?", answer: "Both are excellent guided heritage walks. This route pairs the fort and bazaars with **Patwon Ki Haveli and Gadisar Lake** for a well-rounded old-city introduction. For a slightly different itinerary and guide, some travellers also book the [Jaisalmer Heritage Walking Tour with a professional guide](/india/jaisalmer/jaisalmer-fort-heritage-walking-tour). Either way you get a knowledgeable local storyteller and the same golden lanes." },
      { question: "Can I go shopping in the bazaars, and is bargaining expected?", answer: "Absolutely, the walk passes vibrant bazaars filled with **textiles, handcrafted jewellery, leather goods and Rajasthani souvenirs**, and you're free to browse. Gentle **bargaining is normal and expected** in the markets, and your guide can help you gauge fair prices and point you to genuine artisan workshops. If you want to shop seriously, tell your guide so time can be allowed for it." },
      { question: "Is it good for photography?", answer: "It's a photographer's dream. The golden sandstone of **Jaisalmer Fort**, the lace-like stone carving of **Patwon Ki Haveli**, the colourful market lanes, and the reflections at **Gadisar Lake** all photograph beautifully, especially in the golden hours. Your guide knows the best viewpoints and quiet corners, and can point out spots for that classic Golden City shot." },
      { question: "Will there be food or should I eat beforehand?", answer: "This is a **heritage walking tour**, so meals are not included, but the route passes cafes, rooftop restaurants and street-food stalls where you can grab chai, lassi or a snack. It's a good idea to eat a light meal before you start and carry water. Your guide is happy to recommend trusted local spots for vegetarian Rajasthani food afterwards." },
      { question: "How do I deal with touts or persistent shopkeepers inside the fort?", answer: "Jaisalmer is a friendly, generally very safe city, and having a **local guide** greatly reduces any hassle from touts or over-eager shopkeepers. Your guide will keep the walk on track, steer you toward genuine artisans, and handle any pushiness politely on your behalf. A simple smile and no thank you is all you'll usually need." },
      { question: "What's the cancellation policy and how do I confirm my booking?", answer: "Once you book online you'll receive a confirmation, and we'll follow up to lock in your meeting point and start time. Free cancellation terms and any cut-off apply as shown at checkout, so please review them before paying. If your plans change, contact us as early as possible and we'll do our best to help you rebook." },
      { question: "Can I combine this walk with a desert safari or camel ride?", answer: "Yes, many travellers pair this morning heritage walk with a desert experience later the same day. Our [Jaisalmer jeep safari with camel ride, dinner and folk dance](/india/jaisalmer/jaisalmer-fort-guided-tour) is a popular add-on that takes you into the dunes for sunset. Just leave enough time between the two, and we can help coordinate the timings." },
      { question: "What time should I arrive and how long before should I book?", answer: "We recommend arriving about **10 minutes early** at the agreed meeting point so the walk can start promptly and enjoy the cooler, quieter hours. Booking a day or two ahead is ideal, especially in the peak November-to-February season, to secure your preferred start time and guide. Last-minute spots are sometimes available, so it's always worth asking." },
    ];
  }

  // --- REMAINING: junagarh-fort-spiritual-tour (auto-added 2026-07-02) ---
  if (slug === 'junagarh-fort-spiritual-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What exactly does this Bikaner Heritage Journey cover in the 10 hours?", answer: "Over roughly **10 hours** you'll explore **Junagarh Fort**, built in **1593 AD**, including the palaces, carved balconies and courtyards, then the **Prachina Museum** inside the fort complex with its royal portraits, costumes and weapons. You then continue to the **Karni Mata Temple (the Rat Temple)** in Deshnoke. The tour is designed as a heritage journey en route from Bikaner toward Jaisalmer, so it works well as a full sightseeing day or a purposeful transfer." },
      { question: "Is this a private tour or a shared group tour?", answer: "This is a **fully private tour**. All transfers and sightseeing are by **private AC vehicle** with your own professional guide, so it's just your party in the car. That means you set the pace, linger longer at Junagarh Fort or the Rat Temple if you wish, and won't wait on a larger group. It's ideal for couples, families and small friend groups." },
      { question: "How much does the tour cost and what does the $60 include?", answer: "The tour is **$60** for the option shown. That price includes **all transfers and sightseeing by private vehicle**, plus **toll tax, parking, driver allowance and fuel**, and a **professional guide**. Please note that monument **entry tickets** for Junagarh Fort, the Prachina Museum and any temple donations are **not listed as included**, so budget a small amount extra for those on the day." },
      { question: "Are entry tickets to Junagarh Fort and the museum included?", answer: "The listed inclusions cover your **private vehicle, all road costs and the professional guide**, but they do **not** explicitly include monument **entry fees**. Junagarh Fort and the Prachina Museum charge modest admission, and there may be a separate camera fee. We recommend carrying a little cash in rupees for tickets, and your guide can point you to the correct counters so you don't overpay." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup and drop-off provided?", answer: "Yes, because this is a private-vehicle tour, your driver-guide will collect you directly. Since the day is built around the **Bikaner-to-Jaisalmer heritage route**, it works both as a round trip from your Bikaner hotel and as a one-way sightseeing transfer toward Jaisalmer. Please **share your exact hotel name and pickup time** when booking so we can confirm the logistics that suit your itinerary." },
      { question: "What is the Karni Mata Temple and are the rats really everywhere?", answer: "The **Karni Mata Temple** in Deshnoke, about 30 km from Bikaner, is a working Hindu temple famous as the **Rat Temple**, home to thousands of rats known as *kabbas* that devotees consider sacred and protected. They roam the marble courtyard freely, and spotting a rare white rat is thought to bring good fortune. It's an extraordinary, one-of-a-kind experience, though visitors who are squeamish should know the rats really are present throughout." },
      { question: "Do I need to remove my shoes at the Rat Temple, and how does that work?", answer: "Yes. As at most Hindu temples, you must **remove your shoes** before entering Karni Mata, and inside you'll walk **barefoot or in socks** on the marble floor where the rats roam. Many travelers bring a spare pair of thick socks for comfort and hygiene. Stepping on a rat is considered very unlucky and offensive, so tread gently, and your guide will brief you on temple etiquette beforehand." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved and is the tour physically demanding?", answer: "The walking is **moderate**. Junagarh Fort involves exploring multiple courtyards, chambers and staircases on foot over uneven historic stone, and the temple visit adds more time on your feet. Unlike most Rajasthan forts, Junagarh was **not built on a hilltop**, so there's no steep climb to reach it, which makes it gentler than many. Wear comfortable, closed shoes and take your time." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children and older travelers?", answer: "It can be, with a few considerations. The **private pace and AC car** make it comfortable for families and seniors, and the flat approach to Junagarh Fort helps those who can't manage steep climbs. However, the **Rat Temple is not for everyone** — young children may be startled and some elders prefer to admire it from the entrance. Let us know your group's ages and comfort level and the guide will adapt." },
      { question: "What is the best time of day and season to do this tour?", answer: "An **early start** is best so you cover Junagarh Fort in the cooler morning and reach Karni Mata before midday heat. The most pleasant months are **October to March**, when Bikaner's desert climate is comfortable. April to June brings intense heat often above 40°C, so if you travel then, an early morning departure and plenty of water are essential." },
      { question: "What should I wear for the fort and the temple?", answer: "Dress for a **desert climate and a place of worship**: light, breathable clothing that covers shoulders and knees is respectful for Karni Mata Temple. Wear **comfortable closed walking shoes** you can slip off easily at the temple, and bring a hat, sunglasses and sunscreen for the open fort courtyards. A light scarf is useful for sun and for covering up at the temple." },
      { question: "Can I take photographs at Junagarh Fort and the Rat Temple?", answer: "Photography is generally allowed at **Junagarh Fort**, though a separate **camera or video fee** may apply at the ticket counter, and some interior chambers or the museum restrict flash. At **Karni Mata Temple** you can usually photograph the courtyard and the rats, but always be respectful of worshippers and follow any posted signs. Your guide will tell you exactly where cameras are and aren't welcome." },
      { question: "Is food or lunch included in the tour?", answer: "**Meals are not included** in the $60 price. On a **10-hour** day you'll want a break, and your guide and driver can recommend a good local restaurant in Bikaner or along the route — the city is famous for its snacks and sweets, including its namesake *Bikaneri bhujia*. Vegetarian options are plentiful in Rajasthan, and you can let your guide know any dietary needs." },
      { question: "How far is the drive and is the vehicle air-conditioned?", answer: "Yes, all sightseeing is by **private AC car**, which is a real comfort in Bikaner's heat. The itinerary follows the **Bikaner-to-Jaisalmer heritage route**, with Karni Mata Temple around 30 km south of Bikaner. If you're continuing to Jaisalmer it's a **long desert drive of several hours**, so an early start keeps the day relaxed rather than rushed. Confirm your end point when booking." },
      { question: "Is the Rat Temple safe and hygienic to visit?", answer: "Millions of pilgrims visit Karni Mata every year and it is considered **safe** to walk through. That said, you'll be **barefoot or in socks** near the rats, so bring hand sanitizer, avoid touching the animals, and wash your hands and feet afterward. If you have open cuts on your feet or are uncomfortable with the idea, you can view the sacred inner courtyard from the threshold instead." },
      { question: "How do I book and confirm this tour?", answer: "Simply select the **$60 option** and complete your booking online, then **share your Bikaner hotel, preferred pickup time and whether you want a round trip or a one-way transfer to Jaisalmer**. We'll confirm your private guide and AC vehicle. Because it's a private departure, we recommend booking a day or two ahead so we can lock in your guide and timing." },
    ];
  }

