  if (slug === 'ranakpur-jain-temple-spiritual-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What exactly does this Kumbhalgarh and Ranakpur day trip cover?", answer: "This is a full **10-hour private round trip from Udaipur** taking in two very different UNESCO-linked marvels. You'll explore **Kumbhalgarh Fort** — famous for the second-longest continuous wall in the world and the palaces of Maharana Kumbha — then drive on to the **Ranakpur Jain Temple** with its 1,444 individually carved marble pillars. A private guide accompanies you throughout to bring the history and legends to life." },
      { question: "How long is the drive, and how much time do I actually get at each site?", answer: "Kumbhalgarh sits roughly 2 to 2.5 hours from Udaipur, and Ranakpur is about 45 minutes further on winding hill roads, so expect a fair amount of driving across the 10 hours. That still leaves comfortable time to walk the fort ramparts and take in the temple's carvings without rushing. Travel is in a **private air-conditioned vehicle**, so the road time is relaxed rather than tiring." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup included, and where do you collect me?", answer: "Yes. **Pickup and drop-off are included from Udaipur Airport, the railway station, or any hotel within Udaipur city limits.** Just share your accommodation details when you book and your driver will meet you at the agreed time. If you're staying outside the city boundary, message us first so we can confirm arrangements." },
      { question: "How much does the tour cost and what's included in the price?", answer: "The tour is **$130 for the private round trip**, covering the vehicle, driver, private guide, and all **parking, fuel, tolls and taxes**, plus bottled water on board. Note this is a per-vehicle private price, so it's excellent value split between a couple or small group. Monument entry tickets and any camera fees at Ranakpur are paid directly at the gate and are not included." },
      { question: "Are the entrance tickets to Kumbhalgarh and Ranakpur included?", answer: "**Monument entry fees are not included** in the $130 and are paid on the spot — this keeps the price fair whether you're an Indian or international visitor. Kumbhalgarh has a modest ticket, while the Ranakpur Jain Temple has a separate entry and a camera charge for photography inside. Your guide will point you to the right counters so there's no confusion." },
      { question: "Is this a private tour or will I share the vehicle with strangers?", answer: "It's a **fully private tour** — the vehicle and guide are yours alone for the day. That means you set the pace, decide how long to linger at the fort walls or temple, and can stop for photos or a tea break along the scenic drive. If you'd prefer a city-based private day instead, the [Udaipur private full-day sightseeing tour](/india/udaipur/lake-pichola-full-day-tour) is a good alternative." },
      { question: "What time does the tour start, and what's the best time of day to go?", answer: "Given the 10-hour duration and the driving involved, an **early morning start (around 8 am) is strongly recommended** so you reach Kumbhalgarh before the midday heat and finish Ranakpur with light to spare. Note the Ranakpur temple is typically open to tourists from around noon, so the itinerary is usually timed with the fort first and the temple after. Your guide will confirm the ideal departure time when you book." },
      { question: "How much walking and climbing is involved at Kumbhalgarh Fort?", answer: "Kumbhalgarh is a hilltop fort, so expect **uphill paths, uneven stone ramparts and steps** to reach the palaces and viewpoints. You can see plenty at an easy pace, but the higher sections reward those willing to climb. Wear sturdy, closed shoes with grip, and take it slowly in the heat — there's no rush on a private tour." },
      { question: "Is this trip suitable for kids and older travelers?", answer: "Yes, with a little planning. The private air-conditioned car makes the long drive manageable for children and grandparents, and much of both sites can be enjoyed without tackling the steepest climbs. **Older travelers should know the fort has uneven ground and steps**, so a supportive arm and comfortable shoes help. Ranakpur is largely level and very accessible." },
      { question: "What should I wear for the Ranakpur Jain Temple?", answer: "The Ranakpur temple is an active place of worship, so **dress modestly with shoulders and knees covered**. Leather items — belts, wallets, bags and shoes — are generally not permitted inside, and you'll remove your shoes before entering, so slip-on footwear is convenient. Your guide will brief you on the etiquette before you go in." },
      { question: "What's the weather like, and when is the best season to visit?", answer: "Rajasthan is hot from April to June, so **October to March is the most comfortable window** for this long day out, with warm days and cool mornings. The monsoon months (July to September) turn the Aravalli hills around Kumbhalgarh lush and green, which is beautiful, though occasional rain is possible. Whatever the season, bring a hat, sunscreen and sunglasses — the bottled water on board helps too." },
      { question: "Can I take photos, and are there any restrictions?", answer: "Kumbhalgarh is a photographer's dream — the endless fort wall snaking over the hills is one of India's great shots. At Ranakpur, **photography inside the temple usually requires a paid camera ticket**, and some inner sanctums are off-limits, so follow your guide's cues. Please be respectful of worshippers when shooting inside the temple." },
      { question: "Is food included, and can you cater for vegetarians?", answer: "**Meals are not included**, but bottled water is provided in the vehicle. There are simple restaurants and dhabas near both Kumbhalgarh and Ranakpur where your driver can stop for lunch. Rajasthan is very vegetarian-friendly and the Ranakpur area in particular caters heavily to Jain and pure-vegetarian diets, so plant-based travelers are well looked after — just let your guide know your preferences." },
      { question: "How does the guided experience compare to a Udaipur city tour?", answer: "This is a countryside day trip focused on a historic fort and a temple masterpiece, whereas a city tour keeps you among Udaipur's lakes and palaces. If you also want to explore the old city, consider pairing this with the [full-day sightseeing tour with an official guide](/india/udaipur/city-palace-full-day-tour) or the temple-focused [2-day Eklingji and Nagda excursion](/india/udaipur/city-palace-spiritual-tour). For forts specifically, the [Chittorgarh Fort day trip](/india/udaipur/chittorgarh-fort-full-day-tour) is a natural companion." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "Because this is a private tour with a dedicated vehicle and guide reserved just for you, we recommend booking once your Udaipur dates are firm. **Free cancellation is typically available up to 24 hours before the start time** for a full refund — the exact terms are shown at checkout before you pay. If your plans change, contact us as early as possible and we'll do our best to help." },
      { question: "How do I book and confirm this tour?", answer: "Simply select your date and complete the booking online, then share your Udaipur hotel or pickup details so we can arrange the morning collection. You'll receive confirmation with your driver-guide's contact information ahead of the day. If you have questions about timing, group size or combining it with another experience like the [Venice of the East heritage tour](/india/udaipur/city-palace-heritage-full-day-tour), just reach out before booking." },
    ];
  }

