  if (slug === 'jaisalmer-fort-certified-guided-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What exactly does this tour include for $30?", answer: "The **$30 price covers a licensed, English-speaking certified local guide for 3 hours** inside Jaisalmer Fort. The guide takes you through the main gates (Ganesh Pol, Suraj Pol and Hawa Pol), the Raj Mahal royal palace, the ancient Jain temples, the fort's living markets and the Thar Desert viewpoints. Note that this option covers **guiding only** — any entry fees for the Jain temples or palace museum are paid separately on the day." },
      { question: "Is this a private tour or will I share it with other travelers?", answer: "This is a **private guided tour** of Jaisalmer Fort, so the guide is dedicated entirely to your party. That means you set the pace, ask as many questions as you like, and can linger at the viewpoints or hidden lanes that interest you most. If you'd prefer a group-style heritage experience instead, the [Jaisalmer Heritage Walk](/india/jaisalmer/jaisalmer-fort-walking-tour) is a good alternative." },
      { question: "Does the guide meet me at my hotel or inside the fort?", answer: "This tour is a **guiding service focused on Jaisalmer Fort**, so you'll typically meet your guide at an agreed point near the fort's main entrance (Akhey Pol / the first ramp) rather than a hotel pickup. Most hotels and havelis in the old town are a short walk or tuk-tuk ride from the fort, so meeting there is easy. You'll receive the exact meeting spot and guide contact details once your booking is confirmed." },
      { question: "How long is the tour and is 3 hours enough to see the fort?", answer: "The tour runs **3 hours**, which is a comfortable amount of time to properly explore this living fort at a relaxed pace. Because Jaisalmer Fort is still inhabited, there's a lot to absorb — the three gateways, the Raj Mahal, the Jain temple cluster, and the maze of residential lanes. Three hours lets your guide balance the headline sights with the quieter hidden streets without feeling rushed." },
      { question: "What's the best time of day to do this tour?", answer: "**Early morning or late afternoon** are ideal, when the sandstone glows golden and the temperature is bearable. Morning tours mean fewer crowds at the Jain temples (which have set visiting hours), while late afternoon rewards you with warm light over the Thar Desert from the fort ramparts. Midday in the hotter months can be intense, so an early start is worth it." },
      { question: "What should I wear and is there much walking or climbing?", answer: "Wear **comfortable closed shoes with grip** — the fort has ramps, uneven stone lanes and worn steps up to the palace and ramparts. Light, breathable clothing plus a hat and water are smart given Jaisalmer's dry desert heat. For the **Jain temples you'll remove your shoes and should have shoulders and knees covered**, so loose modest layers are practical and respectful." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children and older travelers?", answer: "Yes. Because it's private, your guide adjusts the pace to your group, which works well for both kids and older travelers. Do bear in mind the fort involves **cobbled slopes and steps**, and there's no vehicle access inside, so anyone with limited mobility should let us know in advance so the guide can plan gentler routes and more rest stops at the viewpoints." },
      { question: "Are entry tickets to the Jain temples and Raj Mahal included?", answer: "No — the **$30 covers the certified guide only**. The Jain temples charge a small entry and camera fee, and the Raj Mahal (Fort Palace museum) has its own separate ticket. These are paid locally on the day and are inexpensive. Your guide will tell you the current rates and help you buy tickets so you don't overpay." },
      { question: "Can I take photos during the tour?", answer: "Absolutely — Jaisalmer Fort is one of the most photogenic places in Rajasthan, and your guide knows the **best angles for the golden sandstone walls, the intricate Jain temple carvings and the Thar Desert panoramas**. Note that some interior temple areas charge a small camera fee or restrict photography, and it's polite to ask before photographing residents in the living lanes." },
      { question: "Why hire a licensed guide instead of exploring the fort alone?", answer: "The fort is an **800-year-old living fort** with layers of history that aren't signposted. A licensed, certified guide unlocks the stories behind the royal palace, the Jain temple architecture and the everyday life inside the walls, plus navigates the confusing maze of lanes for you. You'll also sidestep persistent touts and shop hustling, since a local guide sets the tone." },
      { question: "What languages does the guide speak?", answer: "This booking includes a **certified English-speaking guide**. If you need another language such as French, German or Spanish, let us know at the time of booking and we'll do our best to arrange a suitable certified guide, subject to availability. Confirming language needs early gives the best chance of a match." },
      { question: "What is the cancellation policy?", answer: "We recommend booking with flexibility in mind. As a rule, you can **cancel free of charge up to 24 hours before the tour** for a full refund; cancellations inside 24 hours may not be refundable as your guide's time is reserved. The exact terms are shown at checkout, so please review them before confirming." },
      { question: "How do I book and confirm my guide?", answer: "Simply select the **$30 certified guide option** and your preferred date and start time, then complete the booking. You'll receive a confirmation with your guide's meeting point near the fort entrance and contact details. We recommend booking a day or two ahead, especially in peak season (October to March), to secure a licensed guide for your chosen slot." },
      { question: "How does this compare to the other Jaisalmer tours you offer?", answer: "This tour is focused specifically on **guiding you through Jaisalmer Fort in depth over 3 hours**. If you want to cover more of the city, the [Highlights of Jaisalmer half-day tour by car](/india/jaisalmer/jaisalmer-fort-half-day-tour) adds havelis and Gadisar Lake, while the [Jaisalmer heritage walking tour with a professional guide](/india/jaisalmer/jaisalmer-fort-heritage-walking-tour) explores the old town's lanes on foot. For a desert experience, the [Jeep Safari, Camel Ride, Dinner and Folk Dance](/india/jaisalmer/jaisalmer-fort-guided-tour) heads into the dunes." },
      { question: "Can I buy from the shops inside the fort, and should I bargain?", answer: "Yes — the fort's lanes are lined with small shops selling textiles, mirror-work, jewellery and Rajasthani handicrafts, and **bargaining is expected**. Your guide can point you toward genuine local artisans and give a sense of fair prices, which helps you avoid inflated tourist rates. There's no obligation to shop; the guide won't pressure you into commission-based stops." },
    ];
  }

  // --- REMAINING: kashi-vishwanath-temple-guided-tour (auto-added 2026-07-02) ---
  if (slug === 'kashi-vishwanath-temple-guided-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "What exactly does the $40 guide-only option include, and how is it different from the $90 option with a car?", answer: "The **$40 \"Book Official Tour Guide For Varanasi with Aarti\"** option covers your professional local guide who walks you through the old city's lanes, ghats and temples and takes you to the evening Ganga Aarti — perfect if you're comfortable exploring the compact old town on foot. The **$90 \"Book Tour Guide with Car for the Sightseeing\"** option adds an air-conditioned vehicle, which is what you'll want if you also plan to reach Sarnath or cover more ground comfortably. If you'd like everything wrapped into a single day, the [Varanasi Full Day Private Tour](/india/varanasi/kashi-vishwanath-temple-full-day-tour) is worth comparing." },
      { question: "Is the sunrise boat ride on the Ganges included in the price?", answer: "The morning boat ride on the Ganges is included **only if that option is selected** at booking, so please confirm it when you reserve rather than assuming it's automatic. It's the highlight of many travellers' day — you drift past the ghats as the sun rises and locals bathe and pray. If it isn't bundled into your chosen option, your guide can usually arrange a boat with a local boatman for a small additional fee paid directly on the day." },
      { question: "Is this a private tour or will I be sharing with other travellers?", answer: "This is run as a **private guided experience**, so your guide's attention is entirely yours and the pace is set around your interests. That makes it easy to linger longer at the ghats, ask as many questions as you like about Hindu rituals, or move quickly if you prefer. Families and couples especially appreciate not being tied to a fixed group schedule." },
      { question: "Does the tour visit Sarnath, and which option do I need for that?", answer: "Yes — the day can continue to **Sarnath**, one of the most important Buddhist pilgrimage sites in India, where the Buddha gave his first sermon. Because Sarnath is around 10 km outside the old city, you'll want the **$90 option with the air-conditioned car** to reach it comfortably. Travellers wanting a fuller Sarnath-focused itinerary might also look at the [Varanasi Full Day City Highlights tour with an approved guide](/india/varanasi/sarnath-full-day-tour)." },
      { question: "What time does the tour start, and why so early?", answer: "The day begins **early, before sunrise**, because the boat ride and the ghats are at their most magical and least crowded as the sun comes up over the Ganges. Your guide will confirm the exact pickup time based on the season, since sunrise shifts through the year. It's an early alarm, but nearly everyone agrees the quiet, golden-hour atmosphere on the river is worth it." },
      { question: "Will I get to see the Ganga Aarti ceremony?", answer: "Yes — the evening **Ganga Aarti at Dashashwamedh Ghat** is a centrepiece of this tour, and your guide will position you to see the priests perform the fire ritual with lamps, bells and chanting. It's an intense, deeply spiritual spectacle and it gets very busy, so having a guide who knows the best vantage points makes a real difference. Arriving with your guide beforehand helps you secure a good spot." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup included?", answer: "Pickup logistics depend on the option you choose: the **$90 option with the air-conditioned vehicle** makes door-to-door pickup straightforward, while with the guide-only option you'll typically meet your guide at an agreed point near your hotel or a ghat, since many old-city lanes are too narrow for cars. Once you book, share your hotel name and your guide will confirm the exact meeting arrangement and time." },
      { question: "How much walking is involved, and is the ground difficult?", answer: "Expect a **fair amount of walking on foot through narrow, uneven lanes**, plus stepped ghats down to the river that can be slick near the water. It's manageable for most reasonably mobile travellers, but there's no way to avoid steps and cobbles in the old city. Wear sturdy, closed shoes with grip and take your time on the ghat steps." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children and older travellers?", answer: "It can be, thanks to the **private format** that lets you set the pace and rest when needed — but bear in mind the early start, the walking on uneven lanes, and the crowds at the Aarti. For families with young children or grandparents, choosing the **$90 option with the car** greatly reduces walking fatigue and heat exposure. Let your guide know about any mobility needs in advance so they can plan a gentler route." },
      { question: "What should I wear for the temples and ceremonies?", answer: "Dress **modestly**, covering shoulders and knees, as you'll be visiting active places of worship including temples and the Aarti — a scarf or shawl is handy. You'll also be removing shoes at temple entrances, so slip-on footwear helps. In the cooler months a light layer for the early-morning boat ride is welcome, while summer calls for breathable cotton and a hat." },
      { question: "When is the best time of year to do this tour?", answer: "**October to March** offers the most comfortable weather, with pleasant days ideal for the boat ride and long walks. April to June can be extremely hot, often above 40°C, which makes the car option far more appealing, while the July–September monsoon brings humidity and higher river levels. Whenever you come, the early start helps you beat the worst of the midday heat." },
      { question: "Can I take photographs during the tour?", answer: "Absolutely — the ghats, the sunrise over the Ganges and the Aarti are wonderfully photogenic, and your guide will point out the best moments and angles. Do note that **photography is often restricted or prohibited inside certain temples**, particularly around the inner Kashi Vishwanath sanctum, so always follow your guide's cue. Be respectful when photographing people at prayer or bathing." },
      { question: "Is the old city of Varanasi safe for tourists?", answer: "Varanasi is generally **safe for visitors**, and having a knowledgeable local guide adds real reassurance in the maze-like lanes where it's easy to get disoriented. Your guide also helps you navigate persistent touts, boatmen and vendors around the ghats so you're not overcharged. As anywhere busy, keep an eye on your belongings in crowds, especially during the Aarti." },
      { question: "How long does the tour last?", answer: "It's a **full-day experience**. The guide-only option is listed at up to 24 hours of availability so it can flex around both the sunrise boat ride and the evening Aarti, while the car-inclusive option is structured around a 12-hour sightseeing day. In practice your guide will build a comfortable schedule around the morning river, daytime old-city and temple exploration, and the evening ceremony." },
      { question: "Are entry tickets and food included?", answer: "The price covers your **guide's service** (and the vehicle and/or boat ride if those options are selected), but **temple entry fees, meals and personal expenses are not included**. Varanasi is a superb place to try local vegetarian food — think kachori-sabzi, lassi and the city's famous street sweets — and your guide can point you to trustworthy, clean spots. Carry some cash for any temple donations, snacks and the boatman." },
      { question: "How do I book and confirm my tour?", answer: "Simply select your preferred option — **guide-only at $40 or guide-with-car at $90** — choose your date, and complete the booking online to receive confirmation. After booking, share your hotel details and any preferences (such as adding the sunrise boat ride) so your guide can finalise timings. If you're weighing up itineraries, the [Full Day Private Tour](/india/varanasi/kashi-vishwanath-temple-full-day-tour) and the [City Highlights day tour](/india/varanasi/sarnath-full-day-tour) make good side-by-side comparisons." },
    ];
  }

