  if (slug === 'swayambhunath-monkey-temple-full-day-tour') {
    return [
      { question: "Which four UNESCO World Heritage Sites does this tour actually cover?", answer: "You'll visit **Swayambhunath (the Monkey Temple)**, **Patan Durbar Square**, **Pashupatinath Temple**, and **Boudhanath Stupa** — four of Kathmandu Valley's seven UNESCO-listed sites in a single day. Together they span Buddhist and Hindu traditions, medieval Newar palace architecture, and Nepal's most sacred cremation ghats, giving you a genuinely rounded picture of the valley's heritage in about 5 to 6 hours." },
      { question: "Are the monument entrance fees included in the price?", answer: "It depends on the option you choose. The entrance fees are **only included in the Private All Inclusive Tour with Lunch at Restaurant ($135)**. On the $80 Private/Group tour and the $40 Group Join tour, you pay the monument tickets yourself on the day — budget roughly 1,000 NPR for Swayambhunath, Boudhanath and Pashupatinath, and around 1,000 NPR for Patan Durbar Square. Your guide will help you buy them at each gate." },
      { question: "What's the difference between the three booking options?", answer: "The **$40 Group Join Tour** shares an air-conditioned vehicle with other travelers and comes with a packed lunch box. The **$80 Private/Group** option gives you private transport, a private guide and the same lunch box. The **$135 Private All Inclusive** adds all monument entrance fees plus a proper sit-down lunch at a restaurant with a Khana set or choice of dishes, so there are no extra costs on the day." },
      { question: "Is hotel pickup and drop-off included?", answer: "Yes — **hotel pickup and drop-off are included on all three options**. Most travelers are collected from their hotel in Thamel or central Kathmandu, and the tour offers flexible pickup times. Just share your hotel name and address when you book, and if you're staying somewhere more remote, message ahead to confirm the pickup point." },
      { question: "How much walking and climbing is involved?", answer: "There's a fair amount of walking on uneven ground, and Swayambhunath is the demanding part — it sits on a hilltop reached by a steep flight of around **365 stone steps**. Patan Durbar Square and Boudhanath are flat and easy, while Pashupatinath involves some gentle slopes and stairs. If the steps at Swayambhunath are a concern, ask your guide about the side road that lets vehicles drop you closer to the top." },
      { question: "Is this tour suitable for children and older travelers?", answer: "Yes, with a little planning. The private options ($80 and $135) are ideal for families and seniors because you set the pace and can rest in the air-conditioned vehicle between sites. The Swayambhunath steps are the main challenge for very young kids or anyone with mobility issues, but the vehicle drop-off near the top solves most of that. Boudhanath and Patan in particular are relaxed and stroller-friendly." },
      { question: "What does the packed lunch box include, and can you cater to dietary needs?", answer: "The lunch box contains **500ml bottled water, a muffin, a donut, a banana, seasonal fruit, and juice** — light and handy for a day on the move. If you'd prefer a hot meal, the $135 All Inclusive option swaps this for a restaurant lunch with a Khana set or your choice of dishes. Nepali food is very vegetarian-friendly, so let us know about any dietary needs and we'll arrange suitable options, especially on the restaurant tour. If you love Nepali cooking, you might also enjoy a hands-on [Nepali cooking class in Thamel](/nepal/kathmandu/thamel-cooking-food-tour)." },
      { question: "Can I go inside Pashupatinath Temple?", answer: "The inner courtyard of **Pashupatinath is open only to practising Hindus**, so non-Hindu visitors view the main temple from across the Bagmati River. This is actually a wonderful vantage point — you'll see the sacred cremation ghats, sadhus (holy men), and the riverside shrines. Your guide explains the rituals respectfully, and photography of the wider complex is generally fine, though please be discreet around the cremation areas." },
      { question: "What's the best time of day and season to do this tour?", answer: "An earlier start is best so you reach Boudhanath and Swayambhunath before the midday crowds and catch softer light for photos. The most pleasant seasons are **October to April**, with clear skies and comfortable temperatures. Monsoon (June to September) brings afternoon rain, so a morning departure helps you stay ahead of it. Thanks to flexible pickup times, you can usually adjust the start to suit your day." },
      { question: "What should I wear and bring?", answer: "Dress modestly for the temples — **cover your shoulders and knees**, and wear comfortable shoes you can slip off easily, since you'll remove them at some shrines. Bring sunscreen, a hat, and a light layer, as Kathmandu mornings can be cool while midday sun is strong. A small amount of Nepali rupees is useful for the entrance fees on the non-inclusive options and for any tips or offerings." },
      { question: "Is the tour private or shared?", answer: "Both are available. The **$40 Group Join Tour** is a shared small-group experience with other travelers, which is great value and sociable. The **$80 and $135 options are private**, meaning just your party, your own vehicle and guide, and full flexibility over timing and pace. Couples, families and photographers usually prefer the private options for the freedom they allow." },
      { question: "How good is the guide, and what language is the tour in?", answer: "The tour is led by a **professional, licensed English-speaking guide** who brings the history, symbolism and living rituals of each site to life — from the Buddha eyes on Swayambhunath's stupa to the Newar wood carvings of Patan Durbar Square. On the private options you effectively have your guide's full attention, so ask as many questions as you like." },
      { question: "Is it good for photography?", answer: "Absolutely — this is one of the most photogenic days in Kathmandu. You'll get **panoramic city views from Swayambhunath's hilltop**, the enormous mandala and prayer flags of Boudhanath, intricate temple facades at Patan, and atmospheric riverside scenes at Pashupatinath. Private options let you linger at each spot for the light. Please always ask before photographing people, monks or worshippers, and avoid the cremation ceremonies." },
      { question: "How does booking and confirmation work?", answer: "You can book any of the three options online, and you'll receive a confirmation once your details and pickup point are set. Because these are locally operated tours, we confirm your date, party size and hotel with the operator before finalising. Share your hotel name and preferred pickup time at booking, and reach out with any special requests — dietary needs, mobility considerations, or a specific start time — and we'll accommodate where possible." },
      { question: "What's the cancellation policy?", answer: "Most travelers qualify for a **full refund when cancelling at least 24 hours before the tour start time**. Cancellations inside 24 hours are generally non-refundable, as the guide and private vehicle are reserved for you. If your plans are uncertain, the private options give you the most flexibility to adjust timing, and we're happy to help rearrange where the operator allows." },
      { question: "What else can I do in Kathmandu around this tour?", answer: "Since this UNESCO day fills 5 to 6 hours, many travelers pair it with other valley experiences on a separate day. For thrills and mountain views, [tandem paragliding over the Kathmandu Valley](/nepal/kathmandu/paragliding-at-chandragiri-guided-tour) is a memorable contrast to the temples, while a [Thamel cooking and food tour](/nepal/kathmandu/thamel-cooking-food-tour) is a delicious way to dig deeper into Nepali culture after a day of sightseeing." },
    ];
  }

